The Prague Stock Exchange plunges towards the end of the transaction in the minus.
After the steep waste in the morning, the Prague Stock Exchange was able to correct the losses.
Transactions involving ČEZ's shares achieved almost half of normal day-to-day trading.
The Prague Stock Exchange continued Monday's slump right at the start of Tuesday's dealings, when it fell by another six percentage points.
This time, the reason for the slump on Wall Street.
In the Czech Republic, too, the reaction of the market to the result of the vote of the U.S. House of Representatives, which declined to support a stabilization plan for the financial sector there.
Case of shares in Asia
Shares on the stock exchanges in Asia experienced a dramatic case on Tuesday, although the indices were able to make up for some of the losses over the course of the day.
The Hang Seng Stock Exchange index lost nearly four percentage points over the course of the business day, but was later able to make up some of the losses, thus reducing the decline to about 2.5 percent.
The Hang Seng China Enterprises index, which observes the movement of Chinese stocks on the Hong Kong stock exchange, fell 3.8 percent, while markets were closed in Shanghai.
Shares on the Sydney stock exchange lost more than five per cent, but then reduced losses to 4.3 per cent.
Taiwan's stock market fell 3.6 percent, according to the index there.
"The timing for the bailout in the U.S
Hong Kong, even though it belongs to China, determines its monetary policy itself, i.e. regardless of China's central bank.
Interest rates in Hong Kong are at the same level as those in the United States.
American legislators should return to the negotiating table as soon as possible, according to Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd, passing the draft support of the financial system.
Otherwise, it threatened that other countries would also be affected by the slump.
A bloodbath of American stocks
The U.S. House of Representatives on Monday rejected the plan for support for the financial system, for which up to 700 billion dollars (nearly 12 trillion Kč) should be expended.
Legislators thus ignored President George Bush's request to support the plan.
According to Bush, the plan would address the fundamental causes of the financial crisis and thus help stabilize the entire economy.
American stocks experienced a bloodbath on Monday, and major stock indices posted the biggest slump in more than 20 years.
The Dow Jones index fell nearly seven percent, a similar slump it had last recorded in 1987.
The index had already fallen before the vote, but there were hardly any indications that the draft would not come through in the House of Representatives, the index experienced a vacant fall.
Congress makes concession: U.S. government may pump 700 billion dollars into banks
The top representatives of the U.S. Congress and the administration of President George W. Bush have agreed on a broader form of an agreement on financial assistance to the American financial system.
This is to be voted on early next week.
American legislators made a breakthrough in talks on passing a bailout plan in the form of financial aid to the American financial system amounting to 700 billion dollars (about 12 trillion crowns).
However, this does not mean victory yet.
Congressmen have yet to negotiate some details of the agreement before they are allowed to publish the final version of the bill and vote on it.
The plan in support of the financial system will be negotiated in the U.S. Congress on Monday.
On Sunday, the chairman of the Barney Frank bank and financial services committee told Reuters.
According to different data in the Senate, the plan could probably not be voted on until Wednesday at the earliest.
Economists say a notice that the plan will be adopted should be the first significant psychological factor in reviving the financial markets.
After that, however, there will be "disillusionment" due to the complicated mechanisms, such as how the markets could also be helped in practice.
Paulson: The plan must be effective
“We’ve made significant progress.
We've clarified our differing views on what a package to stabilize the market is supposed to look like," Democrat Nancy Pelosi told Bloomberg.
According to their words, there could still be a final vote on Sunday.
Legislature representatives met with U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson on the night of Sunday to give the government fund its final form.
This provides for a purchase of unsellable mortgage assets from the financial companies, which plunge the financial firms into heavy losses and jeopardize the stability of the entire system.
"We are on the verge of a definitive agreement on a plan that will work and which must also be effective in the market.
You have to continue the recovery plan, but I think we're on target," Paulson said.
A signal for Asian transactions
The global financial crisis is also clearly reflected in the stock markets, which are falling sharply.
According to Nevada Democratic senator Harry Reid, lawmakers are scrambled to reach a definitive agreement in Congress before Sunday.
In doing so, they want to reassure investors before transactions in the Asian financial markets, which are in the first time zone, where a decision of the Congress could affect the business on Monday.
So far, however, it is not entirely clear when both chambers of the American Congress will vote on the law, and there is ambiguity about whether the negotiations at the last minute could still fail because of a problem.
Legislators hope it will be passed in the next few days.
However, the law will still undergo some changes.
The total amount intended to help the system should be divided into two parts.
The initial 350 billion dollars is to be provided as soon as possible, as President-elect George Bush demanded.
However, Congress can block the distribution of the remaining money if it has the impression that the program does not fulfill its function, at another 100 billion dollars and later the final 350 billion dollars.
Bush evaluates progress in negotiations positively
Although the president has veto power in this decision, Congress can overrule his veto.
Despite these changes, however, the basic idea of the program has persisted, which is based on gaining finances for the purchase of poor mortgage securities, whose value has fallen, because hundreds of thousands of Americans are no longer able to repay their mortgages.
"We've worked it all out.
The House of Representatives should be able to vote on the bill on Sunday, then the Senate on Monday," according to Republican senator Judd Gregg.
U.S. President Bush was also pleased with the progress.
His spokesperson Tony Fratto stated that Bush was pleased with Saturday's progress and "appreciates the mutual effort to stabilise our financial markets and protect our economy."
Restriction of golden parachutes
The chairman of the banking committee of the Senate Christopher Dodd, the senior negotiator, announced that lawmakers had agreed on a responsibility for the money program, the protection of taxpayers from losses, the completion of perks and a timetable for financing the plan.
The new law is also intended to restrict the so-called golden parachutes for directors of the companies involved in the programme and sets up a committee to which oversight of the fund is intended.
This should be headed by the Ministry of Finance.
"We've worked very hard and made great progress towards an agreement that works and is important for the market and for all Americans," Paulson said.
The plan also anticipates help for homeowners who have trouble repaying their mortgage.
The government should negotiate new installments on mortgages it buys up, with the goal of lowering the monthly cost for the debtors and allowing them to retain their homes.
Marek: Psychological Help
The proposed plan is a good idea, according to Patria Fininance's chief economist David Marek, but implementation will be very difficult.
"This is because you don't know by which mechanism and at what price the problematic assets are bought.
This will be crucial for how the financial sector and the government divide the losses among themselves," Marek told server iHNed.cz this week.
Whether the plan will be adopted now or early next week is not decisive, according to Marek's view.
"Much more important is that congressmen agree to support the plan and that they say goodbye to it as soon as possible.
The psychological significance of the agreement that there will be a farewell is the most important thing at this moment," Marek stressed.
Duel on knife's edge: MP3 player against mobile phones.
We will advise you on your choice
While almost every phone can play MP3 files, no MP3 player can call yet.
Therefore, of course, it looks like it's better to just buy a phone.
In spite of everything, there are enough reasons why you should buy an independent player.
The choice depends entirely on the type of use and the requirements of the future owner.
Surely it’s hard to buy an expensive turntaker at home to listen to Maxim Turbulenc’s latest album.
Once such musical novelties don't appear on LPs, and from a qualitative point of view, there's no objective reason for that either.
However, if you have LPs with art rock, jazz or blues on the shelf, you're probably at least dreaming of one.
This is also the case with the music when traveling, i.e. with compressed music, in simple terms - music in MP3 format.
There are also price and quality categories here.
It may not be acceptable for one to spend eight thousand crowns on a player, but it is also difficult for a Pink Floyd fan to have headphones for twenty crowns from the market.
Two extremes between which there is enough space for everyone.
The selection is wide-ranging.
The service life of the batteries is also crucial.
Some good music players can play much more than just music, but they don't even manage what cell phones can do.
Not a modern device is as universal as the phone.
In addition to the primary functions, phone calls and texting, it can reproduce music and videos, record both, it sometimes has a better, sometimes a worse camera and may also be able to navigate.
And if you still include the smartphones and communicators, you almost have a small computer in your pocket.
It's a small miracle, because no fridge can grill and not photograph a TV.
But the universality of mobile phones also entails disadvantages.
However, the more features you use on your mobile phone, the more often you will need to reload it.
And playing music is one of the biggest energies.
Few mobile phones can play music for more than ten-twelve hours, mostly it's much less.
In this regard, music players (especially the simplest ones) are significantly more powerful.
That’s why you should reckon with cheap phones that they can’t play music for more than a few hours.
If you call from time to time, you would rather buy yourself a charger for the office for safety - this also applies to more expensive devices.
You won’t have this problem with MP3 players, even the less good ones will last at least 9 hours of uninterrupted operating.
Another problem is if you want to transfer the music to your phone.
Unfortunately, only a small part of the devices has a USB closure, in addition, not every mobile phone can be connected directly to a PC without special software.
Of course, you can transfer the music directly to a storage medium, but only a few card readers are finished without reduction with the tiny memory cards that are used in mobile phones.
With exceptions (iPod), you do not require any special software for players.
All you have to do is feeding the device from your PC via USB port with music.
Most mobile phones and players create a music library themselves, sorted according to the name of the artist, the title of the album, the genre or, for example, the rating by users.
Some devices only represent the tree structure of the address directory, much like Windows Explorer, some can do both (iriver, Samsung, Sony, Cowon, smart phones with operating system).
Players are easier to use, mobile phones can do more
When choosing a specific device, you should inquire in particular about the service life of the battery, the storage capacity, the quality and the type of sound and, of course, the ease of use.
If music is above all a backdrop for you and you only listen to it every day briefly, for example, on the tram, then any mobile phone that plays MP3 files is enough.
What almost any cell phone really can do.
You also don’t have to control which music formats a mobile phone or player can handle.
MP3 will create everyone, and most of the music you get will be in this format right now.
The format is one of the most common, and most devices can also play music in WMA format.
Less common is AAC (a format that Apple and its iPods use).
Better phones also make it possible to buy music online.
You only have to access the corresponding pages of a provider (or another provider) via mobile Internet, and you can buy a song or album.
This service can be a beating argument in itself for why you should buy a music phone.
Unfortunately, the offer of providers is bad and is usually only limited to fast radio hits.
Prices aren't the lowest either.
In general, however, one assumes that it is precisely this type of sales of music (whether via GSM, Internet or wireless internet) that will dominate the music industry in the future.
Most people carry the mobile phone with them.
If you want to listen to something, reach for the mobile phone in the jacket pocket, while the player is forgotten at home.
However, it depends on whether you always carry headphones or at least a wireless hands-free device.
If not, you can access the built-in speaker, which has almost any mobile phone, but only the fewest MP3 players.
Probably the biggest advantage of the music phones will be in the price for many.
The cheapest usually costs less than three thousand crowns.
For three and four thousand crowns you already get a very decent Walkman.
The biggest handicap for most is that there are no normal headphones.
The best and most technically sophisticated phones with a 3.5 mm opening for normal headphones cost up to fifteen thousand crowns.
The advantage of the players is the easy operation.
The simplest - and not necessarily cheapest (see selection) - player can really serve anyone.
A minimum of functions, the resemblance in operation with the formerly normal cassette recorder or, for example, the turntable is obvious.
There is no risk of a collision in the form of erroneously employed mobile Internet and a subsequently horrendous invoice for data transmissions.
Which device should you reach for now
Even the cheapest mobile phones (e.g. Vodafone 527) usually have a slot for interchangeable storage media (type M2, microSD, etc.).
Even if you buy a corresponding card, two thousand crowns are sufficient.
But beware.
The basic models usually only support cards with a maximum capacity of 2 GB.
Only the most expensive ones can also read 8-GB memory cards.
If you already have a mobile phone and still want to buy an inexpensive MP3 player, then reach for Cowon, Creativ, Sony, Samsung, Emgeton or iriver.
Depending on capacity (max 2 GB), you do not have to put more than 1100 crowns on the table.
If you are one of the more discerning listeners, you'd rather take specialized phones - Walkmans (Sony Ericsson, some from Nokia and Samsung).
These devices have stand-alone control knobs for MP3, and the ease of use comes close to that of a single MP3 player.
The furthest here is Sony Ericsson, whose mobile phones from the Walkman series have the same user interface as the MP3 players sold individually.
The same services in the same price category of up to four thousand crowns, but with a higher storage capacity (up to 16 GB) and a long battery life (usually 15 hours, up to a maximum of 35 hours) will provide you with MP3 players from the aforementioned manufacturers.
The first virtual provider is already coming to the Czech Republic this year.
Maybe
The mobile phone market in the Czech Republic is quite mature, the Czech providers offer virtually all services, as they are also offered by their partners in Western Europe.
Nevertheless, there is something missing here - a virtual provider that forces the classic ones at lower prices.
Maybe he won't wait much longer.
We have often reported on Mobil.cz that the Czech mobile operators are working among the most expensive in Europe.
Incidentally, this was also confirmed by ČTÚ’s telecommunications authority, which drew attention to the fact that the price of a text message here is higher than in the rest of the European Union.
According to the last steps of the European Commission in the field of regulation of roaming charges, there is likely to be a paradoxical situation - for some customers of the Czech mobile operators, SMS from abroad will be cheaper than at home.
How is it that the prices in the Czech Republic are so high compared to Europe?
The answer is simple, it lacks the competition, which really makes a battle announcement.
Maybe it sounds paradoxical, in the Czech Republic there are three mobile networks and a penetration of far more than 100%.
The mobile operators, on the other hand, are happy to complain that there is extremely much competition in the Czech market.
Pavel Dvořák, chairman of the Czech Telecommunications Authority (ČTÚ), who in the past had often stated that there was no room for another provider in the Czech Republic, often agrees.
It’s probably true that a classic provider with its own network no longer has many chances (by the way, it would probably hardly find frequencies anymore).
However, there is still one possibility - a virtual provider.
Especially the entry of virtual providers into the mobile phone market entailed a significant price reduction, to which the classic mobile phone providers had to react.
There were similar voices on the market in the UK even before such companies such as Tesco Mobile or Virgin Mobile entered the market as in the Czech Republic.
However, it has been shown that there is room for such providers and that they can be truly successful.
You don’t have to look for examples for long.
In February this we reported on the launch of the virtual provider Mobilking in Poland.
In just half a year, this provider managed to attract more than 100 thousand customers and thus become the fifth-largest provider in the country.
Behind Mobilking is the financial group Penta, which owns U:fon in the Czech Republic, among others.
The advantage of virtual providers is that you can concentrate on a much more specific target group.
In order to function effectively, virtual providers need far fewer customers than a classic provider.
You don’t need a relatively hight cost for the construction, operation and maintenance of a mobile network.
In the world, the virtual providers usually stand out as low-cost companies - they offer cheap telephone conversations and text messages, but this is often accompanied by a less extensive range of services.
Tesco Mobile, for example, in the UK offers SMS at least half cheaper than its competitors.
However, as the example of Mobilking in Poland shows, it can also be quite a good move to rely on a non-clichéd target group.
This provider is specifically aimed at men.
"Real guys choose Mobilking - the provider they like and can trust," said David Seibert, the CEO of the Mobile Entertainment Company, this virtual provider's operating company.
It is difficult to say whether a similar concept as in Poland could gain a foothold in the Czech Republic.
Vodafone Miroslav Čepický said: "The Czech market is relatively small for a market segment on which most virtual providers are based.
In addition, from the point of view of the range of services, it is relatively saturated."
In the Czech Republic without a chance, the mobile operators think
However, it is almost certain that if a virtual mobile carrier conquers the Czech Republic, this will not be mobilking.
"While we are talking about an expansion of this successful project, in the Czech Republic the negotiations do not look particularly hopeful.
There will be a market entry of Mobilking in other countries of our region," says in a telephone conversation for Mobil.cz Jana Studničková, press spokeswoman for the financial group Penta.
Indirectly, it confirmed in such a way that the current mobile operators in the Czech Republic are not particularly inclined towards this idea.
On the contrary, they are trying to do everything they can to ensure that no potential competitor emerges.
Their interest in launching a virtual mobile service provider has been demonstrated for a long time by the GTS Novera group, which wants to take another step to become a real competitor of Telefónica O2 in the area of corporate customers.
"We have a great interest in offering our customers not only landline but also mobile telecoms services.
And the sooner, the better.
We have repeatedly negotiated with all Czech mobile operators about this possibility.
With some of them, we have already been able to achieve negotiation progress," according to the press spokesman of the Pavel Kaidl group.
Maybe better times are coming now.
From non-official information, it emerges that the first virtual provider may be expected by the end of the year.
So far, however, this is surrounded by an insurmountable wall of silence.
"At this moment, we don't assume there's room for a fully-fledged virtual provider in the Czech Republic.
Compared to other markets, the Czech market has large specifics.
So far, no subject has approached us with a complex and reputable project in this area," says Vlastimil Sršeň from Telefónica O2's press department in his statement for Mobil.cz.
The specifics of the Czech market are also indicated by other providers.
T-Mobile's press spokeswoman Martina Kemrová adds, in rare agreement with O2's statement: "So far, we haven't found a viable and mutually beneficial project to build on."
And she adds: "On T-Mobile's network, hardly a virtual provider will emerge until the end of the year.
At this moment, we should already have a concrete offer in order to be able to think at short notice."
Vodafone focuses its attention on meeting the requirements of its own 3G license.
Miroslav Čepický says: "We are open to negotiations with potential virtual providers, in particular in the area of sharing costs and infrastructure.
It is precisely this from the point of view of our customers that we regard as the best thing
Vodafone's main strategy in this area lies in the search for a partner to build a shared network for a fast mobile internet."
The responsible authorities - hand in hand with the providers
The approach of the mobile providers is easy to understand, another provider would mean more competition, this would lead to declining prices and thus also to a decline in profits.
So why should you make it easier for such a competitor to enter the market?
The fact that the providers can pull together when it comes to something also proves the long back-and-forth about the creation of U:fon.
Probably the strongest pressure in the direction of ČTÚ came from the company T-Mobile when U:fon came on the market.
This is also evidenced by the publicly available information from the ČTÚ websites.
But now U:fon is suitable as an argument why you should not allow a virtual provider.
Martina Kemrová commented: "The competitive environment of the mobile phone market has also recently been strengthened by the market entry of a fourth mobile operator.
Taking into account the nature of the mobile phone market in the Czech Republic, it is obvious that a new player would place high demands on the market itself and, above all, its services."
In view of this approach of the mobile operators, however, the absolute disinterest of the Czech regulatory authorities is surprising.
It is not only the attitude of ČTÚ, which unfortunately - in contrast to Great Britain and other highly developed states - does not have the competence to intervene in economic competition.
This has only the Office for the Protection of Economic Competition (ÚOHS).
However, this referred to this entire problem his very typical attitude.
"The market entry of a virtual mobile operator would then be possible after a contract was concluded between a mobile operator and a virtual provider via the rental of the necessary infrastructure.
So far, no such contract has been concluded, because the offer of potential interested parties was probably not sufficiently interesting for the classic providers," according to Kristián Chalupa ÚOHS in his response to our request.
Such a formulation would not be surprising from representatives of the providers.
This is more than concerned by an authority representing consumer interests and, among other things, ensuring that no one abuses their position in the market.
In particular, when Kristián adds Chalupa: "Abroad where virtual providers operate quite normally, it can be observed that their market entry has contributed to a price reduction in mobile telephone services."
Probably, however, the pressure on the correct functioning of the market and a price reduction are not among ÚOH's interests.
What is really shocking about this case is the almost touching harmony of the three mobile operators, which results from their reactions.
The question of whether it is really only a coincidence is obvious.
Just as the absolutely same price of gasoline at the gas stations is certainly just coincidence and is the result of a fierce competition.
"The Authority has not discovered any signs in this matter indicating any action directed against competition, and thus has no reason to intervene in the matter in any way.
None of the providers has a dominant market position, so he does not have the corresponding increased responsibility for his trading on the market.
The necessary instruments for an opening of the mobile phone market to virtual providers are also likely to be lacking for the Telecommunications Regulatory Authority," Chalupa said in a statement.
The interpretation of the dominant position in the market is one of many contentious points.
Of course, there is an analysis of the relevant markets, elaborated by ČTÚ, according to telecommunications from the point of view of economic competition is a single market.
In such a market, of course, none of the Czech mobile operators holds a dominant position.
However, some legal experts offer a different point of view - for the market entry of a virtual provider, the consent of the network operator is always necessary.
And the mobile operator - logically - has the monopoly for its network.
This means that it necessarily assumes a dominant position in a particular segment.
But even if you don’t go along with ÚOHS this argument, it should at least be a warning that three mobile operators are proceeding remarkably similar.
Complaints from a number of interested parties (including GTS Novera (GTS Novera) show that they encounter very similar obstacles in negotiations with all providers.
The transfer of responsibility to ČTÚ is then a fairly common trick in telecommunications services, with which ÚOHS distances itself from the whole matter.
This has been observed in some similar cases.
Unfortunately, it has not been possible to enforce in the recent amendment to the Telecommunications Actidate, which has been passed, the extension of ČTÚ's competences to the field of economic competition in telecommunications, although a number of experts had promised to improve the situation.
The representatives ÚOHS must know this all too well, but they probably have other interests.
It's possible if no one looks
The functioning of some pseudo-virtual providers remains unnoticed, of which there are several in the Czech mobile networks.
They are companies that issue their own price lists for mobile telecommunications services, but which are only partly public.
Normally, these are companies that act in the field of multi-level marketing.
Own mobile telephone services offer, for example, Amway, Bohemia Telecom, Starlife and others.
The price lists of these companies usually only reach the chosen ones.
The joke of the matter is that the company performs opposite the operator as a classic corporate customer who orders a large amount of SIM cards.
As an important customer, he then receives special price offers (and other offers).
The problem is that the terms and conditions of all Czech mobile operators prohibit a similar resale.
"Should there be cases you describe, then it may be a violation of the contractual conditions, which we will then deal with, which can lead to a termination of the cooperation with the corresponding society," Martina Kemrová replied to our question on this issue.
However, it is quite unlikely that such a thing does not spread to those responsible for the mobile operators.
Especially in the case of three companies mentioned, there may be thousands of customers.
However, no operator opposes such a course of action.
From the activities of these companies, they do not entail negligible profits, at the same time - in contrast to the actual virtual providers - they are not affected by the actual official marketing.
Miroslav Čepický draws attention to possible problems: "The law enables such types of speculation, despite everything, the services of these companies are only used by a very small percentage of customers.
In addition, they have to accept that the SIM card in their phone does not run on their behalf, so they can’t have it completely, just like the normal customers of the carriers.”
But that alone, the ČTÚ should at least seem strange.
In addition, another question for the ÚOHS opens up.
If the mobile operators turn a blind eye to such a procedure, why lock themselves against a virtual provider?
It should be added ÚOHS that it ÚOHS has quite strong competences to expose such activities, and if it does not want, it does not have to be satisfied with the provider’s declaration that they don’t know about this and that their terms and conditions would prohibit it.
When the Czech Republic isn't the most expensive anymore
As has already been emphasized several times in the text, the market entry of virtual providers resulted in a significant drop in the price of the various services.
Virtually in all countries, where virtual providers have been created, the classic providers have tried to prevent their emergence practically more or less.
The arguments they used were virtually identical - too small, or too saturated market, problems with the quality of services, etc.
In the highly developed countries, however, the regulatory bodies intervened precisely at these moments, which by no means tried to safeguard the interests of the mobile operators, but those of their customers.
Be it through formal measures or non-formal printing, they achieved that the first virtual operator originated, and this was followed by more.
Unfortunately, the Czech regulatory bodies are throwing themselves the whole case like a hot potato.
Based on our experience, we cannot get rid of the impression that ambiguity in the competences in these cases is more than favorable.
But let’s not throw the shotgun into the grain.
If the background information is right and a virtual provider soon emerges, the Czech mobile phone market may soon set itself back in motion.
If ÚOHS a provider allows the emergence of a virtual mobile carrier, ÚOHS won’t remain idle when it comes to refusing (discriminating) other prospects.
And the Czechs will then finally make no more expensive calls than the rest of Europe.
The new Czech anthem has four new versions.
Are you looking for the best
Listen to the four versions of the new Czech national anthem.
On Sunday, Prime Minister Mirek Topolánek presented the new recordings of the national anthem where is my home country on the occasion of the day of Czech statality in a ceremony.
The hymn was newly recorded by the orchestra of the National Theatre under the direction of Jiří Bělohlávek.
There are four versions available - with male or female solo singing, in choral version and in an instrumental version.
Kateřina Kněžínková and Adam Plachetka took over the vocal version.
The editors of the hymn versions are Otakar Jeremiáš and Jaroslav Krček.
You can listen to all four versions on server iHNed.cz.
For Bělohlávek, the Czech national song is one of the most beautiful hymns.
Earlier, he had already told Czech Radio that he had selected singers for the solo variants with young, representative voices that are distinctive and popular.
"From the very beginning, I tried to maintain a clear diction in both and then with the chorus, so that you understand the text well, so that a harmony arises," the conductor said.
The Czech national anthem is celebrating its 175th anniversary next year.
Kde domov můj was performed for the first time in 1834 on the stage of the Estates Theatre in the originally Czech play Fidlovačka by Josef Kajetán Tyl, the music came from František Škroup.
It became popular, and people adopted it spontaneously.
The first stanza of the song then became an organic component of the national anthem after the emergence of Czechoslovakia in 1918.
One of the seven state symbols remained even after the emergence of the independent Czech Republic.
How does the "new" hymn sound
The Prague transport companies are also transporting to a chip for the first time on Wednesday
Wednesday, October 1, 2008, will go down in the history of the Prague transport companies as a date on which the first passengers boarding a subway, tram or bus carrying an electronic coupon in their bag instead of a paper ticket.
They can currently buy about one hundred thousand people who own the chip card Opencard.
Passengers can buy a 30-day, 90-day or annual coupon (valid from 1 January 2009) and at the same time be placed on their chip card in the information centres and branches of the transport companies at Muzeum, Můstek, Nádraží Holešovice, Anděl
The coupons are also available since yesterday via the Internet on the pages of the transport companies www.dpp.cz.
The service has been used until the afternoon hours by over four hundred customers.
Those who use Internet sales can really forget the dispensing window in the subway stations, where they have so far had to wait regularly in long queues for their paper tickets.
You buy the coupon via the Internet, which is played onto the open card in one of the thirteen branches of the city library.
"At these points there is also a switch with trained staff available to the travelers, which advises interested parties on the Internet purchase and also records the coupon on the map," according to the spokesperson of the Martin Opatrný project.
In addition to the libraries, you can also play the electronic coupons at self-serving machines on the Opencard, which are installed in some subway vestibules.
What do such machines look like?
They are red, reminiscent of ticket devaluers, the passengers may have already noticed them in the subway stations Roztyly, Budějovická, I. P. Pavlova, Florenc and Vltavská.
"Currently there are five of them in the city, but by the end of the year it should be eighty," Opatrný adds.
The coupon checks a vending machine instead of the driver
The operation of the vending machines, which will work from tomorrow, is easy.
You only have to insert the chip card, and the previously purchased coupon plays itself.
The electronic coupons will not only apply in the capital from tomorrow, but also in the surrounding area.
So in the suburban trains and on the bus lines with the numbers 300 to 499, with which mainly the inhabitants from municipalities in the Central Bohemia region get to the capital.
"In the trains belonging to the Prague integrated traffic, the train attendants have reading devices with which they can check the validity of the coupons according to time and zone," Filip Drápal of the organization Ropid explains the ticket control. This organization compiles the timetables for the Prague transport companies.
The suburban buses have card readers at the checkout at the driver.
When boarding, the chip card must not only be presented to the driver, but the Opencard must be placed at a marked location on the side of the cash register.
Thus, the driver checks the validity of the coupons installed.
"If a passenger wants to check the validity of the coupons, nothing is easier than to ask the driver for this information," Drápal said.
A passenger who does not travel with suburban buses and no longer knows when the validity of the coupon ends can in turn go to the self-operating machines, where the coupons are uploaded to the card.
Here, the information on the validity of his documents is displayed on the display of the device.
The Sudeten demanded a referendum from the U.S
In 1945, the Sudeten Germans had requested the annexation of the Bohemian Forest to the United States.
This emerges from a letter stored in the collections of the South Bohemian Scientific Library.
If we cannot become part of Germany or Austria, we would like to live at least under the auspices of the United States of America.
With such a wish, some groups of Sudeten Germans are said to have approached the U.S. president in 1945.
This emerges, among other things, from a letter that has been preserved in a private archive and which is now kept in the collections of the South Bohemian Scientific Library.
In the letter, the Germans of Krumau ask the American President that he should not allow the return of the "Sudetengau" - which was taken on the basis of the Munich agreement by Hitler - to Czechoslovakia.
"We understood the situation correctly and really want to be sure of the words that the American units do not enter German territory as oppressors and enemies, but as liberators," according to a letter signed with the words "people to whom the fate of the Sudets is close to their hearts."
Since joining Czechoslovakia the Sudeten Germans - in the knowledge of the Czech national character - suggests that they would expect brutality, recklessness, incomprehension, oppression and slavery, the authors of the letter ask the American President to take over protection over the Bohemian Forest forever.
"We cannot believe that, without regard to the view of a quarter-million inhabitants, you attribute this territory to a state that is foreign to us, which tries to oppress and eliminate minorities and has contributed nothing to the victory of the Allies," the letter reads.
The reason why the authors are unable to sign is allegedly the fear of revengement of the "Czech oppressors".
If the U.S. President had doubts about the attitude of the Bohemian Forest Germans, it would be sufficient if he would initiate a referendum on whether the inhabitants wanted to become part of the U.S. or live under the protection rule of the United States.
Although the text of the letter may sound somewhat naïve in today’s knowledge of post-war history, however, according to some contemporary witnesses he paints a good picture with regard to the expectations of the Sudeten Germans at that time.
Even if the Bohemian Germans at least suspected that one would negotiate their deportation from the republic, only a few of them admitted such a possibility at all.
Some waited for the United States and Britain to negotiate some kind of autonomy for the Sudeten.
Others believed they could simply stay in Czechoslovakia.
This is also confirmed by the famous Austrian journalist Gustav Chalupa, who comes from Budweis.
He was used in the war as Budweiser German in the navy on the Baltic Sea.
When he came into British imprisonment at the end of the war and he was taken to a prisoner-of-war camp in Holstein, dozens more Bohemian Germans came to him to teach them Czech.
"They believed that if they came home, the Czech would suit them," Chalupa said.
None of the German prisoners of war who had lived in Czechoslovakia before the war reckoned, in his opinion, that he would be expelled from the republic on the basis of the Beneš decrees.
Don't you think - then pay!
An emotionally charged campaign is aimed at motorists
Most expensive action for safety on the streets in Czech history.
Even so, the emotionally charged media campaign, which the Ministry of Transport intends to launch on Wednesday, could be characterized.
Minister Aleš Řebíček’s authority hopes to dissuade drivers of dangerous behaviour behind the wheel.
The campaign for 150 million crowns will run on television, on the radio, in the cinema, on the Internet, but also on flyers.
It is intended to dissuade the drivers of too fast driving and alcohol at the wheel.
And they also reminiscent of strapping themselves on before the start of the journey.
Above all, the campaign wants to address young drivers, motorcyclists, pedestrians and, last but not least, professional drivers.
And what is shown in the spots?
Above all, stories of people who are appealing to emotions.
This is also evidenced by the "bloody" slogan of the whole campaign:
Don't you think - then pay!
The words "don't think" stand in capital letters on a license plate.
And in a pool of blood there is the word "pay".
This should mean that you can pay a moment of inattention with your own life.
The spots will "shoke" viewers until 2010.
The ministry was inspired by similar campaigns in the Anglo-Saxon countries, where you can see pictures with drastic shots of accident victims.
It expects that the number of accidents and the number of traffic fatalities on the Czech roads will decrease.
The spots have been compiled by the Euro RSCG agency.
Carrot and whip for motorists
"For the riders, the motto is carrot and whip.
Sugar bread means that if, for example, they drive at red, they save time.
The whip is then the threat of a penalty or an accident.
The driver must be reminded of the risk," according told Na Homolce Martin Kořán Hospital's traffic psychologist.
And that’s why information campaigns in the press and, above all, negative advertising are necessary, says the psychologist.
In his opinion, even less effective are boards that alert the drivers to a danger.
"If you put up a static board that warns of a danger, it will probably soon flow with the environment, and the drivers will no longer perceive it," says Kořán.
Olmert would return almost all occupied territories in exchange for peace
The chairman of the provisional Israeli government Ehud Olmert has advocated the return of virtually all the territories occupied in 1967, i.e. also the Jordan, East Jerusalem and the Golan heights, if one can make peace with Palestine and Syria in exchange for it.
He emphasized this in an interview printed on Monday by the daily Jediot Achronot.
"We have to reach an agreement with the Palestinians, which means it becomes necessary to return almost all (occupied) territories, if not everyone," Olmert told the newspaper.
"We retain a certain percentage of these territories, but we will have to cede a similarly high percentage (Israeli territory) to the Palestinians, because there will be no peace without this," he added.
He acknowledged that an eventual return would also include East Jerusalem.
"Those who want safety in Jerusalem and, above all, don't want tractors or bulldozers to roll over his legs, as happened to one of my best friends, will have to forego part of Jerusalem," Olmert noted, alluding to two attacks in the city in July in which several people were killed and dozen people injured.
According to the Prime Minister, it will be necessary to find a "special solution for the Templar and sacred and historical places" located on the territory of the old town.
“He who seeks to uphold Israeli sovereignty over the entire city will have to integrate 270,000 Arabs.
Thing won't work," he reiterated.
Withdrawal from the Golan Heights
Olmert spoke out in favour of Israel's withdrawal from Golan Heights, which he believes is an indispensable condition for concluding a peace treaty with Syria.
However, this would also require certain efforts on the part of Damascus.
"My proposal is not to make peace with Syria solely on the basis of a withdrawal from the Golan heights," Olmert affirmed.
"The Syrians know all too well what they have to do without in order to get the Golan Heights back.
They will have to renounce their current relationship with Iran; they will have to forego their relationship with the (Shiite) Hezbollah movement; they will have to foregive the constant support they grant to the terrorism of the (Shiite) Hamas movement, the Al-Khaida terrorist network and jihad (the Holy War) in Iraq," the prime minister concretized.
Questions about the accident: Are stewards on the bus unlawful?
The law does not know them, in a long-distance bus it is not allowed
In connection with the tragic accident of a student agency bus in the vicinity of Padua, the question arises whether stewards and travelers are allowed to walk back and forth while travelling by bus.
"If we wanted to strictly and formally comply with the law, the activity of a steward is contrary to the law on road traffic," according to Karel Hanzelka, spokesman for the Ministry of Transport.
The accident that killed a twenty-two-year-old stewardess last week was probably caused by the driver of the truck, who crossed the bus.
At that moment, the young woman sat next to all misfortune next to the driver, in the least safe place in the car, although she still had two more seats available.
However, a bus can also have an accident at the moment or brake sharply when the steward carries out coffee or newspapers.
It also plays a role whether the bus is also equipped with standing rooms according to the vehicle letter.
If this is not provided on the bus, travelers will have to sit.
Student Agency long-distance coaches have no standing places; which the company's director, Radim Jančura, confirmed to Aktuálně.cz.
"We discussed this with the ministry, and this did not first agree to the movement of travellers.
Later this was withdrawn, and we reached a consensus according to which the travelers are allowed to go to the toilet while driving, if they have their seat," he shared his point of view.
The law doesn't know a steward
"There is no definite legal interpretation for the role of a steward - no legislature describes this role.
However, there is also no legislation that prohibits the carrier from providing services.
The law on road traffic only knows the term vehicle attendant; however, its rights and obligations it does not define," the spokesman for the Hanzelka ministry clearly adds.
The director of the road vehicles department of the Ministry of Transport Josef Pokorný, on the other hand, thinks that the law covers this problem afterwards.
"It depends on whether the bus also has standing places in product approval.
That's all," he insists.
Buses are divided into city buses, where there are standing places and the maximum speed is limited, public transport buses with standing places and a higher permitted speed and long-distance coaches without standing places with the highest permissible speed and seats equipped with seat belts.
As in the plane, the expert says
BESIP road safety specialist Jaroslav Hořín does not see a fundamental problem in the movement of stewards aboard a bus.
"There are not many dangers in a smooth driving style, the situation is comparable to the service on board an aircraft.
Again, it's not certain when turbulence will come, and the stewardess can bump his head.'
"However, if a bus brakes sharply, then of course the laws of physics also apply.
Just like on the city bus or on a tram.
The safety of the stewards must be clarified by the carrier through internal regulations," he says and also thinks that this issue will not be completely solved from the point of view of the law.
Jančura is ready to cancel the services
Student Agency is the only bus company to provide stewards services.
The company employs about two hundred people in this function.
Even after the accident, the director does not see her profession as high-risk.
"But once I get the impression that the profession of steward is risky, I'll delete it.
Coffee and newspapers can be given to travelers when boarding.
That would also reduce costs," he said.
As an interest in security, the director of Asiana Alexej Litvin justifies the lack of stewards in his buses. However, his company operates only a fraction of lines compared to Student Agency.
"We don't have stewardesses, we're resisting it from the beginning.
Sooner or later, this ends with some fatal accident," he had said for Týden.cz not quite a year ago.
It's been an unbelievable
In the accident of a scheduled bus to Naples, a young stewardess from the Pilsen region had crashed, who had worked for society for two years.
The Slovenian truck had probably turned off the parking strip without blinking and had blocked the ride from the bus.
The driver of the bus tried to dodge a sharp manoeuvre, but collided with the right front with the truck.
Jančura denies that the two places had been sold for the stewards.
"These seats cannot be reserved for long-distance connections, they are blocked."
Company wants to sue truck drivers.
With compensation for the young woman's family, the agency will not wait for the court ruling, but she also expects financial damages on the part of the culprit.
A change in the law?
Not in sight
A more precise regulation of the movement of stewards aboard a bus could bringe a legal regulation on which a thirty-member team of experts from the Ministry of Transport has already been working for over a year.
"However, this has not yet come to a concrete conclusion.
Buses aren’t necessarily on the agenda either, rather the points system, the maximum speed on the highway and the use of mobile phones when driving,” according to expert Hořín.
"The Ministry of Transport does not regard the activity of the stewards as something that could significantly endanger the safety of travellers," Hanzelka said, whereby the control of the safety of the buses lies in the competence of the police and occupational health and safety authorities.
Düsseldorf hopes rest on Frankfurt
There is no shortage of great words in the WestLB board’s letter to the house’s employees.
By merging units of the Düsseldorf-based financial institution with Frankfurt's Deka-Bank, "the central capital market house of the Sparkassen-Finanzgruppe" could be created, according to the letter announced on Monday.
WestLB brought a "competence bloc" with sustainable earning power into such a business.
And in general, such a connection could be the decisive impetus for further bundling in the Landesbanken sector.
That's how it reads when a boarder wants to demonstrate ability to act in difficult times.
Because the ailing WestLB is still under enormous pressure.
Not only on the part of the EU Commission, but also through the Federal Financial Supervisory Authority, which has only recently made it clear to the North Rhine-Westphalian state government the time to find a future solution.
However, there has been talk for some time about a connection between Deka-Bank, which savings bank customers know because of their investment funds, and WestLB has been talking for some time - also on the Frankfurt side.
Although Deka-Bank CEO Franz Waas has let it be known in the presentation of the half-year figures at the end of August, in a merger of the house on Mainzer Landstraße with a Landesbank, such as Helaba or WestLB, he sees no sense.
But Deka’s board of directors, which is supported half by Landesbanken and Sparkassenverbanden, has instructed the Executive Board to hold discussions with WestLB on joint business activities.
It could be about the capital markets business or parts of it.
Of course, the talks have only just begun, according to the financial centre.
Deka-Bank, which with its 3000 employees is one of the most important employers in the financial centre Frankfurt, is not mentioned for the first time when it comes to consolidation in the savings bank sector.
In 2003 it looked for a moment that Deka-Bank could merger with Landesbank Rheinland-Palatinate and Helaba.
However, nothing became of this triple covenant.
As soon as the plans had become public, the Hessian state government spoke out against the participation of Helaba.
Even the other houses did not find each other either.
Landesbank Rheinland-Palatinate instead went to Landesbank Baden-Württemberg in Stuttgart.
But there have always been mind games about a connection of Deka-Bank with Landesbanken.
Most recently, however, Günther Merl, CEO of Helaba, had spoken out against simulation games about going together with his house.
Separately, however, it seems to mature the recognition that a collapse of the various houses of the public sector does hardship.
After the state election in Bavaria is now over, a connection of BayernLB with the Landesbank Baden-Württemberg could move closer.
That's not necessarily good news for Helaba.
If the pressure for further mergers increases, it is gradually running out of its partners.
In the south, she would only be a junior partner, even though she has weathered the financial crisis far better than the houses in Munich and Stuttgart.
At the beginning of the year, the Sparkassen- und Giroverband Hessen-Thüringen, which holds an 85 percent stake in Helaba, has put a stop to a merger with West LB.
At best, the Nord LB will remain.
Such geostrategical considerations, however, are only one side.
On the other hand, the question is how the different business models of the individual houses fit together.
In June, Merl expressed the decisive question of which business model should ensure the future viability of Landesbanken has not yet been answered by the German Savings Banks and Giro Association.
In the end, there could also be a decomposition of West LB, which, of course, is not desired there.
Thus, in the letter of the local board, there is also talk of that a "total solution" for the house will be sought in any case.
How to reconcile this with the merger of individual units with Deka-Bank is yet to be seen.
The second Wiesn weekend is known as "Italian weekend"
For Elena it's the first, for Luca it's the third or fourth time.
But this time Elena is involved as his girlfriend, and so everything is different, he says.
Behind them, in the five-litre beer barrel of the Kaiser brand, stands a massively built bearder and proves to the approximately 200 Oktoberfest travelers what deep tones his throat can produce.
Elena gives Luca a slap first, then a kiss.
They came from Bologna to Verona.
From here, the bus parking lot next to Puorta Nuova station, four buses, "destinazione Monaco, festa della birra", arriving probably at 23.45 o'clock this evening.
Three buses are mainly reserved for the Italians, only Brazilians travel in a bus.
In one corner of the parking lot, the South Americans are already singing next to their flag, they have already celebrated at night.
And so loud, says Marco Guarda, that's nothing for Italians.
Marco, who wears a long coat with pelcimitate lining and glasses with wide silver temples, own the buses.
For five years they have been going to Oktoberfest, three tours this year alone.
At night, during the day on the Wiesn, at 22.30 clock back.
Overnighting would only mean unnecessary expenses, Marco says.
The trip costs 75 euros, the welcome reverberation with Kaiser beer, brewed according to the Bavarian purity law, the health insurance and the German-speaking tour guide - Marco has attended a language course in Frankfurt - included.
And already the buses roll into the night, on the Autostrada in the direction of "Brennero".
There's no beer yet.
It quickly becomes quiet on the bus, the travelers sleep.
All right, they're still gonna need their powers.
In Brixen and in Holzkirchen, where several other buses with Italian license plates take another break on the Autobahn 8 just before Munich.
When Marco's group finally arrives in the parking lot near the Theresienwiese at half past seven o'clock in the morning, there are already about 50 other buses from all over Europe.
Elena, Luca and the others are rallying, Marco is giving a final address, explaining the attack plan.
Firstly, put the yellow capper on.
On think is "Oktoberfest", in fracture, next to the Internet address of Marco's travel agency: "conoscersiviaggiando.it".
Second: "When the doors to the beer tent open, it's time to walk!", Marco shouts into the morning.
"Run!" Running and occupying the pitches in the back of the tent.
"Not at the front, otherwise we block, and not all yellow hats on the same benches, distributed in the room!"
The measure, roars Marco, costs 8.25 euros.
Don’t forget tips.
"Showing to the security guards that we're behaving well!
As soon as you leave the seats, they're gone!"
Thirdly: "Tonight point half past eleven can be found here again!" Understood?
And you're off to Wiesn.
On the way, a yellow cap binds to the trees, to signs and even to the election poster of a large Bavarian People's Party.
You want to be prepared for withdrawal.
It's the second Wiesn weekend, known as "Italian weekend."
There is no exact information about figures; there is talk of 50 000, who make their way across the Alps and invade the beer tents, ten percent of all visitors are said to have travelled from Italy this weekend.
The Munich police is reinforced by seven policemen from South Tyrol, Italian volunteers support the Bavarian Red Cross, which maintains nation statistics, in which the Italians take first place ahead of Britons (about 60 missions) and Austrians (about 80) with around 200 medical cares.
Especially popular with Italians is the Hofbräu tent.
Insumptious nights for creditors of Hypo Real Estate
The German banking system is relatively stable, emphasized German Finance Minister Peer Steinbrück last week.
How good that the politician used the word "relatively" and also intervened that the German banking sector would not be spared from the crisis developments.
Because she arrived at the weekend now.
The Munich-based real estate financier Hypo Real Estate (HRE), listed in the Dax, receives a billion-dollar loan from a consortium of German banks to shield itself from the turbulence in the markets.
A person familiar with the situation told Reuters on Monday that the loan assistance amounted to up to 35 billion euros.
Hypo Real Estate would only have to interest on the part it also claims.
The money comes from both private and public banks.
The background is losses among the subsidiary Depfa.
According to a newspaper report for long-term projects for which he lented money, the state financier had refinanced himself extremely short-term - which is no longer possible because of mistrust in the markets.
In the first half of 2008, the bank's cash reserve had shrunk by 95 percent to 2.37 million euros, and the equity ratio had also declined slightly from 1.6 to 1.45 percent.
Current income and net income from financial transactions had fallen to zero compared to the same period last year, revenues from attributions to receivables and certain securities as well as from the dissolution of provisions in the lending business fell by more than 70 percent to 5 million euros.
Only thanks to higher income from refunds of downstream clearing costs from the restructuring of the former Depfa Group as well as from the reimbursement of interest according to the Fiscal Regulations and from the business provision for group companies, the income reduction had still been moderate at around 16 percent.
According to his privatization in 1990, the former state financier had first speculated with commercial real estate loans, which had then been spun off into Aareal Bank.
Because one earned too little in classical state financing, the Depfa had then become increasingly involved in infrastructure projects and the financing of community projects of private investors and the state (Private-Public-Partnership, PPP) and diversified internationally.
On the value of the subsidiary, Hypo Real Estate must now make depreciation, which will have a "substantial material effect" on the profit and loss account of the group, it says quite indefinitely.
The fact is that the dividend has been cancelled
It had already been cut by two-thirds for 2007.
Hypo Real Estate has been a major state and infrastructure financier since the Depfa takeover.
It is more dependent than other institutions on refinancing in the interbank market, as it has no customer deposits.
With the collapse of American investment bank Lehman Brothers two weeks ago, lending among banks has once again largely dried up, as banks hoard their liquidity.
Hopho Real Estate needs short-term funds to refinance a part - around 50 billion euros - of Depfa's loan portfolio annually.
According to Hypo Real Estate, the group's financing needs are covered with the loan for the foreseeable future and the company is shielded "from the influence of the currently largely incapacitated international money markets."
It can be heard from other sources that thanks to the funds the refinancing is secured by the end of 2009.
HRE CEO Georg Funke explained that Hypo Real Estate Group will no longer have to take advantage of the money markets for the foreseeable future.
Bundesbank and the Federal Financial Supervisory Authority (BaFin) assume that the marketability of the Hypo Real Estate Group has now been secured.
This is to be hoped for shareholders and creditors of the bank.
But given the dynamism and uncertainties of the crisis, there's arguably no certainty.
It is even inconceivable that the funding does not have to be used in full.
But even the opposite cannot be ruled out.
After late on Sunday evening, according to the report, the bank was still facing insolvency, even the at least provisional saviation is unable to reassure the tempers.
The share price collapses 75 percent to 3.30 euros.
On the other hand, bond prices hold up quite well.
While the priority debt is barely moving, the prices of subordinated bonds are yielding more significantly.
Above all, the bonds of the former subsidiary Württemberg Hypo are affected and understandably the maturities beyond 2009.
If hypo-real-estate bonds appear formerly a speculative investment alternative, their value must be regarded as dependent on the stability of the international financial system after the significant tightening of the banking crisis and the institution itself, which had only brought the crisis into the house with the takeover of Depfa.
As long as the central banks, governments and less ailing commercial banks are still ready to support the system, as long as a timely repayment of pensions can be assumed.
Or this occurs, which currently hardly anyone dares to hope for - that the financial crisis will be overcome in the next 15 months.
But in view of the reports overwhelming with the partial nationalization, the probability bordering on security has currently only become a justified hope.
This is likely to give long-term believers some sleepless nights by deciding whether to separate themselves from their commitments.
Short-term investors can at least use a final line to form a loss carry-over for future withholding tax payments on price gains of later years.
Sylvana and the secrets of the Bento box
When Sylvana Hiltrop, 16, is supposed to introduce herself on the first day of school at Tachibana High School, the girls begin to chuckle: The German is totally "kawaii"!
What does that mean?
Sylvana already knows the word from mangas - in the new exchange log she talks about Japan.
"The first day of school in Japan.
I've been excited.
Even before the way to school, I had babbles.
I live on the outskirts of Tokyo towards Kawasaki and travel 40 minutes by train to Tachibana High School.
I had to find my way alone, fortunately I didn't get lost.
The start of lessons does not seem as strictly defined at Tachibana as in our home in Lower Saxony.
The students arrive from eight o'clock, until at twen before nine a short meeting session begins with the teacher, the 'home room'.
But before the lessons started, it was my turn to introduce myself.
I'm not even the guy who likes to speak in front of people, especially in front of a class of 40 students.
Because I didn't have a school uniform yet, I put on an old skirt of my host mother, plus blouse, blue knee socks and black leather shoes.
So in this elevator I put myself in front of the desk and tried to pronounce everything correctly: 'Sayonara Wo she Sylvana...' - my name is Sylvana, I'm from Germany and I've got his birthday in a week.
At that moment I was very happy that I had already started to learn the language two years ago.
The 31 girls in the class all cuddled, giggled and waved and kept hearing the word "Kawaii."
That's what I've known from my mangas.
It belongs to the vital vocabulary of Japanese girls and means 'cute, cute'.
Then I swept into my place quickly.
The girl beside me grinned quite broadly, she thought it was an honor that the exchange student should sit next to her.
The rest of the hour was quite quiet, but when the gong sounded, I was downrightened.
Everyone talked and chuckled and asked me holes in my stomach - it's really hard to understand Japanese English.
During the break, everyone unpacked their bentos.
My host mother had also made me such a lunchbox - that's something other than the barren German breads.
I've had rice with herbs, little sausages, lettuce of cucumbers and algae, fruit and a kind of meat in the tofu coat.
I usually don’t have a pale glimmer of exactly what I’m eating here in Japan.
Things look strange and taste completely novel.
It's clear that everything's really delicious.
Well, except for the salty pickled plums, maybe, brrrr!
Bentos aren't just lunches at Japanese schools.
It sometimes happens that a girl gives a self-prepared bento to a boy, which she then prepares particularly artfully - in the hope of getting to know the boy better during the break snack.
I’ve read about it in my manga comics.
I'm curious if I can observe this too.
'Why exactly Japan?' my girlfriends asked me when I told them I wanted to go to school in Tokyo for a year.
Then I always said,
"Because it fascinates me."
After all, I’ve loved reading mangas for years.
And in order for me to better understand the comics and the culture in it, I also started to learn a bit of Japanese at some point.
So it was perfectly clear to me that I had to travel here at some point.
Because I also have a penchant for tradition, customs and customs.
And you attach great importance to them here.
On the one hand, Japan is a country of the latest technologies and trends - on the other hand, it is strict, disciplined and traditional.
And because everything is so well organized, I haven’t felt lost here either, even if Tokyo is the biggest city I’ve ever seen.”
Even rats have feelings
A rat or cockroach flees in case of danger means that it also feels fear?
The neuroscientist Joseph LeDoux is convinced that a lot of animals also have feelings.
But how could one check whether they feel the same if they behave similarly to us?
For several years, contributions from famous scientists have been published in the Internet magazine "Edge".
SPIEGEL ONLINE presents selected answers to the question "What do you think is true without being able to prove it?"
I believe that animals have sensations and other states of consciousness without me or anyone else being able to prove this so far where we can’t even prove that other people have consciousness.
But in this case we can at least hope, since all human brains have the same basic configuration.
However, as soon as we turn to other species and circumstance questions of sensation, or more generally of consciousness, we enter impassable terrain because the hardware does not agree.
If a rat is in danger, it does the same than many other animals: it puts itself dead, flees or attacks.
People behave in a similar way, from which some scientists infer a comparable subjective experience.
I don't believe in that, though.
Two aspects of brain structure make it difficult to transfer our subjective experience to that of animals.
On the one hand, the circuits almost always associated with human consciousness include the lateral prefrontal cortex (because of its participation in short-term memory and execution/control functions).
This broad region is much more developed in humans than the other primates and seems to be completely absent from other creatures.
At least in those aspects of our consciousness that depend on the prefrontal cortex, including the knowledge of who we are and the ability to plan and decide, there is reason to believe that man is even different from the other primates.
Another dramatic peculiarity is likely to lie in the linguistic nature of man: since such a large part of his experience is associated with language, it is often said that consciousness depends on it.
In this case, animals could be excluded.
But even if consciousness did not depend on language, it would certainly affect it, so that animal consciousness, as it may be, is probably very different from most of our states of consciousness.
That’s why it’s hard to figure out what awareness may look like in other animals.
If, as something internal and subjective, it can neither be measured nor, because of the special hardware, analyzed on the basis of our own experience, his exploration presents us with great problems.
Most of my remarks relate to the content of deliberate experience, but there is also another aspect of consciousness, which should be scientifically more accessible.
One could examine the consciousness processes of animals without regard to their contents, and this is exactly what happens in studies on the short-term remembrance of primates, in particular on the example of visualization.
But in this approach, which Christof Koch and Francis Crick followed, one relaces rather to the neural correlates of consciousness than to the causal mechanisms: Both may or may not be identical.
Interestingly, this approach also emphasizes the importance of the prefrontal cortex for the formation of visualization.
What happens now for the sensations?
I attribute them to the fact that an emotional system such as that of fear is activated consciously in one of its own activity.
So what we refer to as "fear" is the mental state in which we find ourselves, when the activity of the defense system located in the brain (or its consequences, such as physical reactions) detects memory.
From this point of view, sensations are closely linked to the regions of cortex, which distinguish primates and in particular humans.
Combined with the linguistic nature, there are fine gradations of the feeling, because with the help of words and grammar we can differentiate and categorize states in order to ascribe them alongside ourselves to others.
On the other hand, Antonio Damasio, for example, thinks that sensations emerge from more primitive activities in the cortex and brain stem’s bodies similar to Jaak Pankseep, who, however, focuses more on the brain stem.
Since this network has not changed much in the course of human evolution, it may well be involved in cross-general sensations.
In theory, I can't disagree with that, but I don't think it's unprovable.
Pankseep argues what appears in rats and people such as fear is likely to feel the same in both cases: but how can you check whether rats and people feel the same thing if they behave similarly?
A cockroach flees in case of danger if she also feels fear?
In my eyes, mere similarity to behaviour cannot yet prove equality of experience.
Although the neural comparison helps further: rats and people have similar brain strains, but cockroaches do not even have a brain.
But is the brain stem responsible for sensations?
And even if you could prove this for humans: How did you want to prove it in rats?
I believe that rats and other mammals, maybe even cockroaches (who knows?), have sensations.
But I don't know how to prove it.
And because their sensations presumably differ fundamentally from ours (since human consciousness is based on special circuits and on language), I'd rather explore emotional behavior than the emotional sensations of rats.
In any case, progress on the neural level beckons here, provided you measure the same thing in rats and people.
As I don’t explore language and consciousness in rats, not even sensations, I don’t know if they exist.
You may think that's short-sighted, but I'd rather move forward rather than constantly run against a wall.
I'm a practical emotionalist.
