Pricies for airline tickets and hotels are falling
In the first half of 2008, flights and overnight stays in Europe were cheaper than in the previous year.
In Germany, according to a recent study, flights even became up to 50 percent cheaper.
Flights and overnight stays in hotels have become cheaper again - this is the result of a study by the travel and financial services provider American Express.
The company analysed its booking data from the first half of 20018 and found a significant slump in the second quarter after a rise in prices in the first quarter.
After prices on the hotel market have risen for years, overnight stays in many places have already cheapened.
From January to March, prices in 41 out of 48 European cities surveyed still rose by an average of 6.9 per cent compared with 2007.
From April to June, however, prices were already 3.6 percent lower than in the previous year.
The trend was most evident in Paris and Rome, where travelers had to pay 30 to 37 percent less than a year earlier.
As a cause, American Express sees the weak dollar price, which has led to a "decreasing desire to travel American tourists."
Financial centres such as Frankfurt or London are also more affected by the decline in hotel prices than industrial locations such as Hanover, Cologne or Stavanger.
In the Norwegian city, hotel prices even hold the record in Europe with an increase of 26.3 percent.
In terms of air fares, the trend in the first quarter also showed upwards:
Due to kerosene prices, travelers in the economy class had to pay an average 7.1 percent more than in 2007, fuel-intensive long-haul flights became up to 29.2 percent more expensive.
From April to June, prices for economy-class air tickets fell by an average of 12.5 percent compared to the previous quarter, but in business and first-class price changes were far lower.
In Germany, prices plummeted even more than in other European countries:
From January to March, prices were up 11.9 per cent, down 23.6 per cent in the second quarter.
According to the study, prices even halved on German domestic flights.
The reason for the slump in prices, on the one hand, is declining economic growth, explains the company.
Demand in many segments of the aviation market is crumbling.
On the other hand, there is increased competitive pressure due to the open-skies agreement, which liberalizes air traffic between the US and Europe.
Signs of the tense situation is the cancellation of unprofitable routes at airlines, as well as the high number of airlines that filed for bankruptcy in the first half of 2008 - in Europe alone this was 24 airlines.
