Dark Matter Affects Flight of Spacecraft
For years, a mysterious phenomenon in researchers has caused perplexity: dark matter affects the speed of space probes during so-called swing-by manoeuvers.
Experts fear that Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity might not be quite right.
For almost 18 years the spacecraft "Ulysses" flew through space.
Due to soon-freezing fuel lines, she now threatens cold death.
Among scientists, a mysterious phenomenon has caused confusion for years - the swing-by anomaly.
Swing-by is a flight manoeuvre of space probes in which they gain additional momentum in the gravitational field of a celestial body, such as the Earth.
As a result, the probes can save a lot of fuel.
The European-American Cassini-Huygens research probe used this physical trick as well as Ulysse's solar probe.
However, the researchers have been astonishing for years: The missiles do not behave 100 percent as one would expect under the time-honoured gravitational law.
There are irregularities in speed.
The probes become unexpectedly faster or slower.
So far the physicists could not explain this effect.
They christened him swing-by anomaly.
Some experts fear that possibly even Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity, which describes the attraction of matter through a curvature of space, might not be quite right.
American physicist Stephen Adler of the Institute for Advanced Study from Princeton still firmly believes that Einstein was not mistaken, and tries to explain the swing-by anomaly using the so-called dark matter that astronomers have identified in the universe.
"Responsible for the anomaly could be dark matter, which could also be very close to Earth," he writes.
Space explorers have only known about the existence of dark matter for around ten years.
From it is obviously an attraction, whose effect on other celestial bodies can be observed.
It itself, on the other hand, is completely invisible, i.e
According to the calculations of the scientists, the dark matter accounts for a good fifth of the total mass of the universe.
Tourists kidnapped in Egypt are free
After ten days in the hands of their kidnappers, the tourists kidnapped in Egypt should be free again, as the Italian foreign minister confirmed.
The kidnappers from Sudan had demanded several million euros in ransoms.
Six hostage-takers are said to have been killed in a shooting before release.
Five Germans were kidnapped in southern Egypt.
Ransom negotiations are already underway.
Time and again, Germans are targeted by kidnappers.
WORLD ONLINE describes the most important cases.
European tourists kidnapped in the Saharah are apparently free.
This was reported by Egyptian state television, the Arabic TV channel Al-Arabija and Italian media.
Italian Foreign Minister Franco Frattini confirmed the release, according to reports by Italy's Sky TG 24: "Our fellow citizens and the other hostages have been freed.
We're reviewing her state of health right now," Frattini said.
The five Germans, five Italians and a Romanian, as well as her eight Egyptian companions, were well up and in safety, Egyptian state television reported.
The 19 participants of a desert expedition are already on their way to Cairo.
The background of a shooting before the release of the hostages remains unclear: According to an Egyptian security official, the kidnappers were ready to end the kidnapping, before the Sudanese army surprisingly killed six of the kidnappers.
"The problem was solved.
They agreed with a ransom," the official said.
“It was just a matter of receiving the hostages” when “this surprise” suddenly occurred.
Sudanese soldiers had shot six of the kidnappers on Sunday in the border area between Sudan, Chad and Egypt and arrested two of them.
The hostage-takers had demanded a ransom of six million euros from Germany; the money was to be handed over by the German wife one of the hostages.
The newspaper "El Masri El Yom" reported, citing a German mediator, that the release of the hostages was delayed because the kidnappers feared for their safety.
According to the report, the kidnappers want to release the female hostages after paying the ransom.
The men were to be detained until the kidnappers had a safe escape route.
The German embassy in Cairo declined to comment on the report.
Abuse of stepdady 'leaded to suicide'
The years-long abuse by her stepdady has caused a woman to kill herself, a court has been reported.
Michael Trerise, 57 from Stithians, Cornwall, received a six-year prison sentence after confessing to eight counts of sexual abuse.
The Truro Criminal Court learned that he regularly captivated his stepdaughter Stephanie Randle as she was between five and seven.
Trerise wrote in a confession: "I feel responsible for her death."
The court learned Ms Randle killed herself at her home in Mutley, Plymouth, on April 1, 2007, at the age of 34.
When she lived in Coventry as a kid, Trerise had tied her up and blindfolded her before carrying out sexual acts while her mother was gone to work in a restaurant.
Trerise and his wife divorced in the 1980s and he moved back to Cornwall.
His stepdaughter went to Plymouth.
However, the court learned that Ms Randle, who volunteered a large part of her time for the National Trust, was prosecuted by the abuse inflicted on her twice a week.
Prosecutor Ramsay Quaife reported to the court that friends had been "emotionally dead" and "renounced their lives" since 2006
In April 2007, her proper father Terrance Randle found her dead in her Plymouth apartment.
It is believed that she killed herself by suffocating herself by means of a plastic bag.
Mr Randle confronted his daughter's stepdady, who wrote a letter of confession that ended like this: "I used to captivate her and connect her eyes."
"I didn't sexually coerce her.
"I feel responsible for her death."
Judge Christopher Elwen told Trerise: "You're an extremely strange fetishist and for her sacrifice it must've been terrifying, especially since she's only been seven.
This unpleasant story ends with the victim's suicide.
Worse still is the fact that it is highly unlikely that the matter would have ended in court if it had not done so."
He added: "I take note of the impact, according to the description of Stephanie's father and mother, had her deed over the years on her daughter.
She would obviously have been a promising, successful and happy young woman.”
Officers responsible for lost data will be charged
The BBC has learned that the cabinet official, who lost strictly confidential documents on a train in June, will be indicted under the "Public Security Act."
The man was seconded by the Ministry of Defense when he lost two extremely confidential documents on a train to Waterloo.
The documents were handed over to the BBC before being handed over to police.
It's the weakest indictment within the law and there's no indication of criminal intent.
BBC defence correspondent Frank Gardner said the action had been a surprise to many in Whitehall.
He said: "They would have thought that would deal with this internally at the Ministry of Defence and Cabinet."
The employee whose name cannot be mentioned is indicted in accordance with paragraph 8.1 of the law, the clause that deals with the secure storage of information.
The royal prosecutor's office recommended that the officer be indicted by metropolitan police because of the highly sensitive nature of his work in the Joint Intelligence Committee.
The documents he relocated included classified assessments about Al-Quaida and the capacities of Iraqi security forces.
He was briefed on Monday morning's decision and was taken from his home to a secret location.
Residential housing associations face B&B costs
British housing associations could face costs of more than 80 million pounds, according to the bailout of Bradford and Bingley Bank.
The government provided 14 billion pounds to protect Bradford & Bingley's 2.6 million customer deposits.
The interests on this loan are paid by all companies that receive money from investors, including the 59 building societies
The director-general of the Association of Housing Societies (BSA), Adrian Coles, said it was "unbearable" that the companies and their members would have to pay.
The 14 Billion Loan will ultimately be repaided when Bradford & Bingley's loans are repaid.
But interest rates accumulated in the meantime are credited every year to those 700 financial companies that accept deposits.
The financial services compensation programme confirmed to the BBC that the 59 housing associations were included in this figure.
It's bitter that those institutions that have behaved prudently ... are now being called upon to cover some of the costs of those institutions that have been far less prudent.
Adrian Coles, BSA
It said that each company would have to pay in proportion to the deposits it would hold, and that the companies together would have to pay about 18% of the cost.
The Ministry of Finance estimates that interest rates on the loan will amount to 450 million pounds in 2009.
This means that the companies must procure 81 million pounds for the interest of the first year.
Adrian Coles, the director-general of the Association of Housing Societies, told the BBC that he believed this was unfair.
It's bitter that those institutions that have behaved prudently during the real estate upswing are now being called to bear some of the costs from those institutions that have been far less prudent.
According to the BSA, no company has failed since the records existed in 1945.
"We're going to observe all aspects of this matter over the next few weeks to see what alternatives there are to protect housing associations and their members of potentially significant costs."
Interest due in 2009 covers the seven months from the end of September this year until the end of March.
In 2010, interest is due for a full year and that could be almost twice as much.
Since building societies are bilateral organizations without shareholders, their members will have to pay for the costs either through higher fees or lower interest on savings accounts.
Milk becomes five pence per litre more expensive
People in Jersey are faced with having to pay more for their milk.
Jersey Dairy has announced it will raise the wholesale price of its milk by another five pence per litre from Monday.
It's the second price increase this year, after a two pence increase in February, and comes after investigations by a consulting firm.
Retailers should decide for themselves whether they want to pass on the price increase to their customers.
Ahead of February's price hike, Jersey Milch's wholesale price last changed in 2002.
Former prime minister of Bangladesh is denied bail
A Bangladeshi court has dismissed a bail request by former Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina, which means she could be arrested if she returns to the country.
Her defense lawyers said that the court refused to blackmail bail regarding the indictment 50 million taka ($729.700) from a businessman while she was prime minister.
The case is one of several floating charges of corruption against them.
Sheikh Hasina's longtime political rival, Khaleda Zia, was released on bail earlier this month.
Sheikh Hasina is due to return to Bangladesh in the next few weeks, but her party, the Awami alliance, demanded that the military-backed transitional government should initially grant her unqualified freedom.
Correspondents say that even though her imprisonment has been granted to go abroad for medical treatment, it is not clear as part of her release whether she will be able to remain on the loose when she returns to Bangladesh.
Her lawyers say she will now appeal to the Supreme Court against the Supreme Court decision.
"The upper court has rejected Sheikh Hasina's bail request because it says she doesn't have to get bail granted, as she is already on detention abroad to be treated abroad," said her lawyer Rafique-ul Huq.
We will file an appeal against the upper court."
Her lawyers argue that she needs bail to return to the country from abroad to prepare for December's elections.
Sheikh Hasina is expected to return home after October 17, an Awami alliance party chairman said on Sunday.
She was imprisoned last year after the army-backed transitional government took power but had been released from prison in June to go abroad for medical treatment.
Both Sheikh Hasina and Khaleda Zia deny all charges against themselves.
Fears over anti-polio initiative in Afghanistan
In the recent anti-polio initiative in Afghanistan, nearly 200 000 children have not been vaccinated because of the violence linked to the Taliban, according to the United Nations.
The United Nations carried out a vaccination programme in six provinces last week.
However, they were unable to reach children in parts of Helmand and Kandahar in the south for safety reasons.
According to a spokesperson for the UN, this was the case despite the Taliban’s promise not to attack or impede the thousands of employees carrying out vaccinations.
BskyB loses lawsuit in ITV case
BskyB has lost its action against the competition commission's decision to reduce its stake in competing broadcaster ITV.
The Competitive Appeals Court has ordered BskyB to reduce its stake in ITV from 17.9% to below 7.5%.
Earlier, the Commission had claimed that BskyB's share distorted the competition and allowed it an unfair influence on ITV.
BskyB acquired its share in 2006 by successfully preventing NTL, now renamed Virgin Media, from buying ITV.
In April 202007, the competition authority reported BskyB's purchase of the stake in ITV raised doubts about the independence of the ITV, prompting the Competition Commission to launch an investigation.
Virgin boss Sir Richard Branson, consumer groups and the Ofcom communications office have spoken out against the purchase.
BskyB argued that the pressure to reduce his stake in ITV was an unreasonable and exaggerated measure, especially because BskyB had announced he wanted to give up his voting rights.
New Inquiry in U.S. Public Prosecutor Affair
The U.S. attorney general has instructed a specialist investigator to deal with the termination of the nine prosecutors in the U.S. in 2006.
The occasion was a report by the Inspector-General of the Ministry of Justice, in which the actions of the administration employees were criticized.
The report drew criticism from White House officials, legislators and former attorney general Alberto Gonzales.
The investigation was driven by fears that the lawyers had been deposed for political reasons.
Specialist investigator Nora Dannehy is now said to be clarifying whether officers interrogated during the investigation lied to investigators to obstruct the judiciary.
Serious allegations, including criminal proceedings, have not been fully investigated or clarified, Inspector-General Glenn Fine said in his report.
The appointment of a special investigator with the power to force the witnesses to testify was one of his main recommendations.
The conflict began when, in December 2006, the Department of Justice removed nine U.S. prosecutors from their offices.
At that point, the ministry argued that prosecutors would be sacked due to weak work benefits.
However, it was later determined that the sacked prosecutors had been judged no worse than many of her colleagues, prompting many observers to view the terminations as politically motivated.
Some of these prosecutors were involved in the judicial prosecution of senior Republican congressionalists, while others had refused to investigate allegations of voter fraud by Democrats.
U.S. prosecutors are tasked with investigating and prosecuting judicial cases for the government.
They can be dismissed at any time, but may not be deposed for political reasons.
The then head of the Justice Department Attorney General Alberto Gonzales has come under fire because of the redundancies and in the end resigned from office.
A number of Justice Department officials have also quit.
The inspector-general's report, in particular, has critically addressed the role of Gonzales by carrying the "main responsibility" for the redundancies, "giving up" his leadership role, and showing himself "extraordinarily disinterested" in the process
Other officials, including former President-elect Bush's chief adviser Karl Rove, have been criticised for inadequate cooperation with investigators.
Meat Loaf's epic song to be honored
Rock star Meat Loaf is to be honored for his epic anthem "Bat Out Of Hell" during the "Q Award" music award ceremony.
The singer, which will accept his special golden award next week in London during the ceremony, said he was honoured to be recognised.
The song, which lasts nine minutes and fifty seconds, was the title track for Meat Loaf's 1977 hit album.
"Coldplay" leads the nominations for this year's award, having been shortlisted in four categories.
The editor-in-chief of the magazine "Q Magazine" Paul Rees said there were some songs that go beyond such matters as time and genre and "Bat Out Of Hell" was certainly one of them.
The song written by Jim Steinman required a powerful voice delivered by Meat Loaf.
It starts with an instrumental part that lasts almost two minutes.
The song was sold in the UK as a single in 1979 and has reached number 15 in the charts.
However, Meat Loaf's "Bat Out Of Hell" has spent about nine years in the charts and sold more than 40 million copies worldwide.
Meat Loaf, whose real name is Marvin Lee Aday, has suffered from serious health problems in recent years. One of them was surgery in 2003 because of irregular heartbeat after he collapsed on stage.
In 2007, he had to cancel concerts due to acute laryngitis.
It was later revealed that he had a cyst on his vocal cord.
Obscure street in city, often forgotten and possibly soon for sale
New York City has thousands of main thoroughfares, avenues, side streets and other bypasses, some of which are famous and others are only convenient.
Still, it's difficult even for experienced taxi drivers and longer-time locals to say where Extra Place is located, simply because most have never heard of it.
This tiny street is located in the East Village and runs from First Street north between Bowery and Second Avenue without reaching Second Street.
It is 30 feet wide and 120 feet long and looks more like a dusty alley than an actively used thoroughfare.
There aren't road signs either.
It's virtually impossible to find them on a standard road map.
This road still has its admirers.
For example, Avalon Bay, a construction company that recently built luxurious buildings on First Street.
It wants to re-pave Extra Place to create a clean drive-through to the shops and boutiques expected in the new buildings.
Among the other admirers is the long-standing neighborhood, who says she's fascinated by the short alley, simply because she's one of the remnants of the block's past.
However, the city administration department, which is responsible for the protection and construction of the residential buildings and is responsible for the street, has no interest in preserving it.
During a meeting of the municipal council's land use committee this month, representatives of the city government and Avalon Bay drafted a proposal to purchase the road by the construction company.
Nevertheless, the committee prefers that the road remains in the hands of the city, and has asked for a new plan to be presented next month, according to Susan Stetzer, the distributor in local council 3.
"Very little room has remained owned by the city and we'd like Extra Place to continue to belong to the city," she said.
"There could be offers on how to repair and operate the road, which could be done by Avalon, but we also want public access guaranteed."
Avalon Bay announced a few months ago that re-paving the small street and setting up seats, which, among other things, are intended for a coffee shop where the company wants to rent space.
The idea that Avalon Bay could buy the road was made public for the first time during this month's session.
Michele de Milly, a spokeswoman for Avalon Bay, said that while the company wanted to use the road, it didn't necessarily own it.
"The main concern of Avalon Bay is renovating this derelict alley and turning it into a public meeting place," she said, adding that the city itself had proposed the sale.
Seth Donlin, spokesman for building protection, said it had actually been the city’s desire to sell the street and emphasized that the task of the department was to develop the properties rather than to manage.
Compared to its colorful The Bowery neighborhood, Extra Place has retained some of its historical impression.
Some believe that the road got its name in 1802, when a large farm belonging to Philip Minthorne was divided between his numerous children and a narrow strip of land was not claimed by anyone.
The back door of the CBGB music club, the punk rock monument that was closed in 2006, opened to the Extra Place and the street is certainly most well-known as the backdrop for the photo on the cover of Ramones' album "Rocket to Russia."
The pavement was great, said Danny Fields, the Ramones manager, who took the photo in November 1976.
The road was full of scrap, rags and parts of barrels, posters, leaves and ropes.
Some residents, including the bloggers who write about gentrification of the East Village, have declined the idea of the sale of Extra Place, arguing that this public property, as modest as it is, should continue to remain public.
Others have praised Extra Place as a rarity – a virtually unchanged splinter of the city that should be left alone.
Jeremiah Moss, who runs the blog called "Jeremiah's Vanishing New York" (subtitized with "The Book of Complaints: A Bitter Nostalgic View of The Extinct City"), has published a part of the conversation of city history about Extra Place, which was published in The New York magazine in 1952.
The author, Brendan Gill, wrote at the time: "Extra Place is a small cul-de-sac that remains dark even during the day and is characterized by rusted metal door of the warehouses, closed windows and weeks-old newspapers flying past rain gutters ".
With a few exceptions, the street looked exactly like this in one evening.
A rat ran through the back of the road, not far from the door that used to lead to the CBGB, where a more expensive clothing store is located today.
Graffiti could be seen on the walls and an empty beer bottle lay on the floor not far from a fresh bundle of discarded newspapers.
Some passers-by said they had rarely thought about Extra Place, but suggested that the road could be polished up.
Fields, added that in his opinion, some people underestimate the appeal of the street in their current state.
The best years of my life, he said, were in the places that were dark, humid and disgusting.
Debate on bilingual training: education in an English-specting environment
In this issue of the "Education Watch," Bruce Fuller and Lance T. Izumi are discussing candidates' positions on bilingual education.
Lance T. Izumi, senior research associate in Caliphonic Studies and senior director in the Department of Educational Sciences at the Pacific Research Institute for Public Policy, is a co-comrader of the book "Not as good as one thinks: why the middle class needs a school selection."
Addressing Hispanic voters is a complicated task.
Barrack Obama's proposals on education are a typical example of this.
Obama's election campaign mentions that "the likelihood among Afro-American and Hispanic students successfully complete the school is lower than with white students" - what's right.
To close this achievement gap, Obama’s education plan specifically recommends a bilingual transitional period in education for those who first learn English.
Still, the question for Obama is whether his commitment to bilingual education, which places the emphasis on teaching in languages other than English, contradicts his interest in reducing the gap in school performance.
Let's take "Sixth Street Prep," an elementary school in eastern Los Angeles.
The majority of pupils are Hispanic and come from low-income families.
More than a third of the students, many of whom have only been in the country recently, learn English.
Nevertheless, in 2008, an astonishing 100 per cent fourth-grade students passed the state exam in math at a good level.
Also surprisingly, 93 percent of children from the fourth school year were rated as good in the English exam.
This astonishing success has been achieved with different methods than those favored by Obama.
Sixth Street school puts its emphasis on review and exercise, on steady scrutiny of knowledge, and an attitude that tolerates no exceptions.
Furthermore, the school practices the approach according to which the training takes place in an English-speaking environment. This is exactly the point that Obama is supposed to pay attention to, according to headmistress Linda Mikels.
This approach attaches great importance to the almost exclusive use of the English language in specialist teaching.
Ms. Mikels, who rejects the idea of bilingual education, told me the school had a remarkable success with a student who had only come from Mexico just before that. 12 months later, one would be able to enter the classroom and no longer recognize it.
"It works," she noted, "it works for us."
Would Obama take a school like "Sixth Street Prep" as a model for other schools with a large proportion of Latin American and other English-learning students?
The achievements of the school would make the answer to this question easy, but the educational policy positions within his own party (the national educational associations have long supported the idea of bilingual education) and his own consistent support for bilingual training makes predictions about Obama’s answer uncertain.
While agreeing that immigrants should learn English, he recently trivialized the issue by noticing that people should stop worrying about legislation aimed at "only English."
Instead, he said, "you're supposed to make sure your child can Spanish."
If Obama actually wants to close the performance gap, he should carefully consider educational models that work instead of despising and trivializing them.
The price of oil is falling as a result of renewed fears about the economy
The price of petroleum fell sharply on Monday due to fears that even the American bailout plan for the financial markets may not be enough to revive the economy.
Crud oil futures on the New York trading exchange have fallen 7 per cent to $99.80 per barrel in the morning session.
Since last Monday, the price has fallen by more than $20.
In the past two weeks, goods markets have been rocked both by financial turmoil on Wall Street and hefty hurricanes in the Gulf of Mexico.
After prices peaked at $145.29 per barrel in July, they fell to almost $90 per barrel this month, then rose again as a result of enormous uncertainty in the financial markets.
On Monday, investors expressed concern again, even though congress representatives had announced that they had agreed on a rescue plan over the weekend, which should be the largest in history.
This news was overshadowed by new worries that the financial crisis was far fround.
In the last chapter of the growing crisis for the time beinging, according to a government announcement on Monday, Citigroup is expected to buy the banking business of Wachovia Corporation.
In the meantime, Belgian, Dutch and Luxembourg governments have partially nationalized the European financial group Fortis.
According to analysts at Barclays Capital, despite the tense negotiations that took place over the weekend and led to a bailout plan, it has apparently failed to enliven sentiment in the markets.
As the economic situation continues to deteriorate, demand for goods, including for oil, is expected to fall further.
Analysts at Deutsche Bank have written in a communication to investors that the forecasts for the global capital markets, interest rates and exchange rates have become extremely uncertain.
We believe, they continue to write, that the goods markets will also be affected.
"From the perspective of the goods markets, we are most concerned about whether the U.S. virus is spreading worldwide, especially to China."
Analysts at the bank have revised downward their expectations for oil consumption due to weak economic growth for the coming year, lowering their forecasts for the oil and gas price for 2009 by 20%.
Team record set by series in the second quarter
Centre-back Shaun Ellis thought he'd seen it all after he's been running up more often than anyone else in the Jets jersey over the past eight-and-a-half years.
Then on Sunday he saw the second neighborhood, in which the Jets turned an inconclusive game into a triumphal procession.
"I've never seened anything like this before," Ellis said.
After a sloppy game between the Jets and the Arizona Cardinals that matched the bad weather, the second quarter began.
However, when quarterbacker Brett Favre's pass arrived at Lavernues Coles for a 12-yard touchdown, this marked the perfect kick-off to an afternoon when their evolving collaboration made a giant step forward.
Favre found Coles in this neighborhood for another two touchdowns that once slipped as an outside-defender and the other time 10 seconds before the end of half-time.
"I didn't put in a lot of effort," Coles says of the passports that went to Coles.
"He's a superplayer.
Our vote was right, no matter what people think.”
While Favre and Cole's Catches were playing, the defense took quarterback Kurt Warner apart.
Defender Darrelle Revis intercepted an accidental throw and turned him into a 32-yard touchdown.
Libero Erik Smith got to another pass and intercepted him.
Linebacker David Bowens then caused a loss from Warner.
The last ball loss, forced at Bowens, was probably the craziest.
The cardinals had captured the ball with 10 seconds left in the game when the Jets recaptured the ball and Jay Feely converted a 20 yard shot to 34 to 0 lead.
These were the most points the Jets had ever scored in a quarter.
When asked to describe the madness answered Erik Mangini, the coach of the Jets "The second quarter or the third quarter?"
To the answers both, because the Jets dropped 21 points in the third quarter without scoring a single one, Mangini said: "Believe me, I've enjoyed the second quarter more than the third.
A lot of points were scored in a short time.”
Reasted Gebrselassie beats his world record
Haile Gebrselassie from Ethiopia has shown again why some consider him to be the best long-distance runner of all time, having broken his own record in the Berlin Marathon on Sunday and has become the first person to make the distance of 42.2 kilometers in less than 2 hours and 4 minutes.
With the victory on the flat course in 2:03:59 Gebrselassie improved his previous result of 2:04:26 by 27 seconds, which he had achieved last year in Berlin.
Gebrselassie, 35, has thus set his 26th world record in his versatile career, in which he has achieved at least one world championship title or set a world record on every distance from one kilometre to the marathon.
Last month Gebrselassie skipped the Olympic marathon in Beijing for fear, perhaps exaggerated in hindsight that air pollution could exacerbate his asthma problems.
Instead, he has signed up for the competition in Berlin.
In almost ideal conditions - with sun and 9 degrees at the start and 13 degrees at the finish - Gebrselassie has won with an average speed of 4 minutes and 43 seconds per mile.
Since long-distance runners like Gebrselassie and Paul Tergat from Kenya have changed, the record time for marathon in the last 10 years has fallen by more than 2 minutes since Ronaldo da Costa from Brazil won in 1998 in Berlin with the result 2:06:05.
After his victory on Sunday, Gebrselassie told the press that he had cramps in his calf two weeks ago and was therefore unable to train for a week.
Obviously he's refreshed after pause and didn't come back rusted.
"I'm so happy," he told reporters.
"Everything went perfectly.
I resumed training a week ago and still had some concerns today, but in the ending everything was great.”
He was criticised - especially in Kenya, Ethiopia's rivals from East Africa - that he overly orchestrates his races, avoids many world's best marathon stars in general, relies too much on the racing leaders, as if this were a track race, and sets world records above the competition.
But this criticism hardly hurts the size of Gebrselassies as a long-distance runner.
Accompanied by four Kenyans on Sunday, Gebrselassie had reached the centre of the track in 1:02:04.
Two elite runners from Kenya, James Kwambai and Charles Kamathi have continued to challenge Gebrselassie after the pacemakers slackened.
Just over three miles before the finish, Gebrselassie made his last start to split from Kwambai, who arrived second in 2:05:36 and thus improved his best time by almost 5 minutes.
Kamathi finished third in 2:07:48.
Among women, Irina Mikitenko, originally from Kazakhstan and now German, has completed a considerable year by showing the seventh fastest result of all times with 2:19:19.
Paul Radcliff from Great Britain holds the world record of 2:15:25.
Last spring, 36-year-old Mikitenko had surprisingly won the London Marathon in 2:24:14.
She has missed out on games in Beijing because of problems described as a back injury. However, she has recovered to win in Berlin and improve her best time by five minutes in her third marathon.
She skilfully planned her run by letting a quartet of Ethiopian and Kenyan runners tear out, but overtaken after 30 kilometers.
Askale Tafa Magarsa of Ethiopia finished second in 2:21:31, while Helena Kiprop of Kenya finished third in 2:25:01.
Pride and hardships in the lives of the black population, in a city of a very special kind
Hidden between the outgrowths of the theme parks around Orlando, a few miles away from the glittering, noisy and gigantic hustle and bustle, lies a quiet town where the pride and complications of Afro-American culture emerge.
Eatonville, the country's first completely black city and the hometown of Zora Neale Hurston, is no longer as easy to describe as Hurston did in 1935: "the city with 5 lakes, three crocket pitches, 300 brown hides, 300 good swimmers, more than enough guavas, 2 schools and no prison."
Nowadays, it's a pilgrimage site.
Alice Walker, Toni Morrison and Ruby Dee have come to the annual Zora! Festival in Eatonville to commemorate the most commemorated author of the Harlem Renaissance.
And yet the city it describes is and will remain – which made it a tourist destination by welcoming it to the Florida travel guide, which was produced at the time of the depression by the federal writer project – in many respects a place of very special variety.
Eatonville is as independent, dignified and private as in the 1930s when Hurston wrote that rural black communities in Florida often refused to share their true thoughts with the white man who "knows so little about us that he doesn't know what escapes him."
Even these days, in a year in which a black presidential candidate, Senator Barack Obama, calls for an open conversation about racial relations, many here are still tired of being watched by outsiders.
"We are very careful how our history is told," said Hortense Jones, 59, from birth a resident of the city and a parishioner of the city's oldest church.
It has to be true."
Eatonville has long been defined as a paradox of triumph and struggle.
It's both a historical example of the black movement and a community of nearly 2,400 residents, where the poverty rate is twice as high as the country's average.
It is, on the one hand, a stronghold of literature and, on the other hand, an example of the - sometimes convicted, sometimes vaunted - black culture of the rural south that has its roots in American slavery.
It's not surprising that residents here are both proud and on guard.
And that's how the concerns about Eatonville's image actually began with Zora, as everyone here calls Hurston.
She introduced her hometown to the world in books that came from the heart and were written in dialect, such as "Mules and Men" (1953) and "Their Eyes were watching God" (1973)
Five paragraphs in the Florida tour guide changed the city, which is very close to Route 77, a street leading through Florida's often-forgotten center, to the scenes of black history and human drama.
Naughty like Oskar in both real life and literature, Hurston led readers to the store of Eatonville's first mayor, Joe Clarke, and then turned to more private affairs.
"Away from the street to the left," she wrote, "is the brown-and-white modern school, with it's well-kept gardens and playgrounds that Howard Miller always takes care of, even though he can't read and write."
She also mentioned widow Dash's new husband and wrote that Lee Glenn "sells drinks of any kind and what-always suits hourly rooms."
In just a few hundred words, Hurston linked Eatonville to self-government, but also illiteracy, second marriage and sex.
This certainly wasn't a travel guide.
In fact, this was not a portrait valued by everyone.
"Zora described things the way they were," according to 90-year-old Ella Dinkins, one of Johnson's girls who immortalized Hurston by quoting the men who sang songs about their beauty that were full of sexual allusions.
She added, "Some people didn't like this."
Hurtson is still remembered here as a buoyant excentrator who returned regularly after her family moved to Jacksonville, Florida.
Augustus Franklin, 77, recalled that when Hurston came into the city, she did this mostly without warning, in a noisy chevrolet, smoking and clothed with trousers, in a city that itself imagines something on its venerable style of clothing today.
According to Mr. Franklin, most residents were intrigued, whereas many despised them.
"People have always been happy to see Zora," Mr Franklin said.
But "she's never stayed too long," he added as he rocked in his chess, on a rear-lying terrace overlooking Lake Sabelia, where Hurston was baptized in all likelihood.
When Hurston died in 1960, she was poor and her books out of print.
Along with much of the world Eatonville seemed to have forgotten them.
Although she was once a famous author, a contemporary Langstin Hughes, and the only black woman at Barnard College in the 1920s, she was buried in Fort Pierce, Florida, where she lived in an anonymous tomb.
There were no grand commemorations in Eatonville, no large-scale public readings.
"I don't think they understood her post in the world or her heritage in the slightest," according to Valerie Boyd, author of "Wrapped in Rainbows," a biography about Hurston published in 2003.
The turnaround came in the 1980s.
Orange County authorities wanted to put a five-lane highway through the city to replace Kennedy Boulevard, the municipality's slow two-lane main street.
Orlando's suburbs had already urged Highway 4 through the western outskirts.
The proposal was made when Eatonville was still recovering from a difficult phase in its history.
Forced integration, among others, had put an end to the relatively idyllic isolation of the community.
In the 1950s, the fight against racial mixing brought hatred into the community.
"During that time, a bunch of white boys often came and they threw oranges and stuff at the off-seated people," Mr. Franklin said.
"There was even once a woman who was killed in the process.
They threw a watermelon out of the car.”
In 1955, Hurston questioned, in a letter to the Orlando Sentinel the Supreme Court's demand to force integration by describing its decision in the Brown case against the school board as "insulting rather than respecting my race."
Inhabitants today say that although the abolition of racial segregation has been positive in some respects that weakened the group cohesion in Eatonville and the self-confidence of its youth.
"Black children were used to being hugged - I remember - one hugged his teacher in the morning and one embraced his teacher in the evening," according N.Y. Nathiri , daughter of Ella Dinkins and executive director of the non-profit group to protect the Eatonville community.
This could be maintained for so long, she added, until teachers and pupils no longer came from the same place.
"You didn't hug his white teacher, because he was a white teacher - I mean there's a cultural divide there," Ms Nathiri said.
However, civil rights have helped create space for further Zora Neale Hurstons - black writers, actors and artists who overruled prejudice, just as she did, with positive self-confidence and statements such as: "How can anyone deny the joy of my society!
“I don’t understand.”
In 1975, writer Alice Walker made her way to Hurston's nameless tomb and began fighting for the revival of her fame.
Five years later, a much-praised Hurston biography by Robert E. Hemenway was found on the bookshelves, making them once again part of American literary canon.
The trunk road projection came exactly at a time when Eatonville's most famous daughter was once again in the public eye.
And this time, Hurston's old neighbors saw her as a savior.
The municipality began planning a Hurston festival in 1988 to show what the district could destroy with the construction of the highway.
Thousands of fans arrived at the opening event two years later, and every January many return for the celebration.
Several years later, the district turned away from its road construction plans.
"The plans of a five-lane trunk road revived the city, accelerated the whole thing at top speed, aroused real civic pride," Ms Nathiri said.
Ms. Boyd put it a little easier: "Zora saved Eatonville."
Victory against the trunk road project has contributed to a change in the city's self-image.
There were more and more arrivals, such as Rachelle Munson, a lawyer who has been going to the church here since 1993, and locals began reappreciating the past.
Eatonville was added to the statewide historical register in 1998.
A new one-storey library (named after Hurston, of course) opened in 2006 on the newly paved and embellished Kennedy Boulevard.
Nowadays, Eatonville is still a peculiarity in Florida: just 6 miles from Orlando's city center, it can sometimes feel like a back alley in the summer rain, as small as it was founded by only 27 black families in the 1880s.
(It’s 90% black these days.)
Outsiders in search of Eatonville's history and its significance are often still treated with caution.
For most interviews, a permit in advance is necessary and certain things - such as the wall painting of Eatonville's oldest church painted by a white and depicting black men in field work - must not be photographed.
Many in Eatonville, such as Ms Jones, a courageous, self-confident teacher with a fondness for dark red, still fear their shielded community being misunderstood.
And yet, at the same time when the Hurston festival was getting bigger, an increased degree of homeland pride was also born.
Young people, in particular, tend to see Eatonville as Hurston saw their entire race: beautiful, with all its problems, neither worse nor better and just as proud, creative and diligent, silly and mixed as any other racial and ethnic group in America.
It's the sincere love of this city you can hear in the voice of Mr Franklin's nephew, 18-year-old Edwin Harvey, who plans to go back to Eatonville after college to work in the city government or with the police who, according to him, could use some help.
And even the younger ones, like Alondra and Alexia Kenon, eleven-year-old twins from Winter Park, have learned how to describe Eatonville properly.
"Most people who only drive through here will think, "Oh, this city is nothing compared to all the others," Alondra Kenon said the other day on a Sunday after the church.
"But if you're actually stopping and looking at your story for a moment, it's a very beautiful city."
Here with us in New York, one speaks fluent financial Latin.
At first glance, nothing has changed.
If tourists in Manhattan stayed away from the newspaper stalls and the formidable television screens in Times Square, they wouldn’t even realize that they’re in the middle of a debacle.
They would never believe that above their heads, in the shoebox-like offices with the glass facades, bankers and hedge fund managers who have the monopolion in New York, daily have the worst horror working day since the 1929 stock market crash.
Suppliers are still using noisy rattles to push the metal tea trolleys along sidewalks, carrying trays loaded with muffins for breakfast sessions.
Latinos are still shripping around between apartments, like human pack animals loaded with around 50 ironed T-shirts hanging from a yoke-like pole lying on their shoulders.
Office workers are still waiting for their breakfast stallards on their sidewalks and it's still impossible to get a yellow taxi on a weekday between 4 p.m. and 7 p.m.
It didn't feel like a crisis at all at all until Thursday afternoon due to all the doomsday talks decided to open a new savings account.
Even though I had decided to put my duty on the quiet late afternoon in my JP Morgan Chase regular branch, there were already about 20 people queuing.
Not at the cash counters to withdraw or deposit money, but at the customer service desk where regular customers order checkbooks, ask tourists for their way to Macy's department store and where new customers open accounts.
Most in front of me, that's how I later learned from Starr, my "personal banker with a license," queued for the same reason.
Contrary to me, however, they had already withdrawn their savings from smaller banks in places from where they commuted, such as New Jersey, Pennsylvania and Staten Island.
They had withdrawn money from so-called thrifts (a bit similar to a building salvy), co-operative banks and that local savings banks that only have branches in one state and say they treat their customers like family.
Last year, 15 of these banks went quietly and quietly bankrupt.
In the last few days, Washington Mutual, a $327-billion-dollar band, went into bankruptcy administration, and as last Friday New Yorkers drove home over the weekend, Wachovia, another bank that owns $440 billion worth of savings assets, was looking for a rescuing buyer.
In the fear of a further collapse, the office and shopping employees who queued before me brought their cash to a bank that is considered a safe haven, a bank responsible for the most bailout purchases on Wall Street.
Normal New Yorkers have acquired fluent financial Latin in recent weeks.
A woman on the phone in my Chinese regular-super market referred to Lehman Brothers with so much familiarity, the bankrupt bank and its emaciated-looking chief executive Dick Fuld, as if blaspheming about a lazy colleague.
And Lyn, the 50-year-old uniformed concierge of my apartment block has strayed from our usual entertainment themes about Brad and Angelina and my food delivery.
"I've gotten all my savings into money market funds now," she told me after my visit to the bank.
“I’ve changed, I don’t take a risk anymore.”
Muslim tombs desecrated during right-wing slide in Austria
Heinz-Christian Strache, a left-wing right-wing political party of Austria, and Filip Dewinter, a member of Belgium's right-wing Pertei Vlaams Belang, take part in a demonstration in Antwerp on Thursday 17 January 20008 to represent the "cities against Islamization" organisation.
They wear signs in the style of traffic signs on which a mosque is pictured, crossed out with a red line.
Police accuse right-wing extremists of grave desecration at a Muslim cemetery in Austria, on the same weekend when far-right parties recorded significant gains in the country's parliamentary election.
In the night from Friday to this morning, more than 90 graves at the cemetery in Traun near Linz were severely damaged in an action organized according to police.
The perpetrators spray-painted Jewish symbols such as the David Stern on some of the tombs, but detectives consider this an attempt to disguise the motive of extremists driven by hatred of Muslim immigrants.
It's not clear whether the incident is in any way related to the elections.
A spokesperson for the local Islamic community said they were "deeply shaken" by the news of the grave desecration that took place as the religious month of Muslim Ramadan draws to an end.
At the same time, there is a round of research of conscience in Austria after the country slipped to the right in yesterday's parliamentary election.
The FPÖ and the Alliance for the future of Austria, the two far-right parties that received almost 30% of the votes, campaigned for a strict anti-immigrant law in their election campaign and some of their electoral rulings were criticized by opponents as xenophobic.
Heinz-Christian Strache, chairman of the FPÖ, which won 18% of the vote, campaigned for a ban on headscarves and burqas and even announced his aversion to food commonly associated with Islam.
At his last rally in Vienna, he spoke of a "European brotherhood" to prevent the growth of Islam.
Both parties want to ban the construction of mosques and minarets with the argument that these are political symbols of a continuous Islamization of Austria and Europe.
Polls and analyses carried out immediately after the election and which state that the far right is the politically strongest bloc in the country suggests that this new development is based on young voters worried about their future in the European Union.
Foreign travel ban for Gary Glitter
Gary Glitter, the glam rock star convicted of child sexual abuse, has been banned in court from travelling to France and Spain.
Judges slammed a six-month lock after informing the authorities controlling him that he wanted to travel to Spain via France.
Police in Kent were granted last Thursday to impose a foreign travel ban after presenting evidence of Glitter's criminal record for child sexual abuse.
Glitter, real name Paul Gadd, did not attend the hearing at the Ashford district court in Kent, but was represented by law firm Corker Binning.
On Thursday, September 25, a spokesperson at Ashford Local Court police requested permission to prevent foreign travel from Mr. Paul Glad to France and Spain until March 25.
Paul Gadd wasn’t present in person.”
As required, according to the sex offenders register, Glitter, 64, had informed the authorities that he wanted to travel abroad.
He must inform the authorities if he wants to travel abroad for more than three days.
He must also notify the police 7 days prior to his scheduled departure.
Failure to notify the authorities could lead to a 5-year prison sentence.
Police in Kent said she would not discuss individual cases or Glitter's possible whereabouts in the country.
A spokeswoman for Kent's police unit said: "The police in Kent will do everything necessary to protect children in this country and abroad."
"In our view, it is easier to control someone who is located in the UK because we have a comprehensive system to effectively prevent any potential risks."
"It is a top concern of the police in Kent to protect the inhabitants of Kent and we work closely together to monitor the behavior of registered sex offenders in the country in order to minimize any risk that could exist for the population.
Glitter returned to the UK last month after spending 27 months in a Vietnamese prison for abusing 2 girls, aged 10 and 11.
When he was released, Glitter flew to Bangkok, where he was scheduled to take a flight to London.
But he didn't feel well and refused to fly to London.
Thain's authorities didn't let him through passport control and he flew back and forth to Hong Kong after some.
The Chinese authorities also denied him entry and he flew back to Bangkok, where Thai police and borders insisted that he return to Britain.
Last but not least, he arrived at Terminal 3 at Heathrow on August 22, where his lawyer David Corker went to Uxbridge District Court in West London, since Scotland Yard had successfully asked for permission to request that he register as a sex offender.
Glitter had previously been imprisoned in the United Kind for possession of images of child abuse in 1999.
According to the Ministry of the Interior, Interior Minister Jacqui Smith was reassured and satisfied that Glitter was deterred from travelling to France and Spain.
`I receive the master behind the stage to capture his character', Günter Brauer, photographer.
No heels, no ruffles, no costumes for appearance.
The nature of the great flamenco dancers, who renounce the concentration and dedicate themselves to the interpretation in order to get a relaxed appearance as a woman or as a man.
On the one hand artists
On The Other Hand Personality
I look into private life, into these moments that are strangers to the audience, I know when I can find typical gesture for the personality, the depth of a stolen gaze or expression strength of the hands that are concerned because they won't enter the stage.
In his project `Flamenco-people', the German photographer Günter Bauer portrays varied theatrics of some productions on stage with singers, dancers and guitarists. From this side he was still little known for the artists.
With this proposal, the photographer creates a special iconography of flamenco representation on different levels.
The exhibition, which belongs to the program of the month of photography of flamenco, reflects the international importance of flamenco, which is increasing from day to day," explains the artist and makes it clear that `Flamenco-people' `can be any person, but if you look at the photos, the viewer realizes that there are no typical images of artists on stage.
Normal people on the portraits are artists who are able on stage to convey passion, sadness, joy and thus move the audience.
In order to capture the personality of these artists, you don't have to take photos on stage.
Her personality and character are in her hearts.
What interested me was to create a personal atmosphere that would allow me to capture the characters of the greatest flamenco masters with the photo camera.
Bauer, inspired by the `Cante de las Minas′′ festival, embarked on a long journey in 1997 to photograph singers, guitarists or dancers with a digital camera.
Thanks to the image sorting function of the camera and the existing material, images were created reminiscent of classical photography.
The application of various stylistic means destroys the classical basis of the image, giving the picture the modernity and the idea of how the farmer Flamenco understands: `music with traditional roots that changes and surprises every day'.
These are not only dance, singing or instrumental playing itself, but the personality of the artist, his character who seduces the audience with his art.
The search for the essence, for a certain something, which becomes visible in the form of listening, of being, is and was the fixed goal of this work, which will be presented in the gallery of El Fotómata in the near future.
Famous personalities in flamenco among them Diego El Cigala, Chano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos, Enrique Morente, El Lebrijano, Antonio Canales, Vicente Amigo and others belong to the narrow selection of the nearly seventy artists who were photographed.
The project became an anthology of flamenco and will soon appear as a book, with all portraits from the exhibition.
Bauer’s passion for the portraits led him to take up this idea again two years ago and to be realized as `Portraits of the veterans of sport' (`SportARTproject').
Although the concepts of the `Flamenco-people′ `Flamenco-people′′ are different, so the photographer admits `I really like to photograph the people, get to know them and put me in their state of life, whether for a short moment or a longer time.
I think that in the field of photography the portrait is the most difficult genre.
You meet or come across people, personalities you sometimes don’t know at all, or they’ll leave you alone for a few minutes to take a photo.
Apart from these circumstances, as a photographer I always try to realize my ideas.”
The collection of portraits of flamenco today entitled `Flamenco-people' is exhibited in the El Fotómata gallery, located in the street called la calle de la Mata and runs parallel to La Alameda de Hércules street. The exhibition can be visited until 16 October.
Vargas Llosa confesses that the Roma he just writes is `a madness.'
He doesn’t yet know where the novel’s plot takes place and what it’s about, but Mario Varga’s Llosa persistently tries to advance the project he’s been working on right now, a novel with protagonist Roger Casemant, a nationalist from Ireland who lived in Congo for twenty years and moved to the Amazon.
Yesterday, during his presence at the Hay Festival Segovia, the preferred Nobel laureate admitted that the project with which he deals is `a toll-audacity' and 'everything points out that he wouldn't have to write the novel'.
`If I were prudent, I wouldn't have done it, but I'm going to do it and I hope to finish the novel at some point', so he graduated from the festival, where the ideas, thoughts and words were exchanged. Within the four days, he saw hundreds of writers, historians, journalists and thousands of people who were in Segovia.
The author of `La ciudad y los perros' was one of the most outstanding personalities at the third festival and made predictions.
Vargas Llosa, who has won Premium of the Festival Hay Festival and to whom most attendees gave her vote, is compared to the old edition of `Our mutual friend' and can be seen at Juan Bravo Theatre.
He was introduced by Félix Romeo and found that he had an audience in front of him, which realized that the Peruvian, who possesses a wonderful memory and a great sense of humour, was not given sufficient time to be able to go through everything again: how his vocation to literature arose, which emerged from `extraordinary adventurousness', from `joy in reading' and from `more wants to experience 'more than before'
The Hispano-American novelist acknowledged that there is 'no way' in which you can measure and how to know whether books change lives, `but I refuse to believe that literature is pure conversation, is a fleeting experience,' emphasized Vargas Llosa, who claims that it evokes `effections' and `puts the people even more apart', therefore
At the festival in Pozoblanco, the weather did not play along: bullfighting festivals were partially cancelled.
After the announced bullfighters determined that the bottom of the course was in poor condition, the bullfighting festival, with which the festival in Pozoblanco was to be concluded, was moved to yesterday morning.
Thus, with the consent of Fermín Bohórquez, Pablo Hermoso de Mendoza and Leonardo Hernández, the organizer has decided to postpone the bullfighting festival.
Thus, the Feast was subjected to unstable weather, which is why two out of three celebrations could not take place.
On Saturday it rained, which is why the second planned bullfighting festival could not be celebrated.
The Matadore group consisted of José Luis Moreno, Curro Días and Juan Bautista, who gradually entered the bullfighting arena.
There was only one celebration on Friday.
The winner there was Daniel Luque, who cut three ears and triumphed, according to which he had left the passage with pleasure and aesthetics.
On the same evening Alejandro Talavante met two horns and an ear of the bull, which he had succeeded more by galloping than by his bullfighting art.
And even worse was El Juli, which passed through the Arena de Los Llanos without difficulty, but also without glory.
After fighting so badly, much of the audience reacted with boh shouts in his second fight.
In Álava, two people wanted to escape the control of their car, which was loaded with two thousand kilos of hashish.
Tomorrow: five minutes before ten.
Traffic controls in Ertzaintza, in the urban area of Armiñon (Álava).
Two police officers asked the drivers of the car to stop, whereupon they fled.
It looked like they didn't have to stop with the load of more than two thousand kilos of hashish.
The police pursued them and arrested one of the drivers, the man, who is 28 years old, known as A. A. J., according to the interior ministry of the Basque state.
The arrested person has been accused of drug trafficking and forgery of official documents.
The incident occurred as Álava's traffic police officers had already completed traffic control on the N-1 and were driving passing through the Armiñon urban area.
They decided to stop a car to carry out the route control. The driver did not follow the instructions, suddenly increased the speed and tried to evade control.
The polices began the chase until the car stopped after a few kilometers on the edge strip. At that moment, the drivers got out of the car to flee across a country road into a wooded area.
Ertzaintza police finally caught up with one of the two. In the car, an Iveco Daily, the drug traffickers were transporting 2.178 kilos of hashish, which was seized by police.
The front part of the car was loaded with hashish bundles, which were later routed to other police departments for accurate laboratory analysis and weighing the bundles.
In addition, the officials determined that the license plate was `double', so that it did not match the license plate specified in the vehicle papers, which is why there was also an arrest for forgery of official documents.
Ertzaintza police are continuing the investigation to carry out the arrest of the second suspect, who, as already mentioned, was able to flee, but also to determine the origin of the seized drugs.
Arrested 28-year-old A. A. J., has been taken to a police station for interrogation. He will be presented to the magistrate in the next few hours.
Actress Heather Locklear has been arrested for driving under the effect of an unknown drug.
Actress Heather Locklear, known through Amanda's role from the "Melrose Place" series, was arrested this weekend in Santa Barbara, California, for driving under the effect of an unknown drug.
One witness had observed how she tried in a rather strange way to get out of her parking space in Montecito, spokesman for the traffic police of California told the magazine `People'.
The witness recounted in detail that Locklear `pushed through accelerator after 4:30 p.m. and made a lot of noise by trying to move her car backwards or forward out of the parking space, and when she drove backwards, she grabbed her sunglasses a few times.'
A little later, the witness, who initially the actress probably didn't realize Locklear stopped on a nearby road and got out of the car.
The witness was the person who notified the emergency service because she was `fear for Locklears live'.
When the police patrols arrived, they found the actress sitting in her car, which stood across the street, blocking traffic.
`She seemed disoriented,' that's why they took the cops to a special drug and alcohol center to take a test.
According to a police spokesman, the actress appeared cooperative, excessive alcohol consumption was ruled out from the first moment. `According to the first observations of the officers, we believe Locklear was under the influence of an unknown drug,' according to the spokesman.
Locklear was arrested under suspicion of driving under the influence of an indefinite substance that could not be determined and taken to local custody around 7 p.m.
Two months ago Locklaer was discharged after receiving treatment from the clinic in Arizona, where she was treated for anxiety and depression.
Llamazares insists that the United Leagues Association of the United Leagues will be responsible for the Silva case.
Gaspar Llamazares, the main coordinator of the VL and a member of the United League, has today assured that the regional association for education will be the one who will make the decision in the case of the former City Council for Youth and Sport City Hall in Seville, Francisco Manuel Silva, who resigned last week after being accused of doing business with family members.
`In such cases I am a great federalist, and the Andalusian organization will be the one who has to take over the responsibility for it," Llamazares pointed out what approach the party must have towards the one who, until now, sits as a city councillor on the municipal council of Seville.
Nevertheless, the chairman of the VL defended the necessity of 'transparency' in the possible investigations of the rule violations that Silva may have begun: `The VL always insisted that such cases receive maximum transparency'.
With regard to this issue, he emphasized that education has 'a strict ethical code for all public offices'.
`Sangre de mayo': Hero's book by Garci
Inspired by 'Episodios Nacionales' by Benito Pérez Galdós, José Luis Carci in `Sangre de mayo' represents the revised version of the famous uprising of the Spaniards against the Spaniards against the French. This is an admirable film in filmography, with about 15 million euros investment for filming.
In this way, the Cineast has tried `to represent a lot of personalities and social analysis', which he has always admired in the works of the famous novelist, Carci explained today during the presentation of the film with Quim Gutiérrez and Paula Echevarría at the beginning of a long directory of the performers.
`Sangre de mayo' with a film length of more than two and a half hours, will be presented this Friday in cinemas and will be shown later on Telemadrid as a four-hour miniseries.
José Luis Garci, a big fan of Gald�ós literature, assures that thanks to this filminging work, his `childhood dream' came true. This is thanks to Esperanza Aguirre, who commissioned him three years ago by the Autonomous Community of Madrid to make a commemorative film for the second-century ceremony on November 2 in Madrid. Above all, because the story was based on 'Episodios Nacionales'
Garci's total contribution of 15 million, which was set for filming by the Autonomous Community of Madrid, offers the `once-in-a-lifetime possibility' with the help of decorations and appropriate clothes the image of 'confused Spain, which selected the false leader for the struggle, because the people had been resigned by the struggle for independence and under the dominance of the Ferdinand of VII.'
However, the Cineast regrets that you couldn't count on the financing as with `Alatriste' or `Los fantasmas de Goya' (twice as high as with `Sangre de mayo'), which is why some scenes from the script could not be filmed with Horacio Valcárcel, with whom Garci worked almost his entire career.
For this reason, Garci, who was also film director in `Volver a empezzar', contributed through his usual way of working: he stood on stage to the actors with whom you first have to rehearse before you start shooting and for those who hadn't done a casting, he seemed `humiliating' and giving respect, `a part of the profession of the film director is
Many performers he comes across in `Sangre de mayo' have to repeat in front of his camera, as in the case of Miguel Rellán, Carlos Larrañaga or Fernando Guillén Cuervo.
Garci, Oscar candidate in four nominations (of whom he won one), is of the opinion and assured that none of the three contestants representing Spain in front of the Academy of Hollywood this year represents a young generation of Spanish filmography. Neither `Sangre de mayo', nor `Siete mesas de billar francés' and, after all, not the selected film `Los girasoles ciegos'
Garci doesn't expect any awards for this film.
`In the beginning, you believe that you work for the progeny, later you realize that it's only for flourishing, and now you're satisfied when you've still produced a film," Garci explains after more than 30 years of professional activity.
Isabel Bayón recalls the memory of the dancer from Sevilla Tórtola Valencia
A flamenco dancer from Seville reveres a dancer who is also from Seville.
Carmen Tórtola Valencia: her father was Katalane (Florenç Tórtola Ferrer) and her mother Andalusian (Georgina Valencia Valenzuela), she was born in the Triana neighborhood and when she was three years old, her family has moved to London.
She was a dancer and an expert on oriental dances.
She had an exciting life, she was one of the first female fighters for the emancipation of the woman, such as Isadora Ducan, Virginia Woolf or Sarah Bernhardt.
She was a Buddhist, a vegetarian and advocated the abolition of the corset, which restricted free female movement.
The life of this woman, who left her mark on the dance and aesthetic revolution in Europe in the time between the wars, fascinated Isabel Bayón.
`I wanted to work with Pepa Gamboa and she proposed this role to me, which seemed impossible for me.'
“We don’t try to talk about their lives but preserve their spirit,” said the flamenco dancer.
Your dance basics have helped me a lot in flamenco.
`The dance of the butterfly' occurred to me, for example, like Guajira, or `the dance of the serpent' like tango.
Her dancing was sensual and I have been told that my dancing is also in this aesthetic realm," she claims.
The staging expects two extraordinary collaborations: Miguel Poveda, who will once again sing for Isabel at one of her performances, as he did in `La puerta abierta'; and with flamenco master Matilde Coral, who exceptionally wanted to work together with one of her ex-students.
`Matilde plays Tórtola, a very mature personality.
I'm happy because she agreed to participate in my work, and I'm very honored.'
The staging also reckons with musical composition and the guitarists Jesús Torres and Paco Arriaga, with the vocals of Miguel Ortega, el Pulga and Moi de Morón; with the percussion of El Pájaro, as well as the viola of Rafa el Viola and the cooperation with the actor Juafra Júarez, he `takes over the role
In this work it is also told in a certain way about the life of this woman, who became known in places as far as India, the United States or Russia and also throughout Europe, even though she usually lived in London.
`I wanted to recall them, because I think she is one of the women who have an enormous influence on a certain epoch and whose works today no one appreciates anymore and because of it she appreciates even less,′′," Bayón asserts.
The performance will take place today at 20:30 at Theater Lope de Vega.
The publication of the book `Diario de un poeta recién casado' also includes the Triennium
The presentation of the first illustrated edition of `Diario de un poeta recién casado' at Saint Stephen's Church (New York), in a place where Juan Ramón Jiménez married Zenobia in 1916, provides a corresponding atmosphere to carry out planned activities on behalf of the Andalusian Council for Culture to celebrate the `Trennium of Zenobia and Juan Ram�ó
Thus `the Triennium of Zenobia and Juan Ramón′ will be completed with the new edition of this work, with the international congress taking place in New York, with the restoration of the house-museum of Juan Ramón Jiménez-Zenobia and with the travelling exhibition `Juan Ramón Jiménez: `Aquel chopo de luz', which was opened in May
The Andalusian Council for Culture has also received eight hundred copies of `Diario de un poeta recién casado', which were edited by Pedro Tabernero and illustrated by Pacobo Pérez-Enciso. The goal is to make the person and work of the Andalusian world poet better known.
The U.S. can't imagine without Paul Newman
“Sometimes God creates perfect personalities.
Paul Newman was one of them.”
This sentence comes from the actress Sally Field, who collaborated with him in `Sensationsreporterin', and from the then prevailing notion of U.S. cinema, which was abandoned, came from the whole of America to Newman with his thrilling notion and followed her.
Have a look at how it took them to give him the Oscar.
And when he withdrew in 2007.
And he knew he was very sick.
Nevertheless, this was a big blow for everyone.
`What one sometimes feels is considerably more than an expression, and now I've lost a great friend,' Robert Redford said crying, 'my life and my country were the greatest drolliness of his life.'
No one was terribule to neither ridicule nor exaggerate.
As 'Father' of cinema and fatherland, he has died at a sad moment when there is fear of terrorists and of the market collapse, in which the dejected self-esteem of Americans is in the hopeless quest for reasons to regain dignity.
If, in this hard time without virtue, only a few people you can admire, you don't necessarily have to be Luis Cernuda to love Paul Newman.
For all, he embodied a platonic ideal of beauty and truth.
He was the official enemy of Richard Nixon and, like various losses, suffered both profits: from the foundation, which is committed to the fight against drugs and was founded in honor of his son from the first marriage, who died of overdose, to his own brand of homemade and organic products.
All this is a gift for the country, in which it took a lot of trouble to prevail against a deadly food culture.
A broad wave of astonishment strikes house-high over Hollywood regardless of nationalities and nationality.
The last James Bond, Englishman Daniel Craig, confirmed that not just one person has died, but the whole era.
Englander Sam Mendes, film director and Oscar winner, said working with Newman was no more or nothing less than the top of his career.
Russell Crowe, the bad boy who came from Australia, dared to describe Newman as his greatest inspiration.
“The greatest of all of us has left us,” confirmed George Clooney, perhaps one of a few who try to live like Paul Newman lived today: high demands on righteousness, lead a responsible private life, far from the cameras.
That's not always easy or not even possible in today's media jungle.
But there's someone who's trying, and Paul Newman was an example.
He divorced once and was married to Joanne Woodward for fifty years afterwards, which he was loyal to, although there were certainly many opportunities not to be.
`Why should I eat a hamburger on the street if I've fillet at home?', he often joked himself.
In heaven, he will be proud that on earth his daughters have the same opinion as his fans, which isn't necessarily common in America.
“Of all the great roles he played, he was not as proud of that of the faithful spouse, the loving father, the observant grandpa, and that of the philanthropist,” they say.
For the Olympics, the streets in Madrid are deficient.
Yes, the dream of many of us is to host some Olympic disciplines in Madrid, after the failure for 2012, it may perhaps be in 2016.
But I wonder, 'Are we prepared for this?'
Yes, we follow the example of the old streets, I say it's not true!
It was described as a perfect road, and according to my assessment, it was the worst road with everything there was.
Of course, that's a lot of nonsense, if one were to heed the assessment and I could examine it, something like this clearly wouldn't have come through.
But, let's go through everything from the beginning.
Nonsense begins with the ticket pick-up, which allows you to go first.
It doesn't seem bad to me either, but if they're already going ahead, then they're supposed to give you everything (including chip to avoid traffic jams on the road).
Well, don't.
Here they only let you get the ticket (the first nonsense that consists of going there only because of it) and (after an infinite queue) there's the surprise! The T-shirt there was only in size `S' (a second nonsense, because I need size XL)
Where do we actually live?
Or is it the case that after the crisis it was decided to buy only small, much cheaper shirts made of less fabric?
All right, things like this are a trifle compared to the days on the street.
Apart from having to go very early to get the chip, there is already a queue of at least 15 minutes and, in addition, you have to leave the backpack.
The waiting took more than 35 minutes, which is incomprehensible in the case of a street where 11 000 people are expected.
Finally, as it was to be expected, the road started and we hadn’t left our backpacks ahead, even if it had once succeeded, but bringing it back would be more difficult.
In addition, it looked, in these 40 minutes waiting after rain, so that it came to the temptation to organize a popular uprising and to raid the cottage.
I don’t think tomorrow some 500 people will have pneumonia and some like me won’t even show up to the classification in time because of this chip I’ve gotten, I can already imagine that the exit has been stamped incorrectly.
Of course, if I were asked for a perfect street, this street would never come to mind.
So I believe we're going to dream for a long time about a city that's well prepared for the Olympics
Microsoft looking for a (virtual) receptionist
At the `Emerging Technology Conference', organized by MIT, Microsoft has presented the progress of the new image recognition system and human-interaction, a essence of artificial intelligence without a lot of intelligence, which, however, is capable of doing some primitive tasks, such as ordering a taxi (or in the case of Microsoft, that's a route planner to move on the company's campus). The system heeds what it hears during entertainment.
Thanks to a camera and a shape and facial recognition system, this `receptionist' can recognize how many people are in the room, who they are and even remember how they were clothed, it can listen and understand the keywords of their conversation.
It can also classify those present into the groups after the talks.
In the presentation video, `the receptionist' notices a third person, but she understands that this person does not belong to the group that has a conversation with it, and that there is no company employee either.
She asks if the person here wants to visit someone and responds that she'll be listening soon.
All without the blue screenshot
Impressive
This is certainly the future.
Now the company has better stuff to focus on, for example on the next version of their Windows operating system.
This week spoke very superficially about Microsoft's change of strategy.
Windows 7 will be faster and won’t have as many integrated programs.
Steve Ballmer found that if the computers were equipped with the necessities, instead of filling them with the commercial garbage, they would work five times faster. Mensch, Steven, that will only be for the few.
Everything is just a statement, considering that Microsoft added one or the other function and `Extras' in Windows versions.
The change of direction is worthwhile
Microsoft is right, the operating system is gradually losing its importance, just like the software.
When Google Chrome appeared on the net, it was part of the theory that it was Google’s first move to compete with Windows.
Skilfully, many pointed out that the browser is not an operating system.
The operating system, as an indispensable part, will always be necessary for hardware and software to work at the same time.
The operating system fulfills the function that can be compared to the function of his wife or his husband when it comes to telling his mother-in-law something.
That's right. That's right.
The system will never disappear, but you have to think of his `death', not as an absolute, but as relative truth.
Whas Ballmer said this week is confirmation of that idea.
People pay less attention to the operating system every time, but more to the web browser.
The computer is only started to run Firefox, Explorer or Chrome and every day we spend more time surfing than in the surroundings of the `windows'.
What does half of existing applications in the computer serve for?
Less is more
And Windows 7 needs to be more compact, much more compact than Vista.
Easier, not so exaggerated, less complicated.
Microsoft's interest is more in integrating its online services, which can then eventually become the alternative to Google.
In reality, they need that, no doubt.
I took the picture of this place in Redmond, in one of the recreation rooms for Microsoft employees earlier this month.
On the soft drink vending machine is attached a sign: `Your search. Our future'. It expresses a request to use your own tool for online search.
In 2006, an independent study concluded that the 66% of employees at Microsoft used Google to research on the net.
By the way, last week it was also confirmed that the new version of the Windows Mobile 7 was delayed.
It will not appear at the beginning, but at the end of 2009, rather even in 2010.
Microsoft is looking for the possibility to bring Windows 7 and Windows Mobile 7 to the mark at the same time.
Every day the phone turns into a computer.
The Android and iPhon are already on the market, but the other delay will cost money.
Le Fabuleux Marcel
Pabo Picasso has worn it in Costa Azul, Leonardo di Caprio had this brand in `Blood Diamond' by Edward Zwick, just like Al Pacino in `The Godfather' by Francis Ford Coppola. At the same time, it belongs to clothing of a well-built pastor.
If we begin to enumerate the myriad Stars performances, we can draw back on the image of a very strong man, with a pointed beard who had raised heavyweights in a traveling circus.
Essentially, that's a typically male t-shirt made of cotton, sleeveless and fitted.
The English speakers call `tank'. However, Lle Fabuleux Marcel de Bruxelles surpasses all other brands.
First, because of his great name, even though she is called `Marcel' in a narrow circle. The legend recounts that the naming is associated with sports and comes from boxer Marcel Cerdan, the lover of the singer Edith Piaf.
`La Marcel′ overlooks everyone because of his excellent cotton, because of his distinguishing unisex cut and is characterized by a small Belgian flag (hardly recognizable) who have these T-shirts as a detail on the neckline.
And beware, the Belgian company does not only focus on the production of `tanks'.
They also have college sweatshirts, boxer retro, cardigans and cashmere, cotton trousers, T-shirts with stripes, babies, mini shorts
Modelled and comfortable underweards.
Although, it's a pity to use them as underwear.
They're very charming.
`I want to be a young mother; I don't worry about the contestant'
Her great demeanour with her sister did not prevent her from developing her own personality.
Mónica Cruz is dainty but full of joie de vivre.
At the moment, the Gruz siblings are taking their new activity in fashion very seriously.
For Mónica, this means much more than for some pretty and rich girls who preserve their reputation.
She was in Paris at the promotion of the company Mango, transferred her residence and also makes the presentation of the most important bonuses in fashion, which the Spanish fashion house sponsors.
Merciful and shy, winner on the short tracks.
Tell us what you're doing in Paris.
I’ve come to present the new collection for this fall/winter season that my sister Penélope and I designed for Mango, I’ve also attended the presentation of the second edition of `Mango Fashion Awards’ and attended Valentino’s dinner.
A very intense working time, which is exciting at the same time.
Do you work for the Spanish house, what is the basis of your new collection for the fourth season for Mango?
My sister, both I are fascinated by the cinema of the 50's and 60's; that's where we find a lot of ideas.
I like to walk through a city and observe women as they are clothed, how they combine their garments, that's what I often do.
Dancer, actress and fashion designer, you're a very versatile woman, isn't she?
I believe yes, but finally, everything meets in one and these areas have a lot in common.
Ever since I was little, I've been excited about the clothes; I've always wanted to go to my grandmother's to rummage around in my aunt's wardrobes and see her clothes.
That was great to go almost better than go to an amusement park.
Even later, as a dancer, I always tried to pay attention to clothing.
Yes, you know, we dancers like it so much to have our own style in lessons and rehearsals in order to look in the mirror and feel good about it.
As an actress, when you get the costume, you put yourself in the real actor role.
After all, everything is connected, it's so, a lot of respect for the costume designer and her professionalism.
Recently you've turned 30, haven't you fallen into crisis?
Oh, what.
I'm feeling better than ever, as a thirty-year-old I'm feeling
I think the woman becomes mature, has experience and can enjoy life with less mistakes.
I'm not scared because of my biological clock.
I wanted to do the things I've been doing since I was little and that gave me a lot of inner joy.
So, aren't you thinking of becoming a mother, isn't it?
For a long time I have known that I will become a mother, because my body demands it and I also want to be a young mother; I don't want to miss it.
As for the candidate, I'm not worried.
I'm going to impart great feelings of happiness to my child and raise them with a lot of love.
As far as film art is concerned, you keep an eye on the works that you participated in and were filmed last year.
That's exactly how it is and I really want them to be presented to the public.
Me, as a woman who comes from the theatre, am very impatient and restless and wants to have everything at once, which of course can't be immediate.
I very much hope that `La última hora' and `En busca de la tumba de Cristo' will be performed for the first time.
Let's see how you like it
A few days ago, you appeared on the walk with Naomi Campbell, right?
It's okay, don't say that.
It's not that I went on the running climb to model.
Naomi Campbell, whom I've known for some time, just called me and invited me to London to attend a parade for the charity event of her organization 'Women for a Better World' and the clothes we're shaving. I know that two garments from our collection for mango have been auctioned off to raise money for Naomis NGO.
People don't know Naomi's good heart.
There's a lot of things being told about her. I, as the one she knows, would tell you they're wrong.
Nevertheless, I will support this good cause in which she is involved.
She asked me to participate in the parade for this big-hearted cause, but if she comes and asks me to make a handstand, I'll do that too.
In order to support women in the Third World, which is also necessary.
The motorists, who can relapse because of alcohol levels, will have to install the `Alcolock'.
The authority orders that the driver, who had three official sentences because of the level of alcohol, installs in his car this system, which prohibits him from starting the car in the drunken state. If he is alcoholic, then the car does not start.
The initiative was already announced last month in June and is already a part of the report of the Prosecutor-General. According to their information, it is a pilot project, which will last for a year.
The purpose is the resocialization of the driver, which is whhere he gets a course for re-educating traffic behavior.
In addition to the Prosecutor General’s Office, the Ministry of Transport and the Foundation of the Technical Institute for Automobile Safety will also participate.
According to statistics, between 30-75% of motorists who were reported more than once because of driving under the influence of alcohol put themselves back on the wheel.
The `alcolock' would prevent this because it is a system connected to the car, it measures the alcohol content in the exhaled air and prevents autostart if the driver has taken an alcohol test; or if an alcohol concentration is detected that is higher than allowed.
It costs more than 1 000 euros, the costs are borne by the one who imposes the sanction.
In this context, a study carried out in the U.S. in 2007 has shown that cars equipped with these devices prevent up to 65% of rule violations already in the first year after its introduction.
In Spain, it is expected to save 115 lives annually.
The measure is limited to administrative sanctions for two reasons: tackling this behaviour before it becomes a criminal offence.
And in order to facilitate its implementation, a lawful reform can be realized in the ordinary way, and yet a reform of the Basic Law, which is necessary for the prosecution process, is called for.
The device is not yet available in the Spanish markets, although it has already been installed in some commercial vehicles and public transport vehicles.
Just tomorrow, the House of Representatives will vote because of the initiative of the Spanish Socialist Workers Party, which proposes to install this system in school buses. This has already been done in France.
In Mexico and without `peep-toes'
