Embraer sticks to its supply forecasts for 2008
The Brazilian aircraft maker expects 215 aircraft delivered this year, despite the looming recession, as a threat to the global economy.
Embraer, the world's fourth-largest aerial vehicle manufacturer is complying with its delivery forecasts for 2008 with 215 aircraft delivered, reaffirmed its President Fredrico Fleury Curado.
In the first half of the year, the aircraft manufacturer delivered 97 aircraft.
The Brazilian manufacturer expects much from the Chinese market, especially for its planes of 30 to 120 seats.
To date, Embraer has delivered 41 machines to Chinese customers in eight years.
AIG would consider handing over more than 15% of its assets
According to Sunday's Financial Times edition, insurer American International Group, which has been financially assisted by the U.S. monetary authority, is considering handing over more than 15% of its assets to repay the 85-billion-dollar dollars it've been advanced.
AIG is ready to target the sale of much of its dealings, beyond its life insurance on an international level and its retirement savings in the United States.
According to the FT, AIG's board of directors met Sunday evening to review possible sales.
No confirmation could be obtained at AIG.
Assets that AIG would potentially separate from include its International Lease Financial Corpor. aircraft leasing store, its 59% share of reinsurer Transatlantic Holdings, as well as its real estate assets and investments in private equity.
No final decision has been made regarding these sales, the FT continues.
AIG will have to make sales as soon as possible in order to repay the loans over 85 billion it has granted the federal reserve, even at the risk that the American authorities will receive 80% of their capital and the current shareholders will be lost to the same extent.
Analysis Historical Paris of Session Historical Morning Meeting
The New York Stock Exchange reported a historic drop in prices on Monday, after the Repository Chamber rejected the Paulson plan to bail out banks: The Dow Jones lost 6.98%, an unprecedented loss of nearly 800 points, and the Nasdag 9.14%.
The Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJIA) is down 777.68 points to 10,365.45 points.
As far as points are concerned, the strongest fall in its history (684.81 points) had previously been recorded on 17 September 2001, the date of the decline in trade following the terrorist attacks of 11 September.
According to definitive firing figures, the Nasdag index, mainly in terms of technology, has fallen by 199.61 points to 1,983.73, and the widely usual Standard&Poors 500 index fell by 8.81% (106.85 points) to 1,106.55 points.
Following the unexpected rejection of the plan to bail out banks by the Chamber of Deputies, which was again supposed to enable some stability in the markets, Wall Street collapsed, which was already battered at the start of the quarter.
By 228 votes against and 205 in favour, the deputies denied Finance Secretary Henry Paulson the funds he had asked for.
Nearly two out of three Republicans rejected his plan.
`Here, a bad picture is circulated that portrays Congress as incapable of adopting a plan," said Lindsey Piegza of FTN Financial.
`This shows how much the market counted on this plan,' she added, and fears that a project `too late' adopted in several days.
`If the project is indeed doomed to failure (...), it will quickly be the task of the Fed (the American central bank) and other central banks to get to grips with the crisis, which would limit credit conditions even more,« warned Brian Bethune, head of business at Global Insight.
And Miss Piegza emphasized, highlighting the feeling of utmost urgency even more, `that every day that escapes the ruin of a financial enterprise' in the United States.
Following the bankruptcy of Washington Mutual on Friday, the American authorities planned to buy up Wachovia's bankings by its competitor Citigroup.
In Europe, the cross suddenly accelerated and thus led to emergency measures in favour of banks in the United Kingdom, in the Benelux countries, Iceland and Germany, an indication that the markets are facing `not only an American but a worldwide crisis', the FTN Financial analyst added.
The market for basic demand goods has grown strongly.
The revenues of the state treasury within ten years have declined Friday evening from 3.632% to 3.827%, and revenue within 30 years last night from 4.161% to 4.357%.
Obama, winner after points in first televised duel
Friday's entire debate of the two candidates for the White House was followed by another to find out who emerged victorious, John McCain or Barack Obama.
As expecteded, the QGs announced their own victory in this televised duel, which was to be followed by one-third of American families.
Political analyst David Gergen estimates John McCain's performance, though quite handsome, was not `brilliant' enough to allow him to still stop his decline in electoral polls five weeks before the presidential elections.
The Gallup Institute, of which an opinion poll was released on Saturday, according to Obama with five points ahead (49% to 44% for McCain), thinks the Democrat is clearly making the better figure.
Among still undecided voters, i.e. those who matter now, Obama emerged victorious from the polls with 39%, while according to a CBS poll, 25% of them see McCain as victorious.
Obama should be able to convict his lead on Thursday with the good feat expected by his competitor Joseph Biden at first and only public debate with his rival Sarah Palin
“It would be a mistake to underestimate them.
She've got good instincts,' but warned Bill Clinton this weekend.
The seventy-year-old McCain did not cease to focus on his undeniable experience in the field of foreign policy, and at the same time clearly underlined his opponent’s ‘naivety’ regarding these questions, which formed the focus of this first television duel.
`Senator Obama doesn't seem to understand...," he emphasized several times.
“I don’t think Senator Obama has the necessary experience and the knowledge needed to become president,” he said.
One point of attenuation that has been preached in Republican election commercials since yesterday.
Obama himself emphasized that he represents the future.
He expressed himself briefly and precisely, without being upset.
He came to the forebance of his ability to make good decisions and renew the image the world has of the United States.
`McCain still hasn’t understood, but Obama does,” he said this weekend a Democratic electoral commercial in which Republicans were accused of failing to capture the full extent of the crisis that weighed on the ‘American from the street.’
"During the 90 minutes of the debate, John McCain had a lot of things to say about me, but nothing about you," Obama denounced yesterday in front of 20,000 people who had gathered in pouring rain at an election event in North Carolina.
He didn’t even mention the words `mediciary’ or `workers.’ The financial crisis gives Obama, whom Americans consider most suitable to save the economy even on the edge of the abyss, a clear advantage.
`The coming week could be a decisive turning point,« the influential Democratic senator Charles Schumer cherished yesterday.
Democratic representative Rahm Emanuel, a former member of the Clinton administration, compared Obama's 'good economic instinctions' with those of Bill Clinton during the 1998 financial crisis.
For the Democratic nominee, the current crisis is “the finishing verdict of an eight-year-old bad economic policy implemented by George Bush and backed by Senator McCain.”
Having emphasized again that he would arguably even manage to lower taxes for 95% of Americans despite fluctuations on Wall Street, the Illinois senator pointed out that the creation of the country’s possible energetic independence and the promotion of renewable energies, along with the introduction of a health insurance system, had a top priority for him.
McCain, on the other hand, wanted to build a plant of 45 nuclear power plants by 2030.
The two candidates carefully dodged to the questions, which is based on the plan to save the financial market over 700 billion dollars (470 billion euros), which was passed by Congress last night and is extremely unpopular with the people.
'Did you notice an election was taking place in Minsk?'
Youths and opposition activists demonstrated Sunday evening in the main square of the Belarusian capital in order to reject the results of the parliamentary elections in advance.
A few hundred Belarusians gathered Sunday night since polling stations in Minsk's market square closed to protest the fraudulent nature of parliamentary elections.
Although no result had yet been announced, numerous youths had come to demonstrate against the power of Alexander Lukachenko: `The dictator should resignate'.
Some admit that they didn't vote in the first place: `What good is this, if they do what they want with my voice anyway,' says a teenager.
They do not believe that the regime, which has shown itself in the best light to the foreign observers to resume negotiations with the European Union, is ready to carry out a democratic turnaround.
Sanctions, including non-distribution of visas for 40 leading Belarusian personalities and imprisonment of certain assets, could be waived if these elections were judged to be clean by the observers.
According to the opposition, they weren't.
`We were expelled by the Electoral Commissions and our observers were not allowed to participate in the recount,' regretted Yuri Potemkin, press secretary for the Union of Democratic Forces, a heterogeneous association of twelve parties.
This is a cosmetic change, insists Alexander Milinkievich, former opposition nominee for the 2006 presidential election and leader of the pro-Europe movement, because it is significant who counts the votes, and with us this is the executive.
If members of the opposition move into parliament, it's because they've been appointed, and they'ren't elected.
But their entry into parliament can mean airtime on television, meetings in the regions and legislative proposals.”
So the opposition is pleaded guilty to getting involved in the political game, because dialogue with the European Union must be opened.
Because if our economy, which is heavily in debt to Russia, gets into a crisis and we don't reform ourselves, we run the risk of becoming a protectorate of Russia.
Well, we want to be independent.'
The opposition fears that the Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe, which has pursued the ballot, does not recognize the election as valid.
She stresses that voter turnout numbers seem too high, and that the expansion of balloting in advance (26% of Minsk voters) has paved the way for any kind of manipulation.
“Did you notice that an election has taken place?” asks the leader of Social Democrat Alexander Kozulin, one of the last two political prisoners released in August this year.
There was no one in the polling stations.
And there will be a maximum of five members of the opposition in parliament, namely those who are most likely to meet the government’s ideas.”
The silence of the night descends again over the Oktoberplatz.
On televisation, President Lukachenko and the members of the central election commission congratulate on their success.
Conversation - From the blood that became safe again
Hema-Quebec celebrated the 10th anniversary of its founding yesterday, and its president, Dr. Francine Décary, had every reason to rejoice, because Hema-Quebec has become one of the safest and most efficient blood banks in the world.
And above all, she has regained public confidence, which had been severely shattered by the scandal of contaminated blood in the 1980s and 1990s.
It is known that national blood reserves managed by the blood donation service of the Canadian Red Cross were contaminated with the HI virus and the hepatitis C virus during the 1980s.
In light of this drama and public dissatisfaction, in February 1994, the Government of Canada launched a public inquiry led by Judge Horace Krever aimed to shed light on the circumstances that had led to such a tragedy and work out policies to prevent a re-disaster of this nature.
While Judge Krever was still reluctant to give up his report, at that time several provinces set up a committee whose goal was to develop a new system for the management of blood preserves in Canada.
In Quebec, the Gélineau committee fulfilled this mission.
According to Gélineau and Krever, two blood banks were established in Canada: Hema-Quebec, which is responsible for Quebec, and the Canadian blood society to supply the other provinces and the two territories.
`1998, when Hema-Quebec started her work, barely 38% of people had confidence in the blood preservation system in Quebec, Dr. Décary recalls.
The situation was daunting.
Through the enormous efforts to improve the safety of blood preserves and by working in this area during the following years, we have regained the trust of the public," she says, before proudly stressing that at the beginning of 2008, almost 94% of Quebec's population now had confidence in Hema-Quebec.
Dr. Décary, however, remains aware that 'trust is something extremely fragile.'
Hema-Quebec has been acting according to the principle of caution since its inception on 28 September 1998.
At that time we witnessed the occurrence of numerous cases of the human form of mad cow disease in Europe, a new type of Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, says this woman, who has been in charge since it was founded in 1998.
“And we didn’t have the slightest idea if this disease could be transmitted over the blood.
So we invoked one of the main guidelines of Krever and Gélineau's reports, according to which exclusion measures are applied if you don't know this and if a disease progresses only slowly, as is obviously the case with Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, which manifests itself through degeneration of the brain, which inevitably leads to death.
So in April 19999, no blood was accepted by people who had been in the United Kingdom for more than a month since 1980.
Well, several years ago it was proved without any doubt that this disease is transmitted via the blood.
In this way, this event has shown us that the principle of caution that we applied makes sense.
In 1999, a much more accurate technique for detecting HIV appeared on the market: the nucleic acid tests (NST), which make it possible to detect the occurrence of the virus itself directly in the blood of the donor, and not that of the antibodies against the virus, as was the case with the tests used so far.
The NST made it possible to shorten the incubation time that takes place between the time the person was infected and when the first viruses can be detected by our techniques in the blood, the specialist explains.
This time could be reduced for less than one week.
Thus, the risk that certain donors could be infected without knowing it, and one could not recognize this, was significantly reduced.
These improvements made it possible to significantly reduce the risk of transferring AIDS via a blood transfusion.
Nowadays, one in 12 million people are at risk of receiving blood infected with HIV.
“In other words, this means that you would have to distribute 12 million blood preserves before someone is infected with HIV.
Since Hema-Quebec distributes between 400,000 and 500,000 blood preserves every year, it would take more than 24 years before a case occurs in which HIV was transmitted via a blood preserve.
I believe that nowadays you can feel good when you get a blood transfusion or prescribe to restore your health," says Hema-Quebec's chief executive.
`Our challenge for the next ten years is to maintain the safety of our products.
You never know which pathogen can get into the system.
Just last year, the Chikungunya virus surfaced on the island of Réunion, which, just like the Westnil virus, is transmitted by insectors via blood.
We need to monitor uninterruptedly," Dr. Décary continues, before stressing that Hema-Quebec must also provide adequate supplies in addition to secure supply.
“Needs are increasing because the population is aging.
Since cancer-fighting treatments are more aggressive, patients often need new red blood cells or platelets.
The ageing of the population also has an impact on the blood donor network, which on average includes only 3% of Quebec's blood donating residents.
Every healthy person, who is 18 years of age or older and meets the eligibility criteria, can donate blood every 56 days, i.e. six times a year.
On average, 14% of the people who provide their blood are not accepted because, for example, they suffer from AIDS or malaria, and those who have traveled to a country where malaria is spread is excluded from donating blood for a year.
`With these 3%, we manage to obtain an inventory that meets the needs of hospitals.
Lack of supplies are very rare.
But you have to renew the members of this donor network as they get older and can't donate blood anymore.
It's hard to convince people to donate blood, but most importantly to keep them and get them to become regular donors.
Life is so hectic these days that one rarely finds the time to spend an hour donating blood," regrets Francine Décary.
With an annual budget of 297 million dollars, Hema-Quebec not only supplies blood preserves, but also human tissues, including bone grafts, heart valves, eyeballs and skin, which is used almost exclusively in case of serious burn injuries.
These tissues are examined, processed and frozen before being temporarily stored at Hema-Quebec, which also manages Quebec's only public umbilical cord blood bank.
In the umbilical cord, which was formerly regarded as biomedical waste, stem cells contain rich blood, which can serve to treat serious diseases, such as leukemia.
`A few weeks ago we celebrated the storage of our thousandth umbilical cord blood test, and we plan to lose more than a thousand annually.
“We should probably start making it available for transplants early next year,” explains Dr. Décary, who describes this umbilical cord loss program as very popular.
“Many mothers are convinced not to have access to it, because Hema-Quebec lacks the means to offer this complex and costly program in all Quebec hospitals.
Sociel - Impact in semi-final
Montreal's impact yesterday, thanks to a 3 to 1 victory (4 to 3 with all goals) against the Sounders from Seattle in the second and decide the quarter-final series game at Saputo Stadium, where the overall goal ratio counted, qualified for the semi-final round of the elimination games of the first USL league.
