`He've been fixating too much on it in the first half, Guy Carbonneau noted.
But I think he liked the end of the game.
He received storming applause.”
Charest bets on the north
The prime minister does not rule out to initiate elections before the end of the year.
As soon as the general assembly of the liberal party was over, Jean Charest went to Jean-Talon in Quebec yesterday, where the new health minister, Yves Bolduc (left), is trying to be elected in today's partial election.
«Pushing the course of our last major border in the north': This is the long-term goal that Jean Charest presented yesterday with visual means at the General Assembly of the liberal party of Quebec in Levis, following the guidelines entirely.
During his lecture, a kind of picture mill was projected behind him.
On three huge screens, spectacular images of hydropower plants, mines, viewed by Quebec in `Google Maps', maps, etc.
Big means like during the election season.
By the way, the prime minister refused to rule out that he will hold the elections this autumn, even if he announced that his schedule was very busy.
Plan Nord is the fourth element of a `vision' that the prime minister supposedly has for Quebec, namely that of a 'new economic area' that envisages an agreement with France regarding workers, an agreement between Canada and Europe and agreements with Ontario.
Although it has been frequently mentioned in the prime minister’s speeches in recent times, the opening of the north – the northern area on the 49th latitude – had never been the subject of a presentation.
However, the prime minister was limited to explaining why the North was 'a gem with enormous potential' but did not specify a date, investment sums or exact priorities for the upcoming projects.
At most, some outlook on what more could be worked on: above all on water energy, forestry, but especially on the mining sector, favoured by the growth of emerging economies and the renewal of public infrastructures.
According to Mr. Charest's enumeration, the North possesses 'nickel, zinc, copper, platinum, gold, silver, iron, titanium and even diamonds.'
This `area twice as large as France' `is close to us, is located in Quebec.
It's not only close to us, it's within us,' he emphasized, as if to clearly show that he's trying to realize a national founding dream.
Quebec has grown a lot through its connection to the north, he emphasized: the road to gold in Abitibi, Quebec's technique, the James Bay.
`It belongs to us, it's our future,' he added after taking a theatrical pause to look at the huge map behind him.
The north, 70% of Quebec's territory, includes part of Labrador that Quebec has always demanded, the prime minister told the press.
“This is a traditional stance that all governments pursued.
There is a borderline on which there has been no agreement for a very long time, if the opportunity presents itself, I will take this stance again on behalf of the government," he said.
In addition, “with climate change, we need to rethink our vision from the north,” he explained, talking about the Northwest Passage, which will be navigable all year round due to the ice melt.
Against this new geopolitical background, the Prime Minister thinks that it is almost a duty to occupy this area.
And the inhabitants of the territory, the indigenous peoples, and the Inuit?
The Prime Minister said that Quebec's government would deal very carefully with them, and that they would benefit from this project, especially the boys.
Two former ministers from various parties, Pierre Corbeil and Michel Létourneau (former pekist deputy of Ungava) have already launched deliberations on about thirty communities.
A ministerial committee headed by Benoît Pelletier, in which Julie Boulet (transport and mineral resources) occupies an important place, has begun the work.
The Prime Minister does not rule out a large assembly, `not necessarily a summit', but an opportunity on which `one will create a common vision'.
Moreover, the development of the North will take place in accordance with the principles of sustainable development.
the Plan North will be ‘discussed, regulated, and environmentally friendly’, he promised.
Jean Charest described this 'vision' as if it was a continuation of that of the liberal prime ministers who were in office before him.
“Like the liberals who were in office before us, we’ve rethought to turn the challenges into new growth and development chants.
By the way, this was one of the weekend's recurring themes, which opened on Friday with a large cocktail party to celebrate the 50th anniversary of Jean Lesage's takeover.
One of the adopted decisions envisages renaming Beauharnoi's power plant to Adélard-Godbout dam.
`By Godbout who created Hydro-Quebec, about Lesage, who achieved the silent revolution and nationalization of water electricity, to Bourassa, who made James Bay, our party always knew how to determine the future," Jean Charest explained.
He referred to the press to a next, this time Conservative project, namely to the program `Roads to the North′′ by John Diefenbaker, Prime Minister from 1957 to 1963.
With regard to the Eastmain1a-Ruper hydropower dam project, Mr Charest emphasizes that its commissioning is planned for 2012.
`This remains between us, but I intend to be part of it,' he confided to his party members, which provoked those applause storms.
The Prime Minister, who said in April that there would be no elections in 2008, and certainly in 2009, has indeed not yet ruled out the possibility of holding an election this autumn, which is possible for him by law.
He stresses, however, that his schedule is full, mainly because of a mission of the Community Council in China and a summit between Quebec and New York.
The general assembly of the weekend gave the liberal party leader a platform of nationalist plans, especially from 'the constitution-related goals', which were readily adopted: The PLQ particularly demands the recognition of the `particularity of Quebec" in the constitution.
It would also like Quebec to appoint members of the Council of Canadian Broadcasting and Telecommunications, and to veto the decisions made in Quebec.
On the part of the opposition, the guidelines of the Plan North were received with skepticism.
The head of Parti Québécois, Pauline Marois, has re-emerged the policy on the development of the Nord-du-Quebec, which the Landry government introduced in 2001.
According to her, when she came to power in 2032, the liberal government has refrained from applying it.
If she'd done it, `would we'd be much further along today', Ms Marois judged alongside her nominee in Jean-Talon (where the election takes place today) Françoise Mercure.
ADQ has advocated the government's endorsement of the government's adoption of a North plan.
"But what we want is that this will not only be a great notion," declared party leader Sébastien Proulx on behalf of Mario Dumont, who comes back from vacation today.
Letters - A dishonored country
Mr Vic Toews, ex-'Justice' minister (what irony) in the Harper cabinet it was who last summer allowed himself to say about Louise Arbour that she was a 'shame' for Canada.
This woman, who has waged a never-ending fight for justice and deserves all our admiration to cover with disgrace, says just as much about the people around the prime minister as about Stephen Parker himself.
We are dealing here with a government that is the only one from all the affected countries that has not brought home its compatrion captured in the Guantanamo concentration camp.
A government that doesn't even take into account that Omar Khadr was still a younger when the things he're accused of happening happened.
The only government that, despite the fact that Canada has signed the Geneva Convention, breaks its word and allows a child soldier to vegetate under inhumane conditions.
The only one degrading itself to a parody of justice in order to assemble the defendant's guilt.
Let's recall that UNICEF in particular, Amnesty International and the Association of Canadian lawyers plead for Omar Khadr to breather home so he gets a fair trial in Canada.
It is precisely this government that closes its eyes to torture: it is of little interest that a young adult, after so many years of torment, will surely be mentally broken forever.
We are not yet aware of this, but here we are experiencing an insidious circumcision of our rights, our freedom and our `democracy', this word that Harper constantly carries like a flag in front of it, and which sounds so meaningless out of his mouth.
So the time has come when there is no morality anymore: one may arbitrarily abuse, torture and imprison a Canadian citizen, according to the discretion of a head of government, who to this day is only supported by the minority (may he completely belong to the minority after 14 October...).
Many voters believe to find their salvation in a party that preaches virtue, law and order. But they don’t see that this government has broken away from what constituted our country’s reputation and dignity.
The shame, Mr. Toews, lies in this renunciation, and nowhere else.
Free Opinion - Barrick Gold's Orwell speech
In its September 17 issue, Le Devoir published a letter from Mr. Patrick J. Garver, deputy director-general of Canada's Barrick Gold.
This confirms, pursuing an Orwellian logic that the world’s largest gold recovery company sought a smear lawsuit over six million against three authors and a small destitute publishing house, with the aim of opening a ‘public transparent debate’.
If it is true that the judges are independent and impartial, then they are not judging about the truth, especially not in the case of libeloury lawsuits, but about the law.
As expected, this fundamental difference has been completely concealed by Mr Garver.
For Barrick Gold, the best way to have a public debate is to ruin anyone who dares to represent one of their opposite opinions.
No matter how the trial turns out, the writers and the editors of Noir Canada will have to spend considerable sums in order to prepare their defenses and fight with very unequal weapons against an army of lawyers paid out of the almost inexhaustible pockets of the gold mine.
The tactic of these unequal battles, by the way, is often to ruin the defenders in order to force a consensual settlement that silences the opposite side and also deters anyone who dares to deal with the question again.
If Barrick Gold really wanted a public debate, she would have had to present her view of things in front of public opinion.
In contrast, in many of the countries where Barrick Gold works, debates in Canada are usually fought through the exchange of ideas rather than by stick punches.
It would therefore have sufficenced if the gold mine had disclosed its books and files and opened a real debate, through the newspapers, the media, publications and the financing of independent audit teams, which could have shed light on this issue.
But Barrick Gold doesn't seem to have confidence in public opinion and wipes aside the petitions and letters in support of Noir Canada's authors with a hand movement.
Perhaps Mr Garver could explain how he can ask for a 'public' debate at the same time and yet disregard the opinions of this very public?
Apparently, in the world of Vice-Vice-presidential, this word encompasses only the generously paid lawyers, and not the community of all Canadians; a quite cramped point of view.
In a democracy, slanderers have the right to speak.
What Barrick Gold doesn't understand - or doesn't want to understand - is that the public that supports the authors and editors of Noir Canada does so primarily because it rejects the gold mine's brutal stance.
This public is willing to listen and judge Barrick, but on the basis of a civilized exchange of arguments, and not in a framework that many perceive as a gag lawsuit (in the course of the `public debate', Barrick Gold has just addressed a demand to the authors of Noir Canada, in which it requires them to no longer use this term...).
Aside from Mr Garver's surreal speech, Barrick Gold's lawsuit is a direct attack on freedom from academic research and truth-finding, which is fundamental to any democratic society.
It completely denies the right to cite believable sources and to discuss facts and interpretations that form the basis of intellectual work.
If it is impossible in a country like Canada to explore and discuss issues that don’t please the rich companies in the world, then who else can do that?
If Barrick Gold really wanted a public and transparent debate, it could run it by following the scientific norms used by the authors of Noir Canada.
She's got a right to respond.
The university environment to which we belong has been known for a long time to get a grip on debates and discrepancies and to resolve conflicts.
Barrick Gold's disproportionate lawsuit clearly shows that, contrary to what she pretends, she has no desire for transparent debate.
This is a loss for the university world, as well as for public debate, and it's time for governments to take action restricting this kind of unjustified lawsuits.
Bailout for Wall Street - Democrats and Republicans get along
The Bush administration and senior congressmen, Republicans and Democrats alike, finally came to an agreement yesterday on the most extensive economic bailout plan since the Great Depression of the 1930s.
The new version of the plan over US$700 billion, which is supposed to stop the financial crisis, which has been rocking Wall Street for months, has yet to be adopted by the two chambers of Congress.
The main contents are: a better scrutiny of MPs over the practical application of the plan, new mechanisms to secure the repayment of the public funds used, new dispositions to curb the number of homes garnered due to arrears, as well as the right to monitor the salaries paided to the officers of the assisted companies.
U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, the president of Nancy Pelosi, and the representative of the Republicans present at the negotiations, Senator Judd Gregg, announced yesterday, a few minutes after midnight, the good news, after a two-day negotiating marathon.
“We’ve made great strife towards an agreement that will be market-effective,” Bush administration representative Henry Paulson said in a statement.
“It all happened to protect the real economy and the American people front of the price crisis on Wall Street,” the Democrat said.
“This is the fundamental point: if we don’t do that, the trauma, chaos, and unrest in the daily lives of Americans will be enormous,” Republican Jud Gregg said.
Negotiations had accelerated during the last two hours, for fear of the effect that a renewed failure on the mood of investors would have.
The deadline that had been agreed was the market opening this morning.
The two American presidential candidates, who only took part in the negotiations late, were more likely to be proponents of the settlement, but without failing to voice their dissatisfacity that they would have to be encountered in this extreme way.
Democratic Senator Barrack Obama insisted on the addition of certain injunctions to the agreement, especially those designed to allow the owners to keep their homes, and those affecting the salaries of senior executives of state-bailed corporations.
`If taxpayers are asked to take extraordinary measures due to the irresponsibility of certain people, it is nothing to congratulate themselves on.
But these measures are necessary," he explained on American television stations.
When he was asked on another station if he'll vote for the proposed plan, his Republican opponent John McCain replied: `I'd like to know the specifics, but I hope so.
“The guiding principles I’ve read [show] that this is a thing that we will all have to digest, and with which we will be able to move forward.
The option to do nothing at all is simply not acceptable," he said.
The 700 billion dollar plan is geared to this, with taxpayers' money
He arrives 14 months after a crisis triggered by the bursting of a housing bubble that initially led to the collapse of high-risk mortgage loans (subprime) before expanding into further forms of more common bonds (PCAA), and eventually resulting in a freezing of financial strength and the ruin of banks and financial institutions.
It was a group of Republican lawmakers that failed last week's first attempt to settle.
Since the Democrats have accepted that certain `toxic' assets of the societies should only be secured by the government in distress and should not be bought, it is believed to escape the impasse.
In order to complete the angles for everyone in Congress, one has also added an injunction, which states that if the government should make losses within five years with this program, it could then collect new taxes in order to recover its invested capital.
The new plan envisages the payment of an initial sum of 250 billion, to which a further 100 billion can be added if the president sees it necessary.
The payment of another 350 billion must be passed by Congress in a renewed vote.
In return for his assistance, Washington will receive shares in the backed-up companies that earn him dividends in the event of profits, and which it can sell again when the storm has passed.
The Federal Government will have the option of halting the attachment procedures relating to the dwellings linked to the real estate loans affected by this plan, and also setting deadlines for repayment and severances paid to the officers of the companies concerned.
The developers of the plan would like the Chamber of Deputies to vote on him as early as today, and then the Senate on Wednesday.
Despite all their efforts, it is not yet certain that their plan will get the green light.
In fact, more votes were raised yesterday against this project, both on the side of Republicans and Democrats.
"Quite obviously Washington doesn't understand us," Republican Scott Garett, a member of the chamber's finance commission, explained to his constituents.
`Yeah, we've got a big financial problem.
Yes, there are solutions.
But not the ones that are asking taxpayers to cash in'.
Democrat Brad Sherman, leader of a group in Congress called 'the caucus of skeptics,' presents things differently, but comes to the same conclusion.
“Four-hundred excellent economics professors, including three Nobel laureates, have wrotten us to let us know: “We’re asking Congress not to be put under pressure, and to carefully consider which path to take,” he said yesterday.
At the same time, in Europe, we were once again proving that this was a global crisis.
At an emergency meeting, the three Benelux countries announced the partial nationalization of the Fortis banking group for 11.2 billion euros (16.3 billion US dollars).
The intervention of the governments of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg became necessary after French bank BNP Paribas announced that it would refrain from buying up its competitor, which is in big trouble since it lost 2 billion euros (2.9 billion U.S. dollars) in the crisis caused by high-risk real estate loans in the United States.
Fortis employs 85,000 people and is one of the twenty largest European banks and the ten largest insurers.
"That's not lost money, Dutch Finance Minister Wouter Bos said yesterday.
To compensate, we get a right to vote and influence in the bank.
This is exactly what savers and households value now that uncertain times arise.”
During this time, on the other side of the English Channel, the British government was just about to nationalize another bank distressed by the financial crisis.
According to the English press, the government would take over all ongoing loans to the bank Bradford and Bringley, of which over 41 billion (US$75.5 billion) from real estate loans.
Spanish bank Santander announced yesterday that it would buy up the British bank's branches and customer accounts.
Bradford and Bringley would be the second bank to nationalize the British government since the start of this financial crisis, according to Northern Rock.
Paul Newman (19252008) - Death of a committed icon
His beauty with the azure eyes was his trump card for a long time, no one would say his weakness.
Did the American actor free himself from the narcissistic mirror with the charming smile?
Yes, and no.
His sensitivity, sensitivity and an obvious talent probably also kept him away from a position of power that gives birth to the truly terrible monsters.
But what a big endurance runner!
Paul Newman, who died on Friday at the age of 83 from the aftermath of lung cancer at his ranch in Connecticut, remains one of the last icons of the American cinema of the golden years.
He was less monumental than Brando, to whom he resembled and for whom he was for a long time a kind of rival on the screen, more careful in his acting, certainly also more fragile, but also an apostle of freedom and blasting dramaturgical conventions.
Mixed blood often yields wonderful children.
He was born in 1925 in Ohio as the son of a German Jew and a Hungarian-born mother.
One of the glorious faces of the American dream was to emerge from its European roots.
Without excessive ego, extreme quality feature.
Because it reflected an entire country that fell in love with the self-made-man, the fighter who was injured in battle in the Pacific War, who studied acting in Yale in the early 1950s and then visited Actor's Studio in New York before he had success at the theater, on television and soon in the cinema.
Just this man, who fought for civil rights, was also the driver, who finished second at the 24-hour race of Mans in 1979, and also a sex symbol, even though he had been living in a flagship relationship together with the actress Joanne Woodward since 1958.
With it, the unknown facets of this mysterious, restrained, committed and hyper-sensitive to the glamour of show business also disappear, the mystery of which one will never solve completely.
It's a decent man in the purest sense.
Paul Newman's death tumbled cinema lovers this weekend, while all his great roles were played on the dark screen of memories.
More than half a century of American film art in constant change passes with him.
That's when the charming cowboy from "Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid" (1969) recalls again, and the young man who rejects his sensuous wife (Elizabeth Taylor) into The Cat on the hot tin roof, which was filmed by Tennessee Williams in 1958.
There is evoked a much shorter past remembrance (1990) that is disturbed to its sensitive Mr. Bridge, who is disturbed at his old age by a wedding that didn't fulfill all its promises.
There have been so many other Paul Newmans throughout history.
This criminal, stormy, violent and unforgettable boxer Rocky Graziano from Somebody Up There Likes Me (A Hand Full Dirt) by Robert Wise, a role meant for James Dean, resumed after his death, and which Newman made real knowledge in 1956.
This sympathetic crook in the towering The Sting (Der Clou), which was awarded the Oskar for best film in 1973.
As an embittered and vengeful billoard player in The Hustler by Robert Rossen (1961),
This face through the times, that was him.
Still him.
Epochs unite in his CV.
The youth knows him mainly from the cult film Slap Shot by George Roy Hill (1977), as Reggie Dunlop, the less orthodox coach of a hockey team.
His fans found him again in The Hudsucker Proxy of the brothers Coen in 1994.
With a final role in 2003 as godfather of the Irish mafia in Road to Perdition by Sam Mendes, the circle closed.
In contrast to several cinema legends who withdrew from the film world early enough, Newman should stay in the saddle for a long time, betraying Hollywood, his fortune, his pomp and his concessions.
French filmmaker Bertrand Tavernier saw him, not without humour, as a living challenge to `underplaying' when he judged his overly expressive performances.
The profession was not always easy for the beautiful Paul Newman, to whom the roles flew for a long time.
However, he was less likely to play Sunnyboys as outsiders: neurotics, alcoholics, nasty or desperates, with whose perfect portrayal he reflected his slidinging and revolting.
As a director, Newman revealed unknown pages of his true sensitive sensibility.
His 1968 Rachel, Rachel (according to the novel of Canadian Margaret Laurence) with his wife Joanne Woodward in the leading role, drew a subtle female portrait full of melancholy.
He, who lost his son Scott through an overdose, dedicated his 1984 film Harry and Son, a work full of sensitivity and sadness.
At the buoyancy of honors sprooted over Newman yesterday, his closer friend and former gunmate Robert Redford (they had played together in The Sting and in Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid) took great action: `His present has improved my life and this land.'
Generosity and joie de vivre were essential qualities of the late actor.
Apart from his legendary career, a great advocate of civil rights, environmental protection and independent cinema, an opponent of drift-off Hollywood, is now also going there.
We've arguably often seen him stand by foot in bowls full of vinaigrette, in sauces, pastries or other food, and considered this to be quite distasteful, even though we found it funny at first.
Nevertheless, the profits from his revenues flowed into his foundation for children in need.
Instead of resting on his myth, Newman got involved up to the shot.
He left his five daughters a letter with these sentences: `Always and to the very end your father was incredibly grateful for his good star.
It was an honor to be here.”
And for us it was an honor to follow the life of such an intelligent and generous American actor.
An exemplary citizen, in the closest and broadest sense.
What was produced in the invoice factory of Zuschlag &amp; Co.?
Negotiations in the criminal case against J�áno's surcharge and his accomplices will begin on 8 October.
The defendant is, in particular, accused of involvement in organised crime, as well as grossly negligent damage and business fraud, and other criminal acts, including forgery of documents.
According to the public prosecutor's office, the funds lucrated from the tenders were used for their own purposes, while the services rendered were often subject to fake invoices.
State funds and grants from the self-government - a total of HUF 65 million - are said to have partly flowed into the coffers of the Fiatal Baloldal organization, partly into the private coffers of individuals.
As the investigative authorities announced, fictitious invoices were presented when receiving the tender funds, and the clubs are also said not to have fulfilled their duties.
According to the indictment, much of the bills are said to have been forged.
The invoice blocks were bought on the market of Józsefváros, the round seals were made in Kiskunhalas.
The decision on which companies the invoices are to be issued was decided on that round trip north of Lake Balaton to which István Lados of János was sent.
In the course of this "expression tour", Lados was supposed to locate guesthouses and restaurants.
He demanded bills on consumption in order to be able to issue bills based on these templates for the Youth and Sports Ministry, the Ministry of Social Affairs and Labour, as well as the self-government of Budapest.
These invoices are said to have been manipulated by István Lados and his accomplice, András Őri, the latter according to our informant being responsible for the constitution of the relevant reports.
One of Lados' "exploration tour" was a beer restaurant.
As two invoices issued by Zuschlag and Co in 2003 can be seen, the gastronomy company is said to have hosted the ninety-strong group of the association Biztos Pont Egyesület.
Now, however, the boss of the company states that such a large group would not suddenly fit into the restaurant.
Another invoice, issued in the summer of 2002 to the Jobb Jövőért Foundation, later known as Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért, also showed serious errors in form and content.
In a pension in Badacsonytomaj, which has only three rooms and cannot accommodate more than ten people at the same time, 130 guests are said to have stayed.
There were apparently no limits to the imagination of the counterfeiters when it came to Pigal Kft., a catering establishment, in whose accounting documents of the customers Jobb Jövőért and Hoppá according to the indictment do not even appear.
According to Győző Farkas of hvg.hu, it was immediately clear to him that they were forged documents when documents were submitted to him by the public prosecutor’s office during questioning.
"Our company name and the tax number were incorrectly attached to the stamp."
And further: Apart from formal errors, the fact that the company is said to have accommodated one hundred people after having a total of eleven rooms was also wrong, which is impossible to accommodate so many people.
Food was also mentioned on the invoice, but the restaurant has not been in operation for a long time.
The busy counterfeiters have also failed to verify that the Budapest travel agency, which they targeted, offers only travel abroad, and so they have indicated Hungary trips on the cash bill, which also contradicted the company’s accounting practice.
As announced in the course of the investigations, they have also cited VAT on the invoices, even though travel agency services related to a country outside the EU are VAT-free.
The total amount of VAT was incorrect on four invoices issued by the Hotel Révfülöp Kft. to the Jobb Jövőért Foundation, as the company charges only 15 percent VAT instead of 25, in addition, the employees issue only machine invoices, but the forged documents were handwritten.
Another mistake that the "invoicers" underperformed was that in one case they had cited "goods transport" as a service rendered, although this is not offered by the company specializing in investment advice, the prosecutor's office said.
Other formal errors - incorrect invoice format, false stamps - was due to the fact that invoices issued to Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért Alapítvány, as well as to XXI. Századi Kulturális Egyesület and Biztos Pont could be exposed as counterfeiting.
Likewise, in the case of Ravenna Bt. and Kunfehértó, whose managing directors claimed to have never issued the invoices.
Another "error" has been subdued to the counterfeiters at HIB Kft. from Nyíregyháza: this company was in dissolution at the time (2001), when the group of Fiatal Városért Egyesület is said to have eaten there - the mass administrator consequently stated that he had not issued the invoices himself.
Deliberately counterfeited
The other group of account managers are persons who were well aware that they would have to make fictitious invoices.
At the request of Fiatal Baloldal - Ifjú Szocialisták, Esély Budapestért Alapítvány has issued invoices for Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért Foundation, amounting to approximately HUF 4 million.
According to the indictment, the managing director of Collab Kft. was persuaded by one of the defendants, Ludovic Györfi, to issue the fictitious invoices.
Biogate Kft., which is currently also in liquidation, is said to have also cooperated with surcharge and Co.: In 2003, the GmbH is said to have supplied the Jobb Jövőért Alapítvány Foundation, as well as Atlasz Egyesület and Vörösfenyő Egyesület with counterfeited invoices with a total value of HUF 5 million, which referred to services not provided.
However, there was also no payment transactions, since the invoices were cancelled immediately after their issuance, but what the persons concerned have forgotten to note in their documents.
The third group of accountants shall include persons directly associated with the defendant.
One of them is Iván Kubatov, the postmen of the Ukrainian managing director of Delta Trió Kft., a Budapest-based fuel and trading company that cannot be found there.
This company is said to have issued several invoices for the sale of computer components with a total value of HUF about 18 million between October and December 2005.
According to the investigative authority, Cash Investment Kft. from Kiskunhalas, which has been in liquidation since 14 September 2005, is said to have acted as buyers of these invoices.
The managing director of this company was a Romanian, to whom the company documents were also to be delivered by Iván Kubatov, between 2002 and 2005.
According to our information, Kubatov confessed during the investigation to have issued invoices on behalf of the GmbH to the associations Atlasz and Hoppá, as well as to Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért Alapítvány and to have conceded their equivalent themselves.
Fa-Bani Jos Faipari Kft. and Jos-Investment Bt. are said to have been founded by Romanian masovers at Iván Kubatov's express request.
In truth, however, none of these companies were active, which according to the defendant was due to a lack of orders.
Nevertheless, he was able to benefit from the companies: at the request of his accomplice, the lawyer Zsolt Szente, who was commissioned with the preparation of the company foundation documents, he issued invoices on behalf of the companies to the associations associated with a surcharge, such as Atlasz and Y-Generáció.
In total, Kubatov is said to have issued 15 invoices with a total value of HUF 14.2 million on behalf of Cash Investment, Delta Trió, Fa-Bani Jos, Jos Investment and Linaxtrade to the organizations.
Prosecutors believe Kubatov may have collected 10% of the invoice amount as well as VAT.
Kubatov is said to have even abused Szi-Beri Bt. for issuing fictitious invoices - in this case, the invoices have been enclosed with the applications from Biztos Pont.
Y-Generáció and Szövetség a Modern Magyarországért submitted their applications in January 2006 to the non-profit organization of the Socialist Party, Táncsics Mihály Alapítvány ("TMA").
Both received 500,000 forints each, but have used these amounts outside of purpose.
To cover this up, they have submitted five fictitious invoices from Works Team Kft. to the TMA.
Incidentally, these invoices were secured during the search of Árpád Kemecsei's office in the Ministry of Education.
In the negotiations, some defendants are now expected to make a confession about the procedure of forgery, as they were already filed before the public prosecutor's office.
A sensation could be if the main defendant Jáno's surcharge decides to make a comprehensive confession.
His information so far has been less detailed, and although he had already pledged several times to report comprehensively about everything, his confession is still pending.
So the question remains whether this is in his interest, whether the Socialists have concluded a pact with him regarding the reparation of the majority of the damage caused by HUF 50 million, and whether a surcharge is willing to take responsibility in return.
However, it is quite possible that the "insulted person" will now play after his requests for mitigation of imprisonment and house arrest fell on deaf ears.
While other criminals, who have caused far greater damage with economic offences and have not remunerated them, are on the loose, or at least under house arrest, surcharge is denied this possibility.
The psychology of outbidding - or why we're willing to pay more
In the case of auctions, the buyers tend to spend more and thus overestimate the party.
Neurologists and economic researchers at the University of New York have now succeeded in discovering the neurological-economic foundations of this well-known and seemingly irrational behavior.
According to a study published in the "Science" magazine, people do not want to admit defeat in a competition, such as an auction on eBay, and are therefore willing to pay even excessive prices.
The researchers tried to explore the neurological scheme of this "game" with experimental auctions.
A group of volunteers were to play lottery, while the other took part in a playful action - in both cases, the active brain regions of the subjects were examined by means of functional magnetic resonance (fMRI).
In both games, the participants were able to win money, but an increased activity of the striatum, a complex below the cortex, was measured in losers of the auction, in which the outbidding of the opponent plays an essential role.
Among many other functions, this brain region is responsible for "self-reward" of the nervous system.
Therefore, the researchers said that the price increase could be explained by avoiding an overreaction of the striatum.
In order to substantiate their thesis, the economists in the research group have made additional investigations to track the money.
In this case, three groups should compete against a partner under different conditions.
The control group was informed of the true value of the product, after which they made their stakes.
In addition, another group was promised 15 dollar rewards in the event that they emerge as winners from the game.
The third group received 15 dollars before the auction, which was also lost in the event of a lost game.
Thus, the difference between the two groups consisted only in the fact that the emphasis at one was on winning, while in the case of the other was on losing.
As proof of the hypothesis expressed by neurologists, however, the members of the "against losing" group were ready for higher stakes than those in the control or "profit" group.
"These results cannot be derived from any earlier economic theory.
Until now, these have only justified the behaviors observed at auctions with the joy of winning or risk avoidance.
The contradiction between these two theories has been resolved thanks to the imaging neurological procedures, so that we now have a third explanation ready," said Andrew Schotter, professor of the Faculty of Economics at the University of New York.
Hepatitis: When is the doctor to visit?
Around half a billion people worldwide suffer from chronic hepatitis B or C, and in Hungary this chronic liver disease is listed among the leading causes of death.
The number of infected people in this country is estimated at hundreds of thousands, but only seven thousand are treated, which is because the patients often do not know that they are infected.
Hepatitis is the collective term for infectious liver diseases triggered by various viruses.
The symptoms are often similar - liver inflammation and jaundice are common, but the pathogens and the diseases differ from each other.
Depending on the pathogen, hepatitis viruses spread in two different ways: hepatitis A and E through the digestive tract (e.g. from contaminated foods or water) in hepatitis B, C and D, the viruses can be transmitted through blood and other bodily fluids (such as sperm, vaginal secretions or saliva).
Travellers are recommended a vaccination against the viruses A and B in any case, there is no vaccine against virus C.
The symptoms of hepatitis A show up about two-three weeks after consumption of the contaminated food and are generally accompanied by discomfort, weakness, abdominal pain and nausea.
Thereafter, jaundice occurs on the skin and in the eyes, the stool becomes light, the urine dark, and the recovery can last for up to two-three weeks.
Globally, about 1 million people die each year from hepatitis, far more serious diseases such as liver cancer and cirrhosis.
The main reason for this is that it is estimated that only one in ten infected people is in drug treatment.
Hepatitis C can also have serious consequences: untreated, it can lead to cirrhosis and liver cancer.
Most infected people don't even know about their disease because the pathogen insidiously destroys the liver cells - in many cases, the first symptoms become apparent after 20-30 years.
Timely prevention, such as regular check-ups, is therefore essential.
There are three groups in which this disease is more common: those that underwent blood transfusion before 1991, drug addicts, and tattooees.
Currently, the number of chronic hepatitis C sufferers worldwide is estimated at 200 million.
Since the disease is already detectable in a routine laboratory examination, it is advisable to consult the family doctor at the first signs of unknown origin.
Detected in time, the disease is curable, the virus can be eradicated, causing the risk of infection to be banned and prevented from the development of cancer.
September 30 is World Hepatitis Day, held for the first time in 2004 by patient representatives worldwide.
The aim of the action is to strengthen awareness of this chronic liver inflammation caused by viruses, their development and the importance of early detection.
Are believers less in pain?
As recent research shows that religious faith acts as a kind of "paining drug" that is said to have facilitated the deaths of Catholic martyrs.
Scientists from Oxford University have conducted an interesting experiment: it was investigated to what extent the belief in God affects man's sense of pain, reported the British paper "Telegraph".
In the course of the rather bizarre investigation, 12 believers and atheists received electric shock each while studying a painting of the Mother of God.
As the results showed, believers were able to eliminate most of the pain.
By means of brain-scan technology, it could even be proven that Catholics were able to activate the part of their brain that is responsible for pain regulation.
The study was the first in a research project in which, in addition to scientists, philosophers and theologians from various departments of the university participate.
During the experiment, the subjects were tethered with a sparkling device to the left hand, which delivered the electric shocks.
Participants were asked to focus heavily on two paintings: on the one hand a picture of the Mother of God, painted by Sassoferrato in the 17th century, and on the other hand Leonardo da Vinci's "Lady with the Hermelin."
The researchers hoped that the sight of the Virgin Mary among the faithful would evoke a kind of religious trance while selecting da Vinci's image because it hardly differed from the other painting and was perceived as reassuring.
The subjects each spent half an hour in an MRI scanner, and during this time received a total of 20 electric shocks in four stages, while they were to look at either the religious or the other painting.
The Catholics reported that they had felt "in safety" at the sight of the Mother of God, as if they were "cared for," and that they had felt "peace and quiet."
During viewing the religious painting, they have reported over 12% fewer in pain than if they had Leonardo's work in mind.
Psychologist Miguel Farias, who also took part in the experiment, said that similar effects could also evoke with other, strong-acting images in the atheists, one would only have to find the corresponding photo.
"We would have to find a picture that triggers very positive sensations, such as a mother or a father," the psychologist said.
Anglican Bishop Tom Wright welcomed the results of the research.
"Practicing faith should change our personality, and in many cases it does," Wright said.
According to these excellent results, there is only the question of what effect a Madonna picture of Leonardo or another comparable painter would have on the pain regulation of the believers.
Would the high-quality Virgin Mary achieve even better results?
You are what you say about yourself
On the occasion of her 10th birthday, Google can rightly claim: it can be found in people’s pockets as well as above their heads in the sky: she knows where people have spent their holidays, to which specialists they go, and what moves them.
And that's still not enough, according to Eric Schmidt, one of Google's chief executives.
In the direction skeptics, who are plagued by Orwell's horror visions, he points out: These would just overlook the essence of Web 2.0.
In an interview with the Financial Times, Schmidt recently commented on Google’s maining objective is to collect even more personal data about users, bringing it closer to the company’s original goal, according to which it wants to “order all information from this world.”
Schmidt explained that Google, after ten years of world-changing work, is still only at the beginning of collecting information.
Asked about the status quo in five years, Schmidt said: he wished Google was able to answer questions such as "What should I do tomorrow?" or "Which job really suits me?", but software programs weren't able to do that yet, because they didn't know enough about the users.
Not all Internet users are aware that in the course of their Internet searches the search terms would be registered, as well as the IP addresses from which they were searched, and the visited pages.
For example, the "Personalized Search" introduced two years ago allows the visitor to save his own search history and use it later, so that the hits can be generated much more precisely later, and on the same logic are also based on the so-called "recommendations", with which the system recommends tailor-made products and services due to the earlier preferences.
However, the users are wondering what happens in the case of a data misuse - "When you look at what I do" - some are worried about the Google data monopoly.
In most cases, however, it is more the potential power represented by the company, which is terrifying, and less the feeling of guilt or the fear of punishment, the pursuit of freedom or revolting against the power, which encounters rejection among many Internet users.
However, it is a proven fact that the search terms are not only stored, but also passed on to third parties in the event, and in these legal cases there is no protection for the personal rights of the Internet user.
A prime example of this has recently been the crackdown on paedophilia in Brazil, in the course of which Google handed over information to the Brazilian government, which was collected by the joint site called "Orkut" and also enabled identification of paedophilies.
However, users concerned about personal rights were able to breathe a sigh of relief in May, when Google, together with the civil rights organization EFF, successfully went to court against the FBI: Google cannot be forced in the future with no point in the constitution to give out information to the FBI upon secret request.
(However, the federal government has successfully obtained the publication of 50,000 web addresses and 1,000 search terms with reference to the youth protection law passed years ago, but was retroactively deprived of the list of individual users, or their web and search term list for two months).
Google's image therefore remains controversial: those who are reluctant to disclose their personal data, because they fear the loss of their privacy and privacy through the use of Google services, scourge the company with the controversial outrage.
Meanwhile, the American "geek culture", which is present in many countries and unites enthusiastic computer fans, activists and users, sees the next generation of "cool entrepreneurs" in them, in contrast to Microsoft, which is the market leader thanks to Windows, even if both fight for the same goal.
One possible reason for this is that Google, unlike Microsoft, does not demand identification codes to its users, and provides its services free of charge, so that an open, constantly updated source code and program package seems to be far more attractive, youthful and cooler than the official practices of the software giant.
On the other hand, the geeks see through the problematic relationship of personal rights and the Internet - a relationship that has come into focus, especially in recent years thanks to the term "Web 2.0".
"Web 2.0" is about nothing more than collaboration, i.e. users deliberately leave traces and use the available communication channels, and even if the dimensions are individually different, the main thing remains the same, namely to show themselves publicly.
An average adolescent can manage his profile simultaneously on several community portals - from Facebook to Xanga, who can connect them through other services, such as Twitter, or music services such as last.fm, and can eventually integrate all this into a tracking system such as FriendFeed or Blip.fm. In addition, they can run blogs that automatically represent the latest photo galleries that are tested on Flickr or Picasa.
And a number of other exemplary cases prove: When looking at the party photos of last night, the girlfriend soon finds out with whom her unfaithful friend has pleasured, or the employee is dismissed, because in his blog he is all too honestly airing his anger about corporate management - one thing is certain: The term "privacy" has now been completely reinterpreted.
Anyone who leads an online life is what they reveal of themselves - such as the basic idea behind the behavior of today's youth could be formulated.
And those who try to keep something a secret, or even circumventing the Google monopoly, are happy to do so - such as Scroogle, a tool that accesses Google's search engine, but deliberately leaves no traces.
Quite a few people deliberately do not register on community pages so that they are not findable, but also Google’s online applications, such as word processing and graphic tools and RSS feeds, can be handled with alternative versions that aren’t tied to huge server parks and therefore don’t generate the best possible profile from the user.
Public opinion, meanwhile, is waiting spellbound until Google shows its true face.
With its current value of USD 156 billion, the company is even ahead of IBM (USD 140 billion), Intel (USD 124 billion) or Coca-Cola (USD 110 billion), the advertising space of AdWords/AdSense threatens the revenues of the advertising agencies, and the ever-introduced services not only Microsoft, but also other software developers are causing headaches.
Critical viewers, however, also find the disturbing counterpoles: For example, the group management has completely rejected a protest of the employees, after the fees of the company's own kindergarten were doubled, in addition, the management said that the multiple meals of the employees were too costly. However, all this seems almost mild against the fact that Google in 2006 before the Chinese government even went to the knees and agreed with an agreement
The internal regulation of the group is that no new service will be advertised in advance, but only when this is already available. Nevertheless, the events of the coming months and years could turn around a lot again vigorously: While some believe that Google’s book and magazine scanning or archiving project now assumes that the company would soon have to deal with the Supreme Council, but others are rather concerned about the “health” system
And the fact that Google builds a genetic database already provides the substance to the next generation of sci-fi writers - such as "Scroogled", the famous novel by Cory Doctorow describes a future in the best Orwellian manner, in which a person becomes frighteningly approachable thanks to data obtained by Google.
Two teenagers help Ózd's double killer to clear traces
In connection with Ózd's double murder, the police have now interrogated two teenagers who are suspected of having assisted in clearing the traces, the spokesman for the Borsod Police Commission said on Tuesday.
The youths have confessed, and they are currently accused of aid.
According to information from the MTI news agency, these are two girls defending themselves on the loose.
Aid is threatened with imprisonment of up to five years.
The suspect in Ózd's double murder, a 23-year-old local man, was taken into custody on Sunday.
The man is under urgent suspicion of deliberately murdering several people out of greed.
In the course of the investigation, it has emerged that the 23-year-old had owed a larger amount of money to a 53-year-old.
However, the suspect did not want to settle his debts, so he lured his creditor to grounds in Ózd last Wednesday, where he murdered him and his nine-year-old daughter.
According to the MTI, the man has planned the deed by a long hand - this is also proven by the fact that he committed the murders with a weapon hidden at the scene.
Together with experts, the police are now investigating the murder weapon and the circumstances of its use.
Warsaw Stock Exchange puts purchase offer for Prague Stock Exchange
Around 200 million euros, or 5 billion Czech crowns, have been offered by the Warsaw Stock Exchange for the one hundred percent purchase of the share packages of the Prague Stock Exchange.
Ludwik Sobolewski, president of the Warsaw Stock Exchange, reaffirmed the offend that the Poles had placed despite the fact that they were formerly excluded by the Czechs as possible owners of their stock exchange.
The reason for this is that the Warsaw Stock Exchange belongs entirely to the state.
99% of the shares are in the hands of the Polish state, which, however, intends to sell more than 60% of the securities in the coming months.
According to reports of the Czech daily newspaper "E15", the offer of the Poles contains the highest purchase purchase price to date, which has ever been offered for the Prague Stock Exchange.
As the newspaper reports, the Vienna Stock Exchange, the Nasdaq Transatlantic, the New York Stock Exchange, the London Stock Exchange and Deutsche Börse have so far made offers.
Petr Koblic, head of the Prague Stock Exchange, said last week that of all those interested the Warsaw Stock Exchange would arguably be the worst new owner, nor did he rule oout that this could even lead to the closure of the Prague Stock Exchange in the future.
Market experts estimate the value of the Prague Stock Exchange at 5-10 billion crowns.
According to shareholder agreements, 90% of the shares could be sold.
The largest owner of the Prague Stock Exchange is Patria Finance Investmentbank with a 25 percent stake, followed by Ceská Sporitelna Bank with 14.7 and Tiger Holding Four S.a.r.l. with 13.5 percent.
The financial rick from the point of view of European newspapers
In her comments devoted to the ever-increasing international financial crisis, some leading European newspapers already spoke of "second 9/11" on Tuesday.
We owe the global financial disaster to the American president, for example "La Repubblica" - the president had not even been able to put his own party for the bill behind him, which should bring some relief into the frightening situation...
Instead of a "new Pearl Harbor," as multibillion-heavy investor Warren Buffett called it, it would be more appropriate to speak of a "second 9/11."
Parts of the press in Madrid also locate the scapegoat in Washington.
According to "El Mundo," the lack of political leadership is to blame that the global economy is now on the verge of collapse.
The American financial system threatens to collapse more and more, and the whole world will suffer.
Bush’s plan was not perfect, but after the vote in Congress there is a risk that Washington does not have a political means of crisis management.
The global economy hangs in the air, just at a time when one of the greatest transformations in the history of capitalism is taking place.
One can no longer ignore the threat, says, for example, the Geneva "Tages-Anzeiger".
Although some countries could still provide bailouts to their volatile financial institutions and save the banks from bankruption with taxpayers' money.
However, if a domino effect is triggered and even more banks go bust, then neither further financial injections nor other immediate measures will help.
In the long term, EU member states would have to jump over their shadows and centralize control of the money markets.
On Monday alone, more than EUR 100 billion were paid to weakening financial institutions, recalls the Vienna "Der Standard". In addition, according to the newspaper, the concerns are increasingly realistic that according to the Americans, European taxpayers would now also have to reach a little deeper into their pockets in order to get out of the crisis.
So far, the EU has not seriously dealt with the crisis, which is now sweeping like a tsunami over the old continents.
The "saviors" would not have to "waggle" immediately with several hundred billion euros, but it would be advisable to record, in which case it would be reasonable to touch the taxpayers' money in order to avert even greater damage.
Intelligent car imitates bumblebee flight
What would be the best way to develop new technologies to avoid accidents, if not to imitate the obstacle sensors from nature?
For example, those of bees.
CEATEC in Japan unveiled an astonishing project that inspired Nissan engineers from bumblebees in the creation of new accident-avoiding systems.
In cooperation with Tokyo University, Nissan has built the BR23C biomimetic system controlled by car robots, a mini-car equipped with the characteristics of the bees, with the aim of supporting the development of a system that avoids any collisions.
In flight, each bee forms its own, oval "private area," which is very similar to our own "protective shield," Kazuhhiro Doi, product manager of technology PR and research development, said at the exhibition.
The composite eye of the bee, which can see in any direction and in an angle of over 300 degrees, plays an important role in this.
To mimic the function of the composite eye, the researchers took up the idea of the laser-guided distance meter.
This identifies obstacles prior to the BR23C in a 180-degree visual field within 2 meters, calculates their distances and sends signals to the microprocessor on board, which immediately initiates the collision-avoiding process.
The obstacle is registered in fractions of a second, and the car robot immediately changes course, similar to the movement of the bees by rotating the tires around the appropriate angle.
In contrast to previous systems, this manoeuvre takes place purely instinctively.
"If it weren't, the car robot couldn't react fast enough to dodge the obstacles," explained Toshiyuki Andoh, head of "Mobiality Laboratory" and car robot project.
The system must react instinctively and immediately, as this technology conforms to the innerst and most vulnerable part of the protective shield - a layer within which accidents are currently still considered unavoidable.
The whole process must reflect the behavior of bees if they dodge other bees.
And that has to take place in a fraction of a second.
However, unlike the bees, the evasion, cannot be up, down, or even diagonal - it can only be two-dimensional, and only in the direction of the reversability of the tires.
While the bees have endless possibilities to avoid other bees, the researchers were only able to use the rotational movement, or acceleration and braking in the car robot.
The BR23C contains lithium-polymer batteries, which are lighter and more energy-efficient, and therefore have a longer service life than conventional lithium-ion batteries.
Andoh explained that it depends on the instinctive, intelligent reaction, and less on the processing of the data, and even less on the ability to store it.
This device only has to process a few inputs per second and respond to them accordingly.
It does not have to save these, or produce an intuitive, logical reaction on the basis of previous maneuvers, so that only a handfold of KB memory is constantly used.
It doesn't require gigabyte and megabyte-heavy memories.
This is the first step in the development of the immediate collision avoidance technology, which Nissan estimates will be in production as standard within the next ten years.
Another application could find the technology in GPS satellite navigation by deliberately steering the driver around obstacles and thus guiding him to his destination.
It's a name for the silent child
Now, the written version of the 700 billion-dollar bailout law has been available, which will be applicable in several stages, aimed at the purchase of bad loans from the banks.
In the first round, half of the money is to be spent.
In a session that came to an end on Saturday after midnight local time East Coast, congressmen went after multi-day debates on an agreement on the 700-billion-dollar bailout, Reuters reported.
"We've made significant progress," Nancy Pelosi, Democratic president in the U.S. House of Representatives, said it only had to be put on paper.
The compromise reached on the bailout was backed by both presidential candidates - Barack Obama and John McCain.
Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson commented on the result: The program will have an effect and it will be efficient, but even more needs to be done. According to Pelosi, this would not be about saving Wall Street, but about tearing around the helm of the economy.
The written version of the package was published even before the opening of the Asian markets, i.e. Washington time Sunday evening 8 a.m. (Centra-European time Monday 2 o'clock in the morning), but has not triggered a treble on the stock exchanges.
On Monday, the House of Representatives first votes on the bill to stabilise the 2008 economy, then it's the Senate's turn.
President George W. Bush spoke to Pelosi on Saturday night, and the White House also welcomed the news about the settlement.
We are pleased with the progress made during the night and values the efforts of both parties to stabilise our markets and protect our economy, said Tony Fratto, White House spokesman.
However, the program deviates from the Bush administration’s original recommendation that the 700 billion dollars at once should be spent on buying lazy securities burdened with loans.
In an initial tranche, a maximum of half of the 700-billion amount is to be spent, with 255bn immediately available after Congress is expected to vote on the case on Monday. This sum is to be spent on the purchase of load-burdened, bad securities, and the president is due to decide on another US$100 billion.
The programme will be reviewed by its own commission, to which the President of the Federal Reserve also belongs.
The other half of the 700bn depends on another congressional decision.
In the sense of the law, the Ministry of Finance can also use a combined tactic: it can at the same time buy bad loans, mortgages, and mortgage-burdened securities, or other "toxic" loans.
As responsible persons of the ministry reported, the purchase can take place both in an auction and directly.
The law allows the companies concerned to participate in an insurance program in which the Ministry of Finance guarantees the worrying funds, and the companies will pay corresponding fees to the expected damages.
Henry Paulsen said: The law defines the necessary funds in such a way that up to 700 billion dollars can be made available as emergency aid to the American financial system, whether for the large-scale purchase of bad funds, their insurance, or the avoidance of systemic risk triggered by the collapse of a larger financial institution.
We are taking the necessary steps so that we are ready to introduce the law once it has been signed, Paulson said.
However, the heads of the banks, which are in trouble and are now dependent on state aid, can expect that their clearances, which are called "golden handshakes" in the overseas slang, will be reduced by the government.
A cut in salaries will only occur in certain cases, and these cuts will depend on how the Ministry of Finance helps out in each case.
The reduction essentially means that if the ministry purchases as part of an auction, according to the "Golden Handshake," is taxed, unless the disused bank boss is deported to retirement.
In the case of a direct purchase of lazy loans, the government will aggressively intervene to ensure that manager salaries are not exaggerated.
The picture also includes that the existing Golden Handshake contracts will not be changed - the restrictions relate only to new agreements so that all the more people participate in the program.
This is the first case in American history that cuts in manager salaries are regulated by law, Democrat Barney Frank, chairman of the Financial Committee, commented on the measure.
The state gains participation rights in the participating banks, so that the risk for the taxpayers decreases, since they can also participate as soon as the economy and thus the rescued bank is better.
The bill envisages that the president will have to submit a proposal to Congress over the next five years about how taxpayers should get their lost money back from the financial sector.
There were skeptics on both sides, however, who expressed their doubts regarding the plan.
That's not what American citizens expect from us, the plan doesn't protect taxpayers' money by any time, according to California Republican Darrell Issa.
His colleague from Ohio, Mary Kaptur, said the measures were driven by the same people, who are also responsible for the collapse of Wall Street.
Industry prices continued to rise
According to the Central Statistics Office, domestic sales prices rose by 0.1 percent in August 2008 compared to the previous month, and compared with August 2007, equal to 12.9 percent.
Export prices were 1.1 per cent higher than those in July 2008, and are down 3.9% from year-on-year prices.
Due to the domestic price changes and the export sector, production prices were 0.7% higher than in the previous month, and 3.2 percent higher than in 2007.
Domestic selling prices are only marginal compared to the previous month, i.e. by 0.1 percent.
Sales prices for the manufacturing industry also rose only slightly - 0.1 per cent - since July.
The biggest price increase in August 2008 was leather goods (2.8 percent) - primarily for bags and belts - here the increase was 4.6 percent, for shoes prices went up by 2.5 percent.
Higher prices for plastics and chemical raw materials drove up the prices of chemicals and productions significantly (1.6 percent).
A decline in prices was recorded in crude oil processing (1.4 percent), for electrical and technical devices (0.9 percent), as well as in wood processing (0.1 percent).
The prices in paper production, as well as among publishers and the printing industry remained unchanged.
In other branches of the manufacturing industry, a price increase of between 0.2 and 0.9 percent was measured.
In the important domestic sales markets of the industry, such as electricity, gas, steam and water supply, prices rose by 0.3 percent in August, primarily as a result of the price increase in gas introduced on 1 July.
Looking at the domestic sales markets of the manufacturing industry year-on-year, prices in crude oil processing have risen the most (32.4 percent).
In the metal processing sector, a 13.2-percent increase was observed, while the increase in the production of food, beverages, and tobacco was 11 percent.
A decline in prices was observed in the textile industry (2.5 percent) and for electrical and technical devices (0.3 percent).
The extent of inflation in the other manufacturing industries ranged from 0.6 to 9.1 percent.
Overall, prices in the manufacturing industry climbed 10.2 percent last year.
The prices of electricity, gas, steam and water rose by 18.7 percent compared to the previous year.
On the domestic sales market, the highest price increase was recorded in the asset sector in August (0.7 percent).
In the production sectors for consumer goods, prices went up 0.3 per centres, while in the energy and processing sector they remained the same compared to the previous month.
The largest increase in prices in August 2008 was observed in the energy and re-use sector compared to the comparable period of 2007 (16.8 percent).
In the consumer goods-making sectors, a price increase of 6.8 percent was noted in the case of fixed assets a price reduction of 1.4 percent.
Industry's export prices measured in forint rose 1.1 percent in August compared to the previous month, but there was a year-on-year decline of 3.9 percent.
Compared to the euro, the domestic currency grew 1.7 percent weaker by 7.1 percent, and compared to the exchange rate of August 2007, it grew by 7.5 percent compared to the euro, and even 15.9 percent for the dollar.
No more visa in the United States from 2009?
Kinga Göncz sees a good chance that the American-Hangurese visa agreement will come into force around 12 January, as the foreign minister explained to the representatives of the Hungarian community in Chicago.
In their view, "now everything depends only on whether the electronic immigration or control system is introduced overseas in time."
However, she also emphasized that there will be no entry permit in the new system, but tourists would have to register in advance.
The head of Hungarian diplomacy will hold negotiations in Washington from today.
She meets Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice, to whom she will hand over the "Middle Cross of Merit with Star" of the Republic of Hungaria, and she is also holding talks with Democrat Democrat Barack Obama's foreign policy advisers and Republican presidential nominee John McCain.
Barack Obama and McCain support bailout for banks
Barack Obama and John McCain, the two presidential nominees of the major American parties, pledged their support for the 700 billion dollar bailout for banks on Sunday. However, there are also politicians calling for the resignation of the Treasury secretary.
The congress and representatives of the government have announced after days-long marathon consultations on Sunday morning that they have come to an agreement on the banking rescue package.
Wall Street's crisis, as well as defrosting the frozen lending market, became the central issues of the presidential campaign.
Obama and McCain - both claim to be better suited as those respective opponent to lead the country out of the economic crisis.
According to recent polls, Obama was able to further expand his lead thanks to the economic issues, while McCain's high, which gave him the presentation of vice-presidential candidate Sarah Palin after the Republican Convention, melted away at the same time.
At a campaign event in Detroit on Sunday, Obama expressed his hope that Congress will swiftly accept the law on the 700bn-dollar-dollar package.
He emphasized that the plan also includes those passages of consumer protection, which he supported.
"We have to act now, responsibly (...) - your jobs, your savings, entire industries, and the stability of the entire economy will be threatened," he said in front of 35,000 spectators.
He didn't fail to call McCain's economic visions "obsolete."
In an interview given to ABC, McCain said, “We’re all going to have a hard-to-kneel on that (...) but doing nothing is certainly not an alternative.”
The Republican senator said the plan also mandates the establishment of a control commission for the Treasury secretary, as well as restricting clearances for top managers - something he insisted on.
The CBS Obama said McCain didn't play a definitive role in the negotiations, whereas he, Obama, was in contact with the minister.
On Sunday, House Speaker Nancy Pelosi announced that the House of Representatives will presumably vote on the bill as early as Monday.
Press review of the German-speaking countries
The right-wing camp is intact, only the voters became more self-confident within the camp - that's how "Der Spiegel" comments on the weak performance of the CSU in Bavaria.
In Austria, Haider and Co are ready to govern to prevent a red-and-black coalition.
Der Spiegel (spiegel.de)
In the online edition of the left-leaning German news magazine Franz Walter analyzes the reasons for the dramatic defeat of the CSU in his article "Bavaria's revolution destroys their fathers".
According to the renowned political scientist, the erosion of the CSU began long before the sad Beckstein-Huber duo took office.
The Christian Socialists have renewed Bavaria for decades - now especially the modern citizens turn away from them.
Epoch break-up is at the same time a problem of Christian democracy, as well as for Angela Merkel.
Surprisingly, the disaster of the CSU did not occur, but on Sunday something came to an end, which, however, already became apparent almost exactly ten years ago: the undoubted hegemony of a state party
Ten years ago, in 1998, the CSU landed at 47.7 percent, falling below the fifty-percent mark for the first time since the 1950s
This was repeated three years ago in the 2005 federal elections, i.e. still in the much-cited Stoiber era.
The modernization consistently operated by the CSU in the federal state has, last but not least, destroyed its own mothers and fathers.
Sociologists had already predicted this 40 years ago, but misjudged themselves considerably at a pace, but without any impact the secularization, the educational successes, the influx of East and northern Germans into the prospering regions of Bavaria on the loyalty of the CSU voters could not remain.
Modernization is always accompanied by option propagation. And this has political consequences.
The right camp is intact - only the voters within the camp became more critical and idiosyncratic.
It is striking that in recent weeks the classical civic groups expressed the greatest distance to the CSU: the liberal professions, the commercial bourgeoisie and voters with high school diplomas such as university degrees.
Here the support of the CSU dropped to even below 40 percent.
The press (diepresse.com)
The conservative Viennese daily reports in its "Who with whom? party leaders reluctant on coalition issues" titled articles about the chaotic domestic political situation in Austria after the new elections.
The SPÖ wants to negotiate with the ÖVP.
The ÖVP forbids the intervention of the SPÖ in internal matters.
The BZÖ is open to talks in all directions, while the Greens do not yet want to commit themselves in relation to a coalition.
The new elections are over, and now everything revolves around the Coalition; in the debates on public television, the party leaders barely let look into their cards.
SPÖ leader Werner Faymann reaffirmed his desire to negotiate with the ÖVP.
The SPÖ wants to show that cooperation between the two parties is certainly possible - in some federal states and in the social partnership this is already the case.
Faymann left open the question of whether he could imagine a coalition with Wilhelm Molterer, the current ÖVP leader as vice-chancellor.
As he said, the voters would have voted out the bowl-molterer course.
A repetition of this direction would be out of the question.
Molterer, meanwhile, reacted cautiously to red offers.
The cooperation could not be fruitful, he said, if Faymann tries to interfere in internal concerns of the ÖVP.
Whether his party goes into opposition, or into the coalition, is currently completely open, and these questions should be discussed ÖVP internally.
BZÖ CEO Jörg Haider was open in all directions.
"In order to prevent red-and-black, we are willing to assume government responsibility," Haider said.
Járóka: The vicious cycle of poverty and segregation must be breached
Today in Paris, the European Summit on Equal Opportunities has begun this year hosted by the EU Presidency country France.
In her speech addressed to plenary, L�ívia Járóka, Fidesz MEP of the European People's Party, drew particular attention to the segregation for education, since it is one of the most serious violations of the law that strikes Roma children, and this is also the greatest obstacle to their need for validity.
The high-level conference was opened by French Employment and Social Affairs Minister Xavier Bertrand on behalf of the French Presidency, and Vladimir Spidla, European Commissioner for Employment, Social Affairs and Equal Opportunities.
Among the participants were representatives of governments, the European social partners, as well as the most important European legal protection and civic organizations in the field of equal opportunities.
As reported by Fidesz's press office, Járóka pointed out that in addition to discrimination in job hunting, Roma would be ousted from the labour market primarily because of their low levels of education.
"The vast majority of Roma youthers have no middle school education, and thanks to poor elementary education, only a small part of them can reach a university degree."
Járóka also emphasized that education is a fundamental human right that would play a key role in asserting other human rights.
Investments in the early education of Roma children could strengthen the economy of EU member states.
According to MEPs, it is important that these relationships become aware of the widest possible circle - i.e. Roma and non-Roma.
Because the sooner a Roma student starts school, the greater his chances of becoming a productive member of the labour market increase; and through income and sales tax, they contribute to national expenses.
"As citizens of the Union, Roma must secure the right to high-quality education - bad and segregated teaching must be discontinued," said Lívia Járóka.
As the MEP explained, a common, targeted and action-oriented approach of the European Union is necessary in order to accelerate the desegregation process in the education sector. To do this, it is essential to correctly comply with the existing and planned anti-discrimination directives.
"We must break the vicious cycle of poverty, segregation, and unemployment and give Roma children the democratic opportunities to contribute to Europe's future as full citizens," Járóka urged.
Since the congress in Berlin last year, the summit has been held annually with the aim of creating a unique forum foring the objectives of equal opportunities and non-discrimination at European, but also national, in the spirit of the "European Year of Equal Opportunities".
CSU: Mercy on time
At the end of October it will be decided on the fate of fallen duo Huber-Beckstein
Despite the election debacle, no heads are rolling for the time being, at the head of the Bavarian CSU the personnel debates have been adjourned.
Party leader Erwin Huber and Prime Minister Beckstein still remain in their offices, the only question is how long this grace lasts.
The fate of the tandem after the catastrophic 17 percent loss of vote will be decided on the last weekend of October at the extraordinary party congress.
However, the much scolded duo already showed that it does not want to stick on their armchairs.
In doing so, they want to appease the disgruntled faction and the party base, which demanded personal consequences one day after the "Bavarian tsunami" in view of the loss of the absolute majority.
Five years ago - admittedly, under the leadership of Edmund Stoiber, who is at his peak in his career's point - the Christian-Social Union still reached 60.7 percent. Now, however, a year after the coup against Stoiber, they were only able to secure 43.4 percent of the vote.
For 46 years, the CSU was "sole-governers" in Bavaria, which generates 18 percent of Germany's GDP.
The Conservatives remain the determining force in the southern federal state, which is also important for the Hungarian economy, but will have to govern in a coalition in the future.
As the first sign of the intra-party restructuring, the potential successor Hubers, vice-president and Federal Agriculture Minister Horst Seehofer will also be involved in the research team, which is holding exploratory talks with the liberal FDP or the right-wing "free voters" about a possible Munich coalition.
An ironyme of fate happens that with the "Free Voters" list Gabriele Pauli enters the Legislature, whose anti-Stoiber appearance the Huber-Beckstein duo first took advantage of it, in order to then mercilessly serve the single-minded politician.
The CSU leadership is now concentrating all its forces on the stabilization of Beckstein.
In this transition process, Stoiber, who so far pulled the strings from the background, will play an active role. The former prime minister and CSU chairman called September 28 the "most bitter day of his political career."
On Monday, Huber made a few poisonous remarks in the direction of the CDU - in his view that the CSU deserved far more support from the sister party.
Other CSU politicians interpreted the defeat as a signal to the CDU and thus also to Angela Merkel.
If the CSU, which also contributed a lot to success at the national level in 2005, should not be put back together with a joint act of strength, Merkel cannot become chancellor again after the federal elections in September 2009.
Merkel, who made no secret of her disappointment about the fiasco, believes the CSU's terrain loss will not have a negative impact on Berlin's grand coalition.
However, what is relative is that the wounded CSU will not be easier to govern, and the crisis of the Social Democrats, however, will continue.
With 18.6 percent, the SPD achieved its best result in Bavaria since 1946.
Lukashenko opposition: 110-0
In Sunday's elections, no member of the opposition made it to the Ukrainian parliament.
Russian observers think this is due to prosperity and stability in the country led by Alexander Lukashenko, but the OECD suspects illegalities in counting votes.
The OECD, which has sent 450 observers from 43 countries, did not recognise the elections.
The legislature of the 10-million-inhabitant state has been without opposition for decades. Nevertheless, the counter-party assembled under the name "United Democratic Forces" (ODS) has seriously hoped before yesterday's elections that Lukashenko, who renewed his presidential mandate for the fourth time in the unclean elections and thanks to a constitutional amendment in 2006, would this time "leave" some members of the opposition
That's why ODS has even refrained from the previously planned boycotts.
Thanks to the rising prices on natural gas imports from Russia, Ukraine is becoming more and more economically dependent. Loans have even been taken out for alleviating the price increases, and the country has also agreed to sale the Beltransgas, the owner of the gas pipeline leading through the country. In the background of the expectations of the opposition, therefore, there was the hope that Ukraine wants to improve its relationship with the EU, after Lukashenko and other high-ranking politicians because
A temporary relaxation was provided by the evaluation report of the OECD elections, in which the emphasis was on the positive movements rather than on the deficiencies - such as the exclusion of a third of opposition candidates from the elections.
However, the rapid report of the OECD already on Monday criticized the campaign - according to which opposition activists should not appear in the press - and in particular the fact that the opposition was not allowed to be present in counting the votes.
Despite all this, the opposition can see an improvement in the political system: political prisoners have been released, for example, and Minsk is even ready for smaller confrontations with Moscow by continuing to deny recognition of the independence of renegade Georgian provincies of South Ossetia and Abkhazia, although Russia, with which Ukraine forms a federative state on paper, has regarded the two territories as independent for a month.
"We have taken two steps forward and one backwards, but the most important thing is that the dialogue between Belarus and the EU has begun," Alexander Milinkevich told our paper.
According to the opposition nominee of the presidential elections of 2006, a continuation is desirable, although "the powerful have been afraid to allow even an oppositor into parliament."
The opposition is not planning any protest measures in light of the results, but is more likely to make clear the necessity of economic reform to voters.
"We are grappling with a lot of problems here - the opposition newspapers have to be printed in Russia, the local authorities put stones in our way when it comes to distribution, but I still see that more and more people are supporting the change, even the older ones," Milinkevitsch said in reference to the fact that not even the most-assisted strange of pensioners agree with the Lukashenko regime, whose only merit is the production of social security.
"Thanks to the indebtedness of the country and the increase in the trade deficit, it is only a matter of time before "social security", which reminds the silence of a cemetery, begins to crumble," says Milinkevich.
In contrast to the opposition politician, Russian observers have described the elections as democratic.
Sergei Lebedev, head of the mission of the Association of Independent States, told Ria Novosti news agency: "The people believe the deeds, and not the words". The absence of the opposition is therefore a testament to the maturity of Belarusian voters.
Brunetta: "From now on breakfast will be served before work"
For the first time in the history of Palazzo Chigi, there are rotatable doors for the entrances and exits of officials.
As of today, the measure will come into force against those Schlaumeiers, who first stamp and then go drinking coffee during working hours.
The new "anti-faulfure law" was unveiled today by Renato Brunetta, the Minister of Public Administration and Mauro Masi, secretary-general of the Presidium of the Council of Ministers.
""Rotatable doors are a means to increase work efficiency due to a higher attendance rate and more services," Minister Brunetta said.
"Now begins a new era of responsibility and transparency.
This is reformism," Brunetta emphasizes once again.
Palazzo Chigi concludes the establishment in all buildings of the Bureau of the Council of Ministers.
Masi says: "The choice of rotatable doors is also of great symbolic value".
There was no resistance on the side of the officials, the secretary-general assured: "This process was supported by everyone, the cooperation came from all sides".
All in all, there is no more breakfast during working hours; end with coffee or croissant .
Brunettas advises with a smile: "A good cappuccino at 7 o'clock works miracles".
During a meeting of the Higher Committee of Public Administration (Scuola Superiore della Pubblica Amministrazione)
from Rome, the Minister announces that "within three months the absences of civil servants have been reduced to 50%, i.e. 60,000 more people per year are present at work."
He adds that it is necessary to strengthen the efficiency of Italy's public administration
"their wage dynamics - according to the minister - have been twice as large as the private sector in the last 8-10 years and far above the rate of inflation."
Meanwhile, thousands of civil servants have teamed up to co-operate the Brunetta decree as part of the Codacons-backed appeal.
"While the minister for the civil service profiles himself with figures on lower absences at work - the consumer centre writes - the people of 'active lazing furs' are growing continuously.
Indeed, thousands of correct and valuable civil servants have come together to declare war on the unlawful norms of the decree.”
In particular, the Regional Administrative Court (TAR) is facing additional financial compensation for work-related sickness, including the planned extension of the mandatory house arrest of the sick worker, during which inspection visits can be made and other aspects of the minister's innovations are contested.
Napolitano: "Prevention of workplace accidents is crucial"
Every day 2500 workplace accidents occur in Italy, three people die and 27 remain disabled for life.
For the year 2000, the data of the Inail (Istituto nazionale contro gli infortuni sul lavoro) confirm that the fatal occupational accidents were around 1200.
These digits prove the seriousness of a phenomenon that causes almost twice as many deaths as caused by murders.
Today the Anmil (Associazione Nazionale Mutilati e Invalidi del Lavoro) in all the provinces of Italy will celebrate the 58th national day for the victims of occupational accidents in order to draw the attention of social institutions and media to this dramatic situation.
"The worrying data circulated by Anmil and the tragic news these days confirms how crucial the issue of prevention in the workplace is," the President of the Republic Giorgio Napolitano confirms in his message to the Associazione nazionale mutilati ed del lavoro (Associazione nazionale mutilati ed del lavoro).
The head-of-state expresses his "great appreciation for the constant commitment to prevention in workplaces, to the protection of injured workers, to aid to the families of the victims and to raise public awareness."
"An outraged "basta" has risen to this; comprehensible in tragedies, which, in terms of their magnitude, trigger some fuss in the media and sympathy of the public. But everyday reality often confronts us with dramatic events, which can even be repeated in their own dynamics".
Napolitano does not hide the fact that "there is definitely also a resource problem: it has to be invested decisively in education and information."
The numerous initiatives are of particular importance for a more direct awareness of school frameworks on the part of young people who are nourishing the world of work.
It is our duty to focus attention on this phenomenon and not to distract from its social seriousness. One has to put a legislative norm into effect and improve.
For his part, the President of the Chamber has emphasized Gianfranco Fini "how the phenomenon of work-related deaths forms an urgent social emergency, which insults the conscience of all of us and forces all institutions to work towards decisive rules aimed at putting an end to the problem".
"Occupational accident data appears to signal a slight improvement, but this does not rule out that the number of work-related deaths is far too high," emphasizes Anmil president Pietro Mercandelli.
"We don't want this "positive tendency" to mean less attention. That's exactly what causes the tragedy in the end."
"As believe that you basically do too little to counter the phenomenon of accidents. You can fight against it through various actions.
In our opinion, stronger checks and inspections are necessary – according to Mercandelli; but also stronger measures to educate the workers who are already starting at school.”
"Another effort is necessary - so he adds - in terms of communication and raising public awareness.
But above all, a greater sense of responsibility on the part of employers towards a phenomenon that constitutes a social emergency is required."
Mercandelli also came down to "the progressive deterioration of protection for occupational accident victims" and denounced the "abolition of the welfare system, against which we have struggled in recent years."
"The goal, therefore, is to return a guarantee to the employee on insurance so that the fate of the victim of an accident at work does not depend on voluntary or accidental donations, which from time to time are decided by a legislator."
Mp3 player under indictment: Danger of hearing damage
Listening loud music over a longer period of time with portable music devices, such as Mp3 players, can lead to permanent hearing impairment.
This is reported by the European Commission, which quotes in a reportance circumstanded in Brussels today the opinion of Cserseri (Scientific Committee on current and recently identified health risks).
"From the report, according to a note, it becomes clear that 5-10% of those who listen to loud music with wearable musical devices for at least five years a week for more than an hour per day risk the permanent loss of their hearing."
"I'm worried that such a large number of teenagers, who usually set portable music devices and mobile phones at a high volume, irretrievably damage their hearing without knowing it," says Meglena Kuneva.
"From the scientific findings – it is said on the part of the Eu – there is a clear risk, which pushes to act quickly.
What really matters is to sensitize consumers and turn this information into common good.
In view of these scientific findings, we need to re-examine existing control systems so that they are fully effective and keep pace with the new technology.”
Brussels had instructed the independent scientific committee in light of the widespread use of wearable music devices among young people.
"Scientists, including the EU communication, are confirming that they are rightly concerned and that, together with the other EU members and those concerned, the Eu Commission will now examine the necessary measures to better protect children and adolescents from the noise that comes from portable music devices and similar devices ".
At the moment, a European standard already exists, which limits the level of noise from portable music devices to 100 dB. Concern about the damage, which could come out of too much noise pollution.
You can limit this damage very much by resorting to solutions such as reducing the volume and the length of noise.
In its report, the Eu Scientific Committee points out that if the users of portable music devices listen to music only five hours a week (louder than 89 decibels), so that the legal limits that apply with regard to the maximum noise level at the workplace have already been exceeded.
Those who push music cross this line risk the complete loss of hearing within five years.
This would therefore affect 5-10 % of the users of such devices, the number of which can be estimated at between 2.5 and 10 million people in the EU.
Roma, Alemanno gives the go-ahead of the "Commission for the future of the capital"
We're starting today, it's supposed to be over in March, it's taking stock in June.
This is the procedure set by the Capitol for the work of the "Commission for the Future of the Capital Rome". The Commission met this morning in the new seat behind the Caracalla thermal baths.
In six month's time, President Marzano and the other 49 members of the Commission will draw up the guidelines for Rome.
Once the projection will be completed, the city and local council will evaluate the analyses and projects in order to be able to present the strategic plan for Rome with the "official stamp" of the Capitol and the Lord Mayor Gianni Alemanno.
"The Commission is absolutely independent and all political, social and cultural opinions are represented in it," - insists Alemanno. "It will offer proposals for a development plan for Rome, whose political leaders remain the city and local council.
There is no redistribution of responsibilities, only the will to have a place where you can develop a great project for Rome.
In March their task will be completed and the Commission will dissolve again, since such a project must have a time-specified endpoint.
In June, the mayor proclaims, we will present the strategic plan in which we will use the work carried out by the Commission."
Those who question the competence of the Commission, since their opinion is lackinged in terms of commitment, Alemanno replies: "Everything depends on the work the Commission will do and on the quality of its proposals.
If, as in France the Attali Commission, it delivers high-quality results – and this will surely be the case in Rome – then their contribution will be fundamental.
Too often, politics deals with the everyday problems and emergencies that occur from time to time.
We, on the other hand, want to keep an eye on a longer period of time, in order to work seriously on the development of Rome and the future of the city."
The mayor of Rome attaches great importance to clarifying the following "This commission is neither a useless institution nor a wreck: it is absolutely free of charge, it will not cost the Roman citizens a penny and it will make us think. It will occupy the best minds of the capital".
“It’s necessary to have a strategic plan that’s neither hermetic nor static in order to give our city a leap in quality – which it really needs – and thus take a common step into the future.”
According to the mayor of Rome, a future that "will not only be limited in the current municipal borders, but will look much further," not only with regard to Rome's role as the metropolis and political capital of Italy, but also as the future capital of Europe and the Mediterranean."
Obama – the first anti-U.S. president? Here’s an explanation
A well-known American politician who is much concerned with being regarded as independent has written: "Barack Obama becomes the second anti-American president.
The first was Jimmy Carter, who decimated through his decisions, the U.S. economy and who maintained relations (and still does so today as a private man).
The attacks against Obama are becoming increasingly violent, especially now that the elections are approaching.
A lady from Miamia has written on an Internet page: "This person is full of ambitions.
Obama is very naughty: he thinks he'll be given the highest office of a nation that has seened a total of two black governors and five black senators!"
Actually, however, these attacks don't have much to say; these are racist attacks and they don't answer voters' question: will he be a good president? And then there's the polls, too; everyone's say that Obama will win with a big lead.
In this case, too, there are people who talk to the heart and not to the head.
A newspaper from Mississippi, in the deepest south of the United States, has written in its editorial: only in the voting booth will we probably know to whom the real American patriot will vote.
This is where a U.S. mentality emerges that was kept hidden until last.
"Say what you want, then we'll decide who we're going to make our creep for, whether for Obama or for McCain."
There are people there sworn: if Obama wins, we're going to emigrate from America.
Something you’ve never heard before – only now that the success of the black candidate seems so certain.
Anyway, despite all these considerations, the definitive weapon for Democrats is George Bush.
1500 people were interviewed: What mistakes did Bush make during his tenure? 89% responded: the war in Iraq.
America is changing.
The nation lost 50000 troops in Vietnam and was sent back home with a kick to the butt.
In Iraq, 4500 troops have lost their lives and the Americans have not been forced to pack.
Even so, Iraq remains Bush's weak point for the majority.
The one who will take his place faces a huge task.
USA. The financial collapse also leaves its traces with journalists: In the newspapers you can already feel the cuts. And many accuse the Internet, the "murderer" of the printing press.
Five hundred American journalists were questioned by the PEW Research Center about their situation and their mental scrutiny about the future.
Today, almost all journalists are pessimistic about their future.
Nevertheless, they did not really express any concern to those responsible for the informent because of general credibility.
You don't feel as alone as you used to be.
The problem for American journalists is money.
For these journalists, the 2008 crisis is an economic model that has been destroyed.
In addition, there are the big problems caused by the newsroom, i.e. the space in which the editors work to put together the newspaper.
It is a problem that has to be solved – according to the interviewed journalists – if you want to prevent traditional journalism, as the large audience understands it, from shrinking significantly.
A clear but complicated pessimism fills the American journalist today.
Six out of ten journalists and executive seniors of the printing press are of the opinion that journalism is on the wrong track at national level.
In the local area, it is even about 65% for executives.
Printed vs. Broadcast.
It's not difficult for the journalists to imagine in which direction the web goes.
The most important publishing groups make their way onto the Internet, which is regarded by many as the "killer" of the printing press.
"Economic pressure" felt by journalists has risen by as much as 79%.
The 500 journalists interviewed have admitted wanting to join the new technology, hoping "they bring something good for journalism."
The biggest rift in American journalism today exists between the editors-in-chief of the newsroom and the reporters who work for them.
56% of editors-in-chief at the national level are sure, their reporters share their values with them, while at the local level it is only 23%.
Some think it has to do with "security at work."
The farm managers, who are afraid of losing their jobs within three years, are 47% at the national level.
There is 30% among journalists.
The topic of "safe work" in the team (our "editors", ed.) is always a current topic in the USA.
Only 13% of journalists work under contract.
In a vast nation like the U.S., with 300 million residents and thousands of newspapers, magazines, radio and TV broadcasters and Internet operators, employers offer as they please and so it becomes a mottory of take it or leave it.
The editor-in-chief very often receives very high wages, the editor, on the other hand, can sometimes only get 10000 euros per month, plus pension and insurance money.
As far as regards the holiday, the matter becomes complicated: a certain amount of days in the first year, such and so many after the third, to finally reach 15 vacation days after a certain number of years.
But American journalists, as they themselves admit, frequently "sick" over the course of the year.
It's a matter of climate.
UNIPOL: COURT IN ROME: DANILO COPPOLA REMAINS IN GROTTAFERRATA
The president of the second criminal chamber of the court in Rome, Carmelo Rinaudo, has decided to reject the real estate agent’s application, who had asked to be transferred from Grottaferrata to Rome.
Coppola is being investigated for bank red as part of the investigation into the counter-agreement in the case of the takeover of Unipol-Bnl.
In the course of the trial against him during last week, his dismissal from house arrest has been converted into a residency requirement in Grottaferrata.
His legantiers Michele Gentiloni and Gaetano Pecorella had asked to allow the real estate agent to be moved from the provincial city to an apartment in the capital.
According to the court, such transfers (against which the public prosecutor’s office had opposed) could give him the opportunity to influence his company and to obtain accusing evidence.
The elite are at risk
The phenomenon had already revealed itself a few days ago, when the Chamber of Deputies voted against the 700-billion-dollar austerity package of the White House: a large proportion of Americans were and are more interested in punishing Wall Street than saving the economy.
This confirms yesterday's New York Times edition, which reports on how the crisis divides the republican party in the running step, which splits more and more into its old and moderate East Coast soul, which always favored the state institutions and the business community and that neo-conservative that is hostile to "those in Washington" and the financial world and, above all, in the south and the center of the states voters.
This part of the party owes its rise mainly to the voice of the one I mentioned above.
However, this example just mentioned is only a small part of the major changes that the current economic earthquake announces or even already represents: not only in the USA, but also in the entire West and perhaps also beyond these will be affected by them.
Both in states and in Europe, the crisis seems to accelerate the long-feared events, which under these new conditions finally take shape and come to light.
The first of these phenomena is rediscovery, the unheared renaissance of the state in conjunction with a kind of renationalization of the economy.
Indeed, the crisis signifies the decline of the banking and financial institutions that have close ties to foreign countries and at the same timely manage an enormous amount of the assets of private households that reside in a single state.
This means an immediate political problem for the governments: namely how to behave towards the affected families, which also represent an enormous mass of voters.
So the crisis appears economically global, politically it is almost purely national.
Political internationalism seems to have been sidelined in itself and has lost its right to speak: the obvious proof of this has been given by the EU, which, due to its division into various national and international conflicts, does not have a common path.
Therefore, everyone is now counting on the intervention of their own state; rescue plans, new rules, and especially the distribution of relief funds: Since the crisis has become increasingly serious, only the U.S
And in addition to the funds, they also have the legitimacy to use them: thus two conditions not to be overestimated – under certain circumstances – to which no international organization or market will otherwise be able to resort in a similar way.
Moreover, the new emphasis placed on the state-national element is in perfect harmony with the ever-increasing importance recently associated in the economy with a closely related sector: territoriality.
Paradoxically, especially when we were so convinced that we had long since entered into a networked total kingdom, into a realm of ever-differentiated technologies, the intangible, that we suddenly have to realize how our future will depend more and more on the essentially material things: water, harvest and oil.
Although these can certainly all be transported from one place to another in the world, they are nevertheless connected to the utmost with a closely circumscribed piece of earth – a territory.
Therefore, he who has such a territory is at an advantage over another who lives in an area without harvest, waterless or free of oil deposits.
I now come to the second novelty, which, however, requires a short push-in.
Who territory, whose government, who says politics, says leadership with it.
In this sense, the financial crisis appears to generate two converging phenomena.
On the one hand, there is the knowledge of the need for respected leaderships, i.e. the need for someone who knows how to regulate things.
On the other hand, there is now a wave of indignance about the existing leaderships, especially those of the economy, which have turned out to be insufficient and full of shadow zones.
Finally, there is a profound and, in a sense, still disturbing aspect: a loss of confidence, an imbalance, a picture of inappropriability, a sense of distance, all aimed at an entire class of managers of Western countries.
It seems that the conviction among large parts of the population seems to pave the way that it is not only the deeds of the social elite, but already their world of ideas, their way of life, their culture in the broadest sense of the word, whose salary presents itself to the majority as increasingly foreign and outdated.
Demand for leadership thus nourishes an underground branched but powerful movement of disempowerment of the leading class and of the conventional political establishment. This is manifested in unforeseen "revolts" of the voters and in the unmediated shift of the collective state of mind of recent times: starting with the rise of the Conservatives in Austria and Bavaria, about the collapse of the Labor Party in Britain, to the rebellion
One gets the impression that the old elite, its parties, its programs, but also its rites, magazines, intellectual creditors, some would say their format, more and more understanding problems and therefore less representative value with regard to what is going on today in the lower social strata of the West and is amplified by the financial crisis and driven to over-dimensitivity.
In view of all this, it would simply be exaggerated to speak of a "rebellion of the masses".
However, keeping your eyes wide open is certainly not.
The superglue that lets rise on walls
Defeating the forces of gravity thanks to an adhesive and being able to climb up walls like Superman.
This is the subject of four American research centers, one of which has now seemingly reached its destination.
With an adhesive applied on an area of only 2.5 sq cm, the researchers have succeeded in fixing a body weighing 100 kg on a vertical wall.
The STUDIO – has an American journal, has published the findings of researchers from the University of Dayton, Akron, the Georgia Institute of Technology and the Air Force Research Laboratory.
The material invented by the research team is a further development of the adhesives already used, which use a special carbon structure, called carbon nanotubes.
"The principle of glue can be imagined as a row to a stack of spaghetti arranged tubes," explains Zhong Lin Wang from the Georgia Institute of Technology in Atlanta.
Scientists have taken inspiration from the feet of geckos covered by microscopic hairs that give the animal a perfect hold.
The tests carried out with various materials, such as glass Teflon and sandpaper, have shown that the new glue is ten times stronger than the holding capacity of the reptiles' feet, and at the same time "easily removed from the wall," according to Liming Dai, a researcher from the Faculty of Engineering at the University of Dayton.
WALKING ALONG WALLS? "This material could be used as an ephemeral adhesive," Liming Dai adds.
For example, how Spiderman go up on walls? Maybe so, but the researchers are thinking more of a less intriguing benefit, such as the connection of electrical devices, because the nanotubes are also conductors of heat and electricity.
Further areas of application could fall in the field of space travel.
So for now, the superheroes still have to wait.
THE OTHER KIND OF RESEARCH – the University of Berkeley, California, is also dedicated to a new type of glue.
In an article of the American Chemical Society, the Californian scientists present an adhesive made of microfibers that, like hairs, purify themselves from the dust through movement on a surface and thus always maintain perfect adhesion.
Medicines: never take with fruit juice
Beware of grapefruit, orange and apple juice.
These drinks, which are consumed due to their refreshing quality and vitamin richness, especially in the summer, can cause serious damage to their health if they are taken at the same time with certain vital drugs, since they can have an extreme effect on the absorption by the body.
David Bailey, a professor at the University of Western Ontario in London, repeatedly points to the risk of packaged as well as fresh fruit juices associated with medication intake. As part of the 236th meeting of the American Chemical Society in Philadelphia, he sounded the alarm again: a glass of apple, orange or grapefruit juice is sufficient to reduce the absorption capacity of certain essential drugs to such an extent that these are virtually eliminated and thus endangering the health of patients.
Grapefruit juice was already under suspicion of being responsible for increasing the effects of some drugs such as antibiotics, antiasthmatics, cardiovascular drugs, anxiolytic and neurological agents, up to toxic levels.
MEDICATIONS IN DANGER – In a report two years ago, doctors and patients had warned about the dangers of this mix and emphasized paying attention to what is drunk in connection with agents such as diazepan or metadone.
IT’s NOT JUST GRAPEFRUIT – According to the experts, orange juice and apple juice also contain substances that can inhibit the drugs.
The list of active ingredients inhibited by fruit juices is long.
Michele Carruba, director of Università Statale di Milano: "Such results are usually forwarded immediately to the various international manufacturers who are free to change consumer information.
Therefore, it is always advisable to read the package leaflet and, if there are still ambiguities, to have no shyness asking a pharmacist or doctor," emphasizes the expert of the Ansa agency.
Nobel Prize goes to Paul Krugman
The Nobel Prize is awarded to American Paul Krugman for his analysis of trade patterns and the settlement of economic activities.
The 55-year-old Krugman is a Neokeynesian who teaches at the University of Princeton and is known for his regular articles for the New York Times.
Krugman is one of the biggest critics of Bush administration's economic and foreign policy.
Few months after the September 11, 2001 attacks, during the days of Enron collapse, Krugman had claimed in a New York Times editorial that this second event had been more crucial to U.S. history than assassinations.
A prediction that received not a few polemics in response and to which economists were kept years later, but today turns out to be completely correct.
"I'm surprised and also shocked by how quickly the memory of the scandals as part of Enron or Worldcom's collapses have disappeared from the public field of vision," Krugman explained in late 2002.
According to Lorenzo Bini Smaghi of the Executive Committee of the European Central Bank, Krugman is an "economist of great value," but also a banaliser with a "very aggressive style, which is particularly directed against the excessive liberalism of the Bush administration."
Open Office, the third version of the "Anti-Microsoft" package appears
The day of the release of OpenOffice.org 3.0 has arrived; the open source software, as a free replacement for Microsoft’s office, is gaining an increasing number of users, both at home and in the office, both among freelancers and in public administration.
After a year of intensive work by the voluntary developers, which is supervised by SUN Microsystems, the new Open Office now presents itself to the audience with some novelties: in addition to a redesigned and now more spartan initial menu, is now finally reached by a thoroughly guaranteed compatibility with Apple's operating system Mac OS x, as well as the support for the new ODF (Open Document Format) 1.2 format, i.
Eight against was also the compatibility with Microsoft's Office 2007, whose new formats (.docx, .xlsx, .pptx) can be read by OpenOffice.
Also interesting are some new features, such as the one that makes it possible to reduce the size of PowerPoint presentations, which makes it easier, for example, to send via e-mail. This also includes the possibility to import PDF files into graphics editing modules and at the same time to preserve the original appearance of the page.
Thus, "hybride" PDF files can now also be generated, which extend the typical graphics of the Adobe format with the elements of a table and a word processing system.
Collaboration is one of the most improved elements of the applications: tables can now be edited multi-handedly by different users, which avoids conflicts in the event of changes; in the case of the word processing system, each editor is assigned a color in order to guarantee a better correction procedure.
Some companies are currently in the process of publishing various functions of OpenOffice on the Internet (on the cloud), while in the coming weeks the definitive result is to be made public.
The Italian version is available for download on the following link: http://it.openoffice.org/download/3.0.0/download300.html. Thus it follows the English version, which has been located on the mirrors since Friday.
In addition to Windows and Mac OS, the program is also available for the Linux operating system.
On request you can have the installation CD sent to your home or to the office.
However, cost-frequency can be an incentive for the dissemination of the program in private households, so it is also especially in the field of business, where OpenOffice the large litter could succeed: after all, various institutions of public administration and schools have already moved to OpenOffice.
In fact, there is not even a lack of a training offer, which means optimal help for small and medium-sized companies, and thus even opens up business prospects.
OpenOffice consists of five main components: Writer, the Word corresponding text creation program; Calc, the table program aimed at a displacement of Excel; Impress, to create presentations such as with Powerpoint; Dbase for operating databases instead of the better-known access; and most recently Draw, a graphics module.
The goal for 2008 is to reach 5 million downloads – a goal that can be easily exceeded with 3 million and 600 thousand already reached.
Kohl also tested positive and thus the fourth doping case
A new doping case opens in the context of the past Tour de France: it is the Austrian athlete Bernhard Kohl of the Gerolsteiner team, the third-classified and winner of the jersey of the best climber.
The news was published by L'Equipe on its website.
The 26-year-old Kohl from Team Gerolsteiner is the fourth driver who tested positive for Cera, the epo of the third generation. Previously, this was already the case with the Italians Riccardo Riccò and Leonardo Piepoli as well as the German Stefan Schumacher.
The analyses from Kohl's blood samples are among those carried out in recent weeks by Losanna and Chatenay-Malabry laboratories.
If the counter-rehearsals now confirm the positive results on Cera, the Austrian will lose his third place and will be excluded from the races for two years.
The head of the French anti-doping commission Pierre Bordry confirmed the positive epotest, and, according to the Associated Press, also notifies the Austrian authorities.
The 26-year-old Austrian climbed to the podium behind Spaniard Carlos Sastre and American Cadel Evans and also claimed the jersey of the best climber for himself.
IMMIGRATION: THE NEW YORK TIMES ASKS: IS ITALY RACIST?
Italy, "the former land of emigrants has recently become one of the preferred countries for immigrants," which are received only with reluctance.
Does the Belpaese make this a nation of racists? The New York Times asks this in a lengthy article devoted entirely to the issue of racism in Italy and emanating from the case of the young Burkina Faso-born Abdul William Guibre, who was murdered in Milan a month ago after stealing a pack of biscuits.
The case bears witness to what the Times refers to as the "paradox of the north": the northern regions are those where "the most integration and job opportunities exist," and yet "the highest degree of anti-immigrationist sentiments and the greatest sympathy of the Northern League."
As confirmation of the growing tension between Italians and immigrants, the Times cites further episodes of intolerance from Milan, Rome, Parma, Ciampino and Castel Volturno.
And not just this.
The new legislation against immigration introduced by Lega and the faster expulsion of illegal immigrants is also cited as a sign of climate change in Italy.
"Giorgio Napolitano asked the pope at a meeting at the Quirinal for cooperation between the church and the state to combat racism."
Female students Victims of up to 900-euros per room high rental prices
The market for housing for students knows no crises.
According to the last examination of the Sunia, the tenants' union, a single room can cost a student up to 900 euros in Milan, 700 in Florence, 650 in Naples and 600 in Rome.
"This phenomenon has already taken on very serious traits. Whole crowds of students are effectively losing their right to study, as their families are unable to support them at this level," according to those in charge of the Sunia, Laura Mariani - "in these cities, the great demand on the part of the students leads to a deformation of the market, which has also harmed the other inhabitants.
In addition, most rental offers include a number of violations: free contracts, missing registrations, no rental price limit, unauthorised sublets.
For the Sunia, there is a need for exceptional measures, which lead to lower rental prices through a revision of taxation.
Occasional consideration of the investigation, it turns out that in Milan a sleeping place in the Brianza district is needed about 450 euros; a single room starts at 650 euros (in the Bande Nere district) and ends with 800 euros in the Lambiate, Udine and Fiera district or even 900 euros in the Vittoria district.
In Florence, a sleeping place costs an average of 350/400 euros, a room 700.
In Bologna, where the students concentrate in the neighborhoods around the university, 250/280 euros for a place to sleep are necessary, 370 to 500 for a single room.
In Rome, the prices in the neighborhoods around the university are about 600 euros for a single room, 450 for a sleeping place in a double room.
Rents are only slightly deeper in the neighborhoods around Rome's other universities: 550 euros in Ostiense and Cinecittà.
Only those who swerve to the outskirts can save money: 300 euros for a sleeping place and 450 euros for a single room in Prenestina, Centocelle and similar quarters.
In Naples you need 300-450 euros for a place to sleep, for a room the price is between 400 and 600 euros in the most expensive quarters, the Policlinico, Vomero and Colli Aminei.
In Bari, a sleeping place costs 250-350 euros, a single room at least 350.
In the smallest cities, prices are lower, but they have a greater weight in the urban economy.
In the cities where the phenomenon of leasing to non-European students is more common, such as Perugia, there is even another anomaly: a rent, which is 25-30% above average, is demanded by foreign students.
Goodbye to the old Paradiso cinema – the cities are closing their historic cinema halls
The historical cinemas are closing due to beneficiary multiplex cinemas.
"A city without cinema halls is a blind city, like a face without eyes."
This picture by the director Giuseppe Tornatore describes with effect what is currently happening in the large urban centers of Italy, and also in the provinces.
The process of excluding cinemas, which began with the birth of the great multi-room complexes in the periphery, means, in the long run, the death for single-hall cinema in the center. A process that not only seems inevitable and irrevocable, but also carries the traits of a cultural crime.
Mostly historical places full of memories are affected, "it's like losing a friend with whom you've traveled a piece of street full of emotions," according to the director of the film "Nuovo Cinema Paradiso," which earned him the Oskar and the Cannes Grand Prix 20 years ago. It's the story of a suburban cinema and its nostalgia, which consists of living in arguably lost cinema world.
The figures provided by Anec, the central trade association, speak clearly: in the last five years, the enormous amount of 316 traditional cinemas have had to close; for the most part, these collieries paid cinemas in the big cities: 10 in Florence, 9 in Milan, Naples, Bologna and Rome, 6 in Turin and 5 in Palermo.
Among the worst-hit regions are Emilia-Romagna with 82 lost cinemas, followed by Tussany with 44 and Lombardy with 41.
This testifies to the fact that the main culprits are multiplex cinemas, because it is precisely these three regions in which the largest number of these modern cinemas have been registered: at 195 in the last 5 years, Lombardy tops the lists, 81 are both in Tuscany and Emilia-Romagna.
"The business has developed in an irrational way," says Paolo Protti, president of Anec, "it is useful that new structures also emerge, but in the first place is the preservation of the already existing one, because if a hall in the city closes, not all its regular customers are ready to visit a multiplex cinema in the periphery instead of the old one.
And here remains the missing budget for the fund of the trade federation outsponse eight: the debt burden recorded by the state amounts to about 10 million euros.
It's a disgrace from an ethical-moral point of view that the government doesn't heed the existing laws.
In order to preserve the sector, between 16 and 18 million euros a year would be necessary."
Interesting is the case of Bologna, the first Italian city which, due to the fears surrounding the old cinema halls, has decided to protect them through an agreement between the municipality and the Anec; it is the first agreement of its kind in Italy aimed at protecting and supporting traditional cinemas, both in the historical centre and in the periphery.
Among the foreseen measures are also tax advantages for cinema operators as well as advantages for the public with regard to local traffic: it will be allowed to drive into the traffic-limited areas of the centere and also park at cheaper parking fees.
"It's a pleasing initiative," says Giuseppe Tornatore, "I hope that other cities will join our example and also design new concepts.
However, we certainly also need better audio and video equipment in order to be able to do something against the superpower of the multiplex halls, because after all, today’s audience is more demanding.
The defense of traditional cinema has always been an important topic for me: the closure of Baghiera's "Supercinema", the place of my childhood and adolescence, was the decisive point that led me to found the "Nuovo cinema Paradiso", even though I had been playing with the idea for 10 years.
I have lived in Rome for 25 years; when I past the Rialto or Quirinale in Via Nazionale, where I have experienced so many different emotions, I turn to the side in order not to have to see the dust-covered posters.
The ties between politics and 'Ndrangheta lead to the arrest of the mayor of Gioia Tauro
"The mayor of Gioia Tauro was prepared to adapt the decisions of the municipality to the interests of the mafia group Piromalli.
Accusations by the anti-mafia authority of Calabria's legalitacy against Mayor Giorgio Dal Torrione and his deputy Rosario Schiavone of the municipality of Gioia Tauro, who were arrested for involvement in mafia dealings this morning, are serious.
Gioacchino Piromalli, the older mediator between the clan and the public administration, as well as his nephew, was handcuffed: the lawyer, who bears the same name as the boss of the clan, had already been convicted for involvement in criminal associations of the Mafia, at that time to a compensation amount of 10 million euros.
"Disregarding any kind of moral and legal norm," the lawyers write, "the two local administrations had decided to pay funds for advisory activities to lawyer Piromalli."
“Thus it had been made possible for the mafia group – according to the judges – on an official path into the town halls and to influence the politics of the public administration.”
The president of Calabria's regional committee Agazio Loiero does not deny that in his region it is "anything but an exception to encounter links from local administrations and organized crime." "There are places in which the concentration of the mafia is greater than in others in which such connections are practically inevitable.
But there are also local politicians who resist the pressure of 'Ndrangheta: heroes who sustain themselves a white vest."
This morning's arrests follow those of last July with which, according to the police, the clan Piromalli was beheaded.
Since the end of last April, Gioia Tauro’s municipal council had been dissolved, but as early as 1991 – and also at that time due to alleged links between organized crime and the local administration – the local politicians had been relieved of their municipal offices.
Giorgio Dal Torrione, then mayor of the Udc, has been elected the head of the centre-right coalition after the run-off election of May 2006.
He shouted scandal when the prefecture ordered the dissolution of the local council.
He pleaded guilty to Deputy Interior Minister Marco Minniti, whom he accused of having invented everything for political-strategical reasons, but in connection with this morning's arrest warrant that the Piromalli clan even dared to request an amendment to the A3 project.
From an eavesdropping operation in connection with the investigations into the Dal Torrione case, the willingness to meet the wishes of an entrepreneur who is known to be a representative of the large group around the Piromalli has been shown – whereby the measures of the public administration became identical to the interests of this clan."
When the energy springs out of the waves, suddenly the big ones rise
It is a non-driving source of money that covers over 70% of the surface of the planet and only now do the world’s big energy companies seem to understand it.
Waves, currents and tides are the new renewable energy sources that everyone is now looking at, and are also looking to occupy, such as giants such as Chevron.
The possibility of extracting energy from the movements of the sea has already been considered and investigated for 70 years; not coincidentally, these studies intensified considerably since the first petroleum crisis.
When the price of oil collapsed around 1980, the idea of marine energy was gassed, but now the environmental organizations and the increased oil price are pushing for new stimulus in this sector.
The World Energy Council (WEC) in London estimates that 15% of the world's energy needs could be covered by the energies generated by the ocean waves.
This would be double the energy currently produced by nuclear power plants.
In addition, there would also be the currents and tides.
Giants in attack.
The first company to rely on marine energy is Enersis, which in 2005 signed a contract for the construction of a power plant in the north of Portugal.
When this news was published, it seemed to be a revolutionary move.
Two years later, there are now more and more companies from the traditional energy industry that rely on marine energy production.
“Chevron explores these technologies,” according told the spokesperson for a well-known company in the Wall Street Journal, and weighs on the opportunity to work with us.”
It is about obtaining an approval from the American Energy Commission, which is necessary to start a project off the coast of Alaska.
This is a not to be underestimated sign, especially when you consider that, especially in Alaska, oil continues to be extracted by the U.S. and President Bush recently launched the plans for new drilling.
In addition to Chevron, Verdant Power Inc. from New York and Ocean Power from New Jersey, which are investing in the new industry; if the details are unknown, observers speak of increasing interest.
Technologies that are still in need of improvement.
Experts speak of a technology that is still on the rise, not least because the last doubts have not yet been clarified by the environmentalists.
So far, projects such as those in Portugal and Scotland are of a rather small dimension and do not seem to cause any serious ecological problems.
However, in order to produce large-scale energy, technologies with a far greater impact on the environment are required.
There are various possibilities: Ocean Power, for example, uses a system of connected buoys.
The thrust of the shafts, which moves the buoys up and down, is converted into hydraulic pressure within the floats via pistons and cylinders.
This drives a turbine, which in turn operates a generator.
The electricity is then sent to the coast via underwater lines.
So far, there are four planned facilities of this kind, which are only waiting for state approval. Some of the generated energy could also serve for military facilities.
Verdant Power, meanwhile, is already producing energy for a department store and a car park using submarine turbines in the East River of New York.
The water movements during the tides drives the turbines, which feeds the generator due to the resulting rotational movement.
According to Verdant, a long queue of possible clients is already waiting for the necessary authorizations to use these energy sources.
First the waves, then the tides.
According to the Institute for Electricity Generation Research, a special interest immediately deserves the potential that lies in the tidal tides.
Although it is also an irregular phenomenon, the tides are more predictable than wind currents, solar radiation and waves, and therefore a more trustworthy power source.
Especially the tidal energy is which states such as New York, Maine and Alaska, as well as other coastal states, have already invested 7.5 million dollars in 2008 – up to 35 million are already planned for 2009.
